\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Steady; 9,030-9,070 Band Tipped Next Week\b0\par\par\uc2 1,380 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2011\par 10:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0944 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is steady at 9,065 in thin volume as the market lack fresh leads, dealers say. "We still expects the rupiah to strengthen on inflows and strong fundamentals, it's just a matter of time and of course, Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's (suspected) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," a dealer at local bank says, tipping the pair to stay in a 9,030-9,070 range next week. BI's Gov. Darmin Nasution says the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will let the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciate gradually. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0940 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds have pared gains while gilts are now in negative territory, with yield spreads on peripheral euro-zone government bonds narrowing further. In addition, French Finance Minister Christine Lagarde says officials are looking at raising the size of the EFSF, which could feed into concerns about an increase in German costs of bailing out its neighbours. March bunds are now unchanged at 124.64, and March gilts are down 0.24 at 117.40. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0939 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro's across-the-board surge has broken many short-term resistance levels notes \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . However, the bank says with the exception of EUR/CHF, where the risk is toward 1.31 and possibly 1.34, there is little evidence that the current move will be a long-term game changer. EUR/CHF now at 1.2894. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0939 GMT [Dow Jones] HUF weakens slightly, correcting from a recent peak on increased risk appetite, in turn the result of favorable bond auction results both in euro zone periphery countries and the region in recent days. EUR/HUF is 275.25 from 274.75 late Thursday. Takarekbank sees the EUR/HUF cross stable around 275. Forecasts a downward correction in EUR/USD later today, therefore doesn't expect significant HUF strengthening. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says 275 is a significant resistance level in EUR/HUF, which is unlikely to be broken in the absence of market-moving news or announcement.(veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0935 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite the slight rise in the Swiss December import and producer price index, the underlying inflation pressure remains very subdued, says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . "Apart from energy, metals and agricultural products, nearly all other goods prices declined in December, partly reflecting the impact of the franc's appreciation," according to economist Fabian Heller. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0935 GMT [Dow Jones] Pakistan shares trade higher, led by oil shares. The benchmark KSE-100 index is up 0.5% at 12,517.17. "Sustained foreign fund inflows are keeping the upward momentum intact," says a dealer. According to local firm National Clearing Co., foreigners have bought shares worth $22.49 million through the secondary market in the previous 4 sessions. He adds, "share prices are also rising on reports the ministry of law has approved a proposal, submitted by the Karachi Stock Exchange to allow investors to buy shares on borrowed money". The draft will be forwarded to the Securities Exchange Commission of Pakistan, stock market regulator to give its final shape. He tips the index to face resistance at 12,630. \uc2 Pakistan State Oil\uc2  (PSO.KA) is up 3.7% at PKR308.75 and Attock Refinery (ATRL.KA) is up 1.5% at PKR388.90. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0928 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB is steady at 30.44-30.45 vs 30.43-30.44 late yesterday in Asia after a volatile day, with the pair rising to a high of 30.58 before selling from exporters helped drag it back down. Sharp gains by the euro vs the USD also prompted some USD-selling, says a Bangkok-based dealer. "It's been quite volatile and I expect that to continue next week. Some people are trying to push up the USD as economic data out of the U.S. seems to be improving, but I think most expect it to trend lower over the medium term against the THB, so it's being capped at the moment." Resistance is put at 30.80, with support at 30.30. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are higher on buying mainly in the 3-5 year notes. Foreign players, local funds are active on the buy side, says a trader. Thursday's rebound in U.S. Treasurys is also helping to bolster sentiment. "The market has already digested Wednesday's rate hike and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hawkish stance. As the next policy meeting (on March 9) is still some time from now, punters are accumulating bonds for short-term profit," says the trader. Given the current buying momentum, yields are expected to drift further down by up to 5 bps early next week. The bid/offer yields of issues due December 2015 are quoted at 3.15%/3.13% from 3.21%/3.18% late Thursday, while October 2017 yields are quoted at 3.47%/3.40% from 3.50%/3.46% and June 2019 yields are at 3.49%/3.42% from 3.57%/3.45%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0923 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is at 29.600, up from its 29.013-29.028 range most of the day, after two state-owned banks struck a deal at 29.600 late in the session, two traders say; the pair was at 29.605 late yesterday. One of the traders says profit-taking in local equities today helped relieve some of the rising pressure on the TWD, but expects the USD/TWD will fall below 29.00 some time next week, as "there are no signs suggesting that foreign inflows will abate in the foreseeable future." The Taiex ended flat at 8972.51. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0918 GMT [Dow Jones] Natixis says it prefers to adopt a relatively defensive allocation strategy for the European bond market early on in 2011, and for this reason it's interested more particularly in triple-A debts and possible arbitrages within this class of assets. It argues that peripheral debts are delicately poised, even in the short term, because volatility is fuelled by an unending stream of speculation. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0918 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares end lower, with the benchmark KLCI down 0.1% at 1569.89 on across-the-board selling, in line with weaker regional markets, says a local dealer. "It's an excuse to book profits after pretty strong gains recently, but 1565 support was quite firm today," says the dealer, tipping further gains next week to test the all-time high of 1576. The KLCI has risen 3.3% since the beginning of the year. Maybank analyst Wong Chew Hann says she's keeping a year-end target of 1710, with 1H likely stronger than 2H; in the near-term; "we expect the market to remain buoyant, (but) with profit-taking ahead of the Chinese New Year," says Wong. Market breadth ends negative with decliners beating advancers 551 to 317. Among decliners, SP Setia (8664.KU) is down 3.3% at MYR6.70, Genting (3182.KU) is off 0.8% at MYR11.88, \uc2 UEM Land\uc2  (5148.KU) is lower by 4.3% at MYR3.11 and AirAsia (5099.KU) is down 3.0% at MYR2.95. Advancers include \uc2 Petronas Chemicals\uc2  (5183.KU) rising 2.0% to MYR6.15 and \uc2 Malayan United Industries\uc2  (3891.KU) climbing 15.9% to MYR0.255 on plans to sell its insurance unit. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Lloyds Banking Group\uc2  has been hearing "more and more talk" about the fact that while the overall national financial position is more favorable for Italy, the relative low level of debt stock makes Spain better positioned in terms of sustainability of financing costs in the face of rising yields, at least in the short term. "We think this might be a symptom of a market reassessment of Italy's status as 'darling of the peripheral investor,' say Lloyds analysts. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com) [ 01-14-11 0444ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110114e71e0006b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
