\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Steady; 9,005-9,030 Range Tipped - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Januar 2011\par 02:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR is steady at 9,020 as market participants are cautious after falls in local stocks and bonds Friday, while uninspiring U.S. jobs report damps demand for the greenback, dealers say. "The dollar will likely trade within a narrow range of between IDR9,005 and IDR9,030 today," a dealer says; he adds Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the pair rises above IDR9,030. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is up 0.2% at 1575.12, led by property and construction stocks. A local brokerage dealer says "though the market may look firm, there should be some profit-taking along the way, given the steep 4% rise last week," says the dealer; he tips immediate support at 1570, with resistance at 1585. TA Research cautions investors to be prepared for profit-taking, especially towards the last week of January as market players look to cash out profits ahead of the Lunar New Year holidays starting early February. Among gainers, Ekovest (8877.KU) is up 19.3% at MYR3.21 and MRCB (1651.KU) is +6.9% at MYR2.33 on The Edge report their JV may bag a MYR8 billion river clean-up contract. On the downside, BAT (4162.KU) is off 1.0% at MYR45.50 and \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) eases 1.l% to MYR22.38 on profit taking. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] The CAD's close correlation with rising oil prices continues to work in its favor in early Asian trading after the price of crude oil futures jumps 2.0% following the closure of the Trans Alaska Pipeline this weekend. While the USD/CAD has held above the 0.9900 mark thus far, the EUR/CAD has fallen to a new 7-month low at 1.2776. In 4Q10, the CAD had the strongest correlation with crude oil prices among major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The USD/CAD is at 0.9915, with near-term support pegged at 0.9895 (Friday's New York low), Nymex crude is at $89.59/bbl. (mark.cranfield@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares may drift lower after a weaker performance on Wall Street following the disappointing non-farm payrolls data and amid a dearth of domestic leads. The benchmark PSEi is likely to slide to a 4180 support after falling 0.3% to end at 4202.52 Friday; resistance is pegged at 4230. "The market will continue to consolidate, with a downward bias. There's no major market-moving news and even the U.S. (stock markets) ended flat," says Astro del Castillo, managing director at First Grade Holdings. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are likely to rise slightly with support from likely continued buying interest in banks, which will offset possible weakness in property developers after the latest tightening measure in Chongqing. The Shanghai Composite Index is tipped to face resistance at 2850 after last ending up 0.5% at 2838.80. "The cheap bank sector may attract more buying interest after Friday's rise, while property developers are likely to open lower on the news of Chongqing's real-estate tax," says Huatai Securities analyst Zhou Lin; but adds the uptrend in property stocks remains intact due to "the low valuation of the sector and robust property sales." Note, Xinhua reports yesterday that China has decided to collect real-estate tax on high-end residential property in southwestern Chongqing city. Shenzhen Index ends down 0.8% to 1292.06 on Friday. (amy.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0115 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB is higher at 30.37-30.39 from 30.34-30.36 late Friday in quiet trade. Concerns over euro-zone sovereign debt are lifting the pair, outweighing any falls in the USD on disappointing U.S. non-farm payroll data. "Between the U.S. dollar and the euro, players prefer the dollar (on hopes that the U.S. economy will recover faster than expected)," says a local dealer. He adds, investors will likely focus on the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 's policy rate decision on Wednesday. The dealer tips a 30.30-30.45 range for today. Ten of the 13 economists surveyed by Dow Jones tip the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise its policy rate by 25 basis points to 2.25% this week. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0113 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI may stay in the 23,500-23,700 range as the modest decline in the DJIA on Friday is not expected to provide strong cues for the local market, says Conita Hung, head of research at Delta Asia Securities. "The local market is expected to witness some further consolidation in the near term, after its recent gains." The index ends down 0.4% at 23,686.63 Friday, snapping its 7-session winning streak (+5.2%). She expects HK property stocks to outperform, as the positive outlook of the sector remains intact, while some pressure in the mainland developers is expected, after Xinhua reports yesterday that China has decided to collect real-estate tax on high-end residential property in southwestern Chongqing city. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY is likely to fall as buying momentum slowed sharply late Friday after the dollar's recent gains. A Beijing-based trader at a local bank tips the pair to move in a 6.6200-6.6250 band for most of the session; the USD was at CNY6.6280 late Friday. Friday's weaker-than-expected U.S. payrolls data is also expected to hurt USD demand, the trader says. In addition, China is set to release its December trade data between 0100 GMT and 0200 GMT; though the trade surplus is widely expected to have narrowed last month, economists say the expected decline is unlikely to help alleviate tensions between China and the U.S. over the yuan exchange rate. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is expected to move higher as the EUR slides on renewed concerns over euro-zone debt. The USD/PHP is tipped to initially rise to 44.20 after settling late Friday at 44.15, with resistance at 44.30 and support at 44.00. But a local bank trader says, that gains maybe limited by the weaker-than-expected U.S. non-farm payrolls data. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's November retail sales figures (+0.3% on-month) illustrate why the RBA will likely be on hold through 1H11, especially given the wet weather coupled with higher mortgage costs mean consumers will remain very cautious, says \uc2 UBS\uc2  Chief Economist Scott Haslem. "We expect only a modest improvement in retail spending near-term. Overall, today's data reinforce our non-consensus view that the RBA will be on hold for most, if not all, of the first half of 2011." \uc2 UBS\uc2  looks to July for the next \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] Aussie retail sales will likely remain subdued in the near-term given that rising borrowing costs are hurting consumers, says Macquarie Economist Benjamin Dinte. "We expect activity to remain relatively subdued into early 2011." He adds, strong employment and wages growth is helping households, but the higher cost of living and mortgages will continue to keep sales tempered. Macquarie expects December's sales to have been pretty moderate overall. "It's seems to be a story of rising income being offset by rising costs." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is 0.2% lower at 2082.40 in choppy trade. Analysts say the market has lost a bit of its strong upside momentum after last week's 1.7% rise, though the overall upward trend could persist for a while. "Technical pressure has mounted in the market as there has been no significant correction since the end of November," says \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2  analyst Cho Byung-hyun; he tips a 2050-2100 range for this week. Other analysts tip 2077 as support for today. Heavyweight tech plays mixed, with \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) +3.1% at KRW117,000, on hopes for an earnings turnaround helped by improvement in TV, handset businesses, but \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  (000660.SE) is down 1.0% at KRW25,850 on profit taking after its recent gains. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com) [ 01-09-11 2036ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110110e71a0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
