\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Weak Euro To Cut French Trade Deficit -Asteres\b0\par\par\uc2 1,370 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2011\par 11:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1014 GMT [Dow Jones] The weak euro, trading near a four-month low against the dollar early Friday, is likely to have a positive impact on the French trade deficit in early 2011, Asteres says. The French trade deficit was little changed at EUR3.87 billion in November 2010 compared with EUR3.71 billion in October. November exports rose 19% on year, while imports increased 15%. Asteres attributes the export increase to the sale of Airbus jets and a satellite and to a port blockade caused by strikes in October. (inti.landauro@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1012 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia local government bonds continue to fall on a broad sell-off amid high inflation pressures, traders say. Two traders at a local securities firms say the falls may continue well into the middle of next week before selective bargain-hunting by investors emerges. The five-year yield is up at 6.78% from 6.69% Thursday, the 10-year yield is higher at 7.79% from 7.70%, the 15-year yield is at 8.40% from 8.32%, and the 20-year yield is at 9.17% compared with 9.04%. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1011 GMT [Dow Jones] CZK trades lower at 24.657-24.677 against the euro from 24.592 late Thursday as investors are closing short positions following a string of impressive Czech data releases this week, says a trader at Raiffeisenbank in Prague. "The good news is out and they can close positions; it's typical profit taking," the trader says. "In one month we could be lower, testing the 24.50 psychological level if German and Czech industry go well." (sean.carney@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1007 GMT [Dow Jones] The Markit iTraxx corporate credit indexes are still little moved on the day as banks and sovereigns continue to suffer, says data-provider Markit. Notes both the Europe and Crossover indexes are nearly flat at 108/109bps and 429/433bps, respectively, with trading light ahead of key U.S. labor data due at 1330 GMT. However, says banks are wider after the European Commission's proposed framework for senior bank debt, and sovereigns are continuing to move out amid worries about impending government bond supply. Markit SovX Western Europe index is 5bps wider at 216/218bps. (art.patnaude@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1005 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares end down 1.4% at 1036.45, snapping three straight sessions of gains in active trade. Most big caps retreat on consolidation mode. The SET Index is expected to continue to consolidate next week as the downside is weighed by expectations the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hike its benchmark rate Wednesday, says Asia Plus Securities' Therdsak Thaveethiratham. He adds, the selling force in certain tax benefit mutual funds due to be redeemed is likely to continue. He tips support at 1030 and resistance at 1050. Among the most active stocks, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) is down 2.8% at THB102.50, \uc2 True Corp\uc2 . (TRUE.TH) slides 8.7% to THB6.30, PTT (PTT.TH) falls 3.0% to THB322 and Thai Oil (TOP.TH) drops 3.3% at THB73.25. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1004 GMT [Dow Jones] Takarekbank expects Hungary's industrial output to have accelerated further in December after unexpectedly strong November growth data, released Friday. Adjusted November output is up 4.3% on the month and 14.5% higher on the year. Economist Gergely Suppan says it's likely December growth rose due to demand growth from German car manufacturers and a new tire plant owned by Hankook that began production at the end of November. Suppan expects strong industrial data to support Hungarian financial market assets given the implied improvement in economic prospects. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1001 GMT [Dow Jones] The impact of the severe floods in Queensland is being felt beyond Australia's borders and the AUD, notes \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . The bank says alternative suppliers of the affected commodities will benefit in terms of increased demand and higher prices and this is another reason to be short AUD/CAD, in addition to China's continued policy tightening. AUD/CAD now trades at 0.9923. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1000 GMT [Dow Jones] Norway's manufacturing output for November comes in at +0.9% on the month, reversing the previous month's dip, and the rise was higher than expected, says Handelsbanken. "If the December reading stays flat, the 4Q annualised growth in manufacturing production will be 3.1%, not much above \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2 's forecast," notes Handelsbanken, so this "shouldn't significantly alter \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2 's view of the manufacturing sector." (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0944 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares end down 2.8% at 3631.453 in moderate volume as foreign funds trim their positions due to growing concern over the country's inflation. Investors are worried because rising food prices will add to Indonesia's inflationary pressures in January, traders say; they add, higher inflation could inspire the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hike policy rate next month. Traders tip the main index to trade in a 3600-3670 range Monday. "Selling in most interest rate-sensitive and commodity stocks drove the main index lower despite sporadic bargain-buying by local funds," says a trader with Erdhika Securities. Among decliners: carmaker Astra (ASII.JK) is down 3.9% at IDR49,000, \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) is down 4.5% at IDR6,400, while Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) is down 5.3% at IDR9,800. Foreigners are net sellers of IDR1.5 trillion worth o