\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesia Shares End Down 2.8%; 3600 Support\b0\par\par\uc2 1,541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 2011\par 10:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0944 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares end down 2.8% at 3631.453 in moderate volume as foreign funds trim their positions due to growing concern over the country's inflation. Investors are worried because rising food prices will add to Indonesia's inflationary pressures in January, traders say; they add, higher inflation could inspire the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to hike policy rate next month. Traders tip the main index to trade in a 3600-3670 range Monday. "Selling in most interest rate-sensitive and commodity stocks drove the main index lower despite sporadic bargain-buying by local funds," says a trader with Erdhika Securities. Among decliners: carmaker Astra (ASII.JK) is down 3.9% at IDR49,000, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Mandiri\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BMRI.JK) is down 4.5% at IDR6,400, while Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) is down 5.3% at IDR9,800. Foreigners are net sellers of IDR1.5 trillion worth of shares. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0943 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is down 2.2% at 19,750.33 on growing concerns India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may soon raise interest rates to tackle rising food inflation, say local dealers. Investors are deeply worried that "the RBI may raise policy rates by 50 bps (basis points) after market hours today and that is causing the sell-off," says a senior dealer. The RBI's next policy review is scheduled for January 25 but investors fear the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will move early to combat high food inflation, the dealers say. Among losers, \uc2 Infosys Technologies\uc2  (500209.BY) is down 2.8% at INR3,382.00, \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) is 2.1% lower at INR1,061 and \uc2 Housing Development Finance Corp\uc2 .(500010.BY) is 3.0% lower at INR685.35. The Sensex is likely to find support at 19,680, according to Dow Jones technical analysis(sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0934 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is higher at 9,020 from 8,993 late Thursday, breaching the 9,000 resistance level, tracking the dollar's gains against several Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and as foreigners continue to cash in their gains from local stock and bond markets, traders say. "We don't see Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  today. They may see the rupiah slide as roughly in line with the region's," says a trader with a foreign bank, tipping the pair to trade in a 8,980-9,040 range next week. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0932 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are higher in lackluster trade. Prices rebound after their recent sharp fall, tracking gains late Thursday in U.S. Treasurys. "There's still some demand in the medium-term bonds. Overall, the emerging market remains attractive for investors," says a local dealer. He adds, the focus is on the U.S. non-farm payroll data later in the day and the message from the \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  after its rate decision next week. The market expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hike its policy rate by 25 bps Wednesday. The bid/offer yields of issues due December 2015 are quoted at 3.16%/3.14% from 3.20%/3.18% late Thursday, while October 2017 yields are quoted at 3.44%/3.37% from 3.52%/3.42% and June 2019 yields are at 3.52%/3.45% from 3.65%/3.55%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0931 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF turns lower as expected, the down trend of the last two months is intact and the Fibonacci extension target of 1.2150 is alive and well, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Now at 1.2520; the bank says support at 1.24 may hold for a temporary bounce. However, any break below opens risk to the aforementioned target. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0927 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB ramps up to 30.34-30.36 from 30.20 late Thursday amid more signs the U.S. economy may be emerging from its lengthy slumber, with all eyes now turning to non-farm payrolls later in the day. "People are expecting a good number, so right now a lot of offshore investors are buying back the USD," says a Bangkok-based dealer. He adds, the positive trend for the USD may extend for another one to two weeks, though over the medium term it should swing back in favor of the THB, particularly if the pair fails to break above 31.00. Resistance next week is tipped at 30.50, with support at 30.20. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0921 GMT [Dow Jones] India short-end overnight indexed swaps are flat to mildly lower as the liquidity crunch in the banking system eases, says a dealer at a state-run bank. The one-year OIS is at 7.12%/7.16% from 7.14%/7.17% at Thursday's close, while the five-year OIS rate is at 7.86%/7.90% from 7.88%/7.91% last close. "In the very short-term, these levels are good for receiving for both short and long ends after a surge triggered yesterday by elevated inflation data. For today, the market is tracking the government bond sale due later in the session," the dealer says. He tips five-year OIS in a 7.85%-7.95% band for the session. Indian banks have borrowed about INR600 billion-INR700 billion daily from the RBI over the last three days, compared with more than a INR1 trillion borrowed almost every day in December.(sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0920 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro remains under pressure pretty much across the board as a sell-off in euro zone peripheral government bonds knocks sentiment ahead of the key U.S. non-farm payroll report due at 1330 GMT. The dollar is also stronger against the yen ahead of the labor report which is likely to set direction in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets later in the day with a strong report seen boosting the dollar further, while the yen and Swiss franc would benefit from a weaker report. EUR/USD is down at 1.2976, USD/JPY is up at 83.52, while GBP/USD is little changed at 1.5441. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0916 GMT [Dow Jones] Switzerlands labor market continues to improve despite a rise in December unemployment, with the number of open positions still at high levels even though modestly lower on the month, says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . "The very cold weather in December might however overstate the actual increase in the number of unemployed as the seasonal adjustment probably does not fully offset this impact," says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  economist Thomas Herrmann. (katharina.bart@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares end higher, with the benchmark KLCI +0.3% at 1572.21, led by technology and construction stocks. A local dealer says the market recovered from "shallow profit-taking" earlier in the day; "it's in a healthy consolidation phase now, but with the market flush with funds, it's poised for further gains next week," says the dealer. The index had risen 3.6% this week amid strong buying interest in blue chips. Volume is heavy with 2.53 billion shares changing hands. Maybank IB chartist Lee Cheng Hooi says "firm technical buy signals" remain, and sets the next KLCI targets at 1576, then 1600 and 1618. Market breadth is positive with 525 advancers, compared with 324 decliners. Among advancers, \uc2 Telekom Malaysia\uc2  (4863.KU) is up 4.8% at MYR3.73, Dialog (7277.KU) is 7.0% higher at MYR2.15, \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2 . (2194.KU) is +6.2% at MYR3.10 and Notion (0083.KU) gains 17.6% to MYR2.00 inline with gains among laggards in the tech sector. Among decliners, \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) is off 1.1% at MYR22.38 and Tan Chong (4405.KU) is down 2.3% at MYR5.07. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0914 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD index has cleared resistance at 80.28, and a daily close above here Friday would add to bullish momentum, says \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . Such a close would open the topside to the November peak of 81.44, and then the 50% Fibonacci retracement target of 82.17. The USD index now trades at 81.003. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0910 GMT [Dow Jones] Portugal is likely to access the European Financial Stability Facility soon and several sovereign debt restructurings are likely in the euro area zone in the next few years, says Willem Buiter of Citi. In the European debt crisis "we have only seen the opening and second act, with the rest of the plot still evolving," he says, adding that the risk of default is manifest in all of western Europe, but especially in the periphery of the euro area. Buiter says Ireland's financial support package has only bought time and the current size of the liquidity facilities looks insufficient to fund Spain completely for three years. Portugal/Germany Yield spread is 11 basis points wider at 411.9 bps. (angeline.benoit@dowjones.com) [ 01-07-11 0444ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110107e7170005i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
