xcept that Austria and Luxembourg will \par instead impose a withholding system for a transitional period unless during such \par period they elect otherwise. \par  \par The Bank undertakes that it will ensure that it maintains a paying agent in a \par country which is a member of the European Union (a "Member State") that will not \par be obliged to withhold or deduct tax pursuant to the Savings Directive. \par  \par 2.         United States Tax Matters \par             A) United States Internal Revenue Service Circular 230 Notice: To \par ensure compliance with Internal Revenue Service Circular 230, prospective \par investors are hereby notified that: (a) any discussion of U.S. federal tax \par issues contained or referred to in this Pricing Supplement, the Prospectus or \par any other document referred to herein is not intended or written to be used, and \par cannot be used, by prospective investors for the purpose of avoiding penalties \par that may be imposed on them under the United States Internal Revenue Code; (b) \par such discussions are written for use in connection with the promotion or \par marketing of the transactions or matters addressed herein; and (c) prospective \par investors should seek advice based on their particular circumstances from an \par independent tax advisor. \par             B) The "Tax Matters" section of the Prospectus and any tax \par disclosure in this pricing supplement is of a general nature only, is not \par exhaustive of all possible tax considerations and is not intended to be, and \par should not be construed to be, legal, business or tax advice to any particular \par prospective investor.  Each prospective investor should consult its own tax \par advisor as to the particular tax consequences to it of the acquisition, \par ownership, and disposition of the Notes, including the effects of applicable \par U.S. federal, state, and local tax laws and non-U.S. tax laws and possible \par changes in tax laws. \par             C) Due to a change in law since the date of the Prospectus, the \par second paragraph of "-Payments of Interest" under the United States Holders \par section should be read as follows: "Interest paid by the Bank on the Notes \par constitutes income from sources outside the United States but will, depending on \par your circumstances, be "passive" or "general" income for purposes of computing \par the foreign tax credit." \par  \par             D) Due to a change in law since the date of the Prospectus, the \par fourth paragraph of "-Purchase, Sale and Retirement of the Notes" under the \par United States Holders section should be read as follows: "Capital gain of a \par noncorporate United States holder that is recognized in taxable years beginning \par before January 1, 2011 is generally taxed at a maximum rate of 15% where the \par holder has a holding period greater than one year." \par 3.         Noteholders should consult their own tax advisors concerning the \par consequences of owning the Notes in their particular circumstances under the \par Internal Revenue Code and the laws of any other taxing jurisdiction. \par  \par 4.         Additional Investment Considerations: \par  \par The Notes offered by this Pricing Supplement are complex financial instruments \par and may not be suitable for certain investors. Investors intending to purchase \par the Notes should consult with their tax and financial advisors to ensure that \par the intended purchase meets the investment objective before making such \par purchase. \par  \par There are various risks associated with the Notes including, but not limited to, \par exchange rate risk, price risk and liquidity risk. Investors should consult with \par their own financial, legal and accounting advisors about the risks associated \par with an investment in these Notes, the appropriate tools to analyze that \par investment, and the suitability of the investment in each investor's particular \par circumstances.  Holders of the Notes should also consult with their professional \par tax advisors regarding tax laws applicable to them. \par  \par The Bank will hedge its obligations under the Notes by entering into a swap \par transaction with an affiliate of the Dealer as swap counterparty.  Assuming no \par change in market conditions or any other relevant factors, the price, if any, at \par which the Dealer or another purchaser might be willing to purchase Notes in a \par secondary market transaction is expected to be lower, and could be substantially \par lower, than the original issue price of the Notes.  This is due to a number of \par factors, including that (i) the potential profit to the secondary market \par purchaser of the Notes may be incorporated into any offered price and (ii) the \par cost of funding used to value the Notes in the secondary market is expected to \par be higher than our actual cost of funding incurred in connection with the \par issuance of the Notes.  In addition, the original issue price of the Notes \par included, and secondary market prices are likely to exclude, the projected \par profit that our swap counterparty or its affiliates may realize in connection \par with this swap.  Further, as a result of dealer discounts, mark-ups or other \par transaction costs, any of which may be significant, the original issue price may \par differ from values determined by pricing models used by our swap counterparty or \par other potential purchasers of the Notes in secondary market transactions. \par Payment of each Interest Amount and the Redemption Amount will be based on the \par Applicable Rate, which is a measure of the rate of exchange between the \par Indonesian Rupiah and the USD.  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rates are volatile and will \par affect the holder's return.  In addition, the government of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can from \par time to time \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.  These \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 07-12-10 1332GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par other governmental actions could adversely affect the value of the Notes, as \par well as the yield (in USD terms) on the Notes and the amount payable at maturity \par or upon acceleration.  Even in the absence of governmental action directly \par affecting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rates, political or economic developments in \par Indonesia or elsewhere could lead to significant and sudden changes in the \par exchange rate between the Indonesian Rupiah and the USD. \par  \par The Indonesian Rupiah is an emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  Emerging market \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be subject to particularly substantial volatility, as well as to \par government actions including \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  controls, devaluations and other matters \par which could materially and adversely affect the value of the Notes. \par  \par The methodologies for determining the Applicable Rate may result in a Redemption \par Amount (or Early Redemption Amount, as the case may be) of the Notes, or an \par Interest Amount on the Notes, being significantly less than anticipated or less \par than what an alternative methodology for determining the IDR-USD exchange rate \par would yield. \par  \par INTER-AMERICAN DEVELOPMENT BANK \par By: \par Name:  Edward Bartholomew \par Title:     Chief Financial Officer and General Manager,        Finance \par Department \par  \par  \par This information is provided by RNS \par             The company news service from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par    