\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Up On Korean Tensions;Resistance At 9,010\b0\par\par\uc2 1,643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2010\par 08:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR sharply higher at 9,000 vs 8,965 as rising Korean tensions drives safe haven USD up vs most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dealers say. "I think 9,010 should be the resistance for today. And if the dollar comes back down, it will likely be supported at 8,985," says dealer. Dealers say no sign of suspected Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow USD/IDR rise so far. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0712 GMT [Dow Jones] News of explosion inside North Korea rattles Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with USD/SGD pushing up around 30 ticks to just below 1.3200. Trader at local bank expects pair to remain capped at 1.3200 for now, tips likely support at 1.3150. "I think there's still risk in the Korean peninsula that is going to create more uncertainty in the market," trader says. Notes 1.3200 is about 2.5% above estimated MAS NEER mid-point. Pair now 1.3181 vs 1.3092 early. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0710 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR implied volatilities tad higher as spot inches up on Korean tensions, but market still betting against heavy spillover into INR with impact of Korea unlikely very much beyond initial knee-jerk uptick, says trader with foreign bank; 1-month ATMF at 8.90%/9.90% vs 8.70%/9.70% late Thursday. Tips belly, back-end to remain unaffected, although geopolitical cue from Korea could keep front-end supported at current levels. Adds, real story may be on risk reversals, with front-end risk reversals beginning to find firmer bidding interest, which may pick up pace through session. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0710 GMT [Dow Jones] Asian local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds are at risk of rising inflation, without taxes, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . Says reintroduction of withholding taxes in Korea, Thailand on offshore bond holdings, risks that further restrictions on portfolio flows may be imposed across region merit reassessment of attractiveness of Asian local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds. Notes Korean and Singaporean government bonds sharply down this week. "Most Asian local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bond markets continue to have very flat curve slopes, which implies that most curves currently offer modest compensation to investors for extending duration beyond the belly of the curve," House says. Recommends reducing duration to front-ends in most markets across Asia. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0708 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CHF erodes the psychologically-important resistance at 1.0, but needs a [daily] close above that level to confirm a positive upside bias, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "A close above 1.0 will target 1.0329 [the 38.2% retracement of the move down from June] en route to 1.0480, the upside measured target of the base," says technical analyst Karen Anne Jones. USD/CHF trades at 1.0015. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0706 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF is still under pressure and even an expected weakening of the Swiss KOF leading indicator, at 1030 GMT, is unlikely to boost the pair, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "It's all down to the weakness of the euro, though we've also had positive Swiss trade and employment data recently," says analyst Ulrich Leuchtmann. EUR/CHF trades at 1.3301. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0701 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 1.0% at 22,819.17 vs 23,036.18 midday, tracking A-shares' fall (Shanghai Composite last down 0.9%) on lingering tightening worries, which pressured China, HK stocks since higher-than-expected China CPI data out earlier this month, stoking fears of tightening by Beijing. Still, "(we) understand the temptation to 'go defensive' on China as policy starts to tighten, but with a gradual U.S. demand recovery still ahead, the notion that H-shares have already peaked strikes us as jittery," says Macquarie. Adds, with Chinese, HK policymakers steering flows away from stretched property markets, stocks "may relatively benefit." Blue chips' weakness across the board with 42 of 45 index members down, but \uc2 Li & Fung\uc2  (0494.HK), not exposed to China, HK risks, +2.0% at HK$46.70, outperforming for second day. Market volume tepid at HK$42.33 billion. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0700 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD higher, tracking USD strength vs EUR, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; some investors repatriate funds amid rising tensions on Korean Peninsula ahead of planned U.S.-South Korea naval exercises Sunday, says trader at China-based bank. Tips pair in 7.7620-7.7650 band near-term. "I've spotted some overseas banks covering short positions. I expect the pair to rise further." Pair last at 7.7623, vs 7.7588 yesterday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones] Investor apathy in Singapore stock market persists, keeping overall volume light, STI tad lower. Index off 0.2% at 3152.20, still in tight morning range of 3151-3164. Any further downside not likely to push STI below current November low of 3118 set this week. "We expect the local market to remain rangebound today in a sleepy session," says CIMB. New listing \uc2 Sabana REIT\uc2  (M1GU.SG) falls prey to soft market conditions, last at S$1.01 vs S$1.05 IPO price, although off early low of S$0.97. Market breadth negative at almost 2 decliners for each gainer vs neutral by end of morning session. Among blue chips, notable decliners include SembMarine (S51.SG), off 3.0% at S$4.88, CityDev (C09.SG), off 1.3% at S$12.18, ST Engineering (S63.SG), off 1.2% at S$3.24. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower; Kospi ends down 1.4%, extending fall after AFP reports North Korea says planned U.S.-South Korea naval exercises would bring peninsula closer to war; Nikkei down 0.4%; S&P/ASX +0.1%; HSI down 1.0%; Taiex down 0.5%; Sensex down 1.1%; Shanghai Composite down 0.9%; STI down 0.2%; Indonesian shares down 0.5%; Philippine shares down 1.1%; SET down 0.5%; NZX-50 flat; KLCI down 0.4%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, USD/JPY at 83.90, near 8-week high, vs 83.56 in NY late Thursday, EUR/USD at 1.3292 vs 1.3369, EUR/JPY at 111.51 vs 111.75. PBOC adviser Li Daokui says October interest rate hike signals further rate adjustments likely, priority shifted to containing inflationary expectations over maintaining fast, steady growth. State-run China Securities Journal reports, citing unnamed sources, some Chinese investment banks asked property sector clients to turn to Hong Kong bourse to raise money. State-run Shanghai Securities News reports, citing unnnamed source, China will continue to target 8.0% GDP growth in 2011. Japan October core CPI down 0.6% on-year vs down 1.1% in September, down 0.6% expected. Kyodo reports Japan economic minister Kaieda says worried about consumer spending from next year; not mulling additional stimulus now. RBA Gov. Glenn Stevens says policy settings little tighter than average but appropriate for period ahead. Korea October current account surplus $5.37 billion vs September's revised $3.95 billion. BOK Official says exports to remain robust in November; current account surplus to easily exceed $30 billion this year. Thai Prime Minister reiterates expects Thai GDP to grow close to 8% this year, expects BOT to continue to enter \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but only to limit volatility. Singapore October manufacturing output +31% vs revised +26.8% in September, +26.5% expected. Philippines September trade surplus $751 million vs deficit $32 million year earlier. Spot gold at $1,367.90/oz, down $7.20 vs NY close. January Nymex crude oil futures at $83.62/bbl down 24 cents compared with Wednesday's New York settlement. (john.phillips@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may rise, possibly to 87.00 sometime in December on any further gains in U.S. Treasury yields, says Hideki Amikura, deputy general manager at Nomura Trust and Banking. "The main driver for further gains would be continued rises in Treasury yields, particularly in the short zone." Many investors watch spreads between short-dated U.S.-Japan government debt as indicator for direction to take on pair. Says continued tensions on Korean peninsula may also help USD/JPY. While both USD, JPY considered safe havens, investors likely to prefer USD to JPY given Japan's proximity to conflict. "The tensions will probably continue to prompt some global investors to consider trimming their yen holdings further." U.S., South Korea to commence 4-day joint naval exercise in Yellow Sea on Sunday, following North Korea's shelling of South Korean island there earlier this week. USD/JPY last at 83.86 after hitting fresh near 8-week high at 83.92.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0639 GMT [Dow Jones] Credit Agricole says PBOC's recent "hawkish" rhetoric reflects mounting price pressures. House expects monetary tightening to accelerate as inflation likely to remain elevated in medium term, rise towards 5% on-year by mid-2011. Revises rate hike call to 100 bps vs 50 bps by mid-2011. Expects 200 bp increase in RRR within same time frame. Revises down end-2011 USD/CNY target to 6.30 from 6.45, says faster CNY rise likely to limit imported inflation. "We believe that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is behind the curve and is only at the beginning of a scramble to control prices." PBOC Deputy Gov. Hu Xiaolian said earlier \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to normalize money, credit growth using rates, required reserve ratio, open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (jean.yung@dowjones.com) [ 11-26-10 0215ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101126e6bq00047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
