\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:USD/TWD Down; \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Eyed; 30.00 Floor Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,542 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2010\par 03:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD down at 30.075 vs 30.600 late yesterday; pair remains weighed by foreign fund inflows, exporters' stop-loss selling, says local bank trader. Pair also weighed by Commercial Times report, which cites Taiwan Vice Premier Sean Chen's comment Wednesday criticizing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Trader notes exporters, banks "have been complaining about the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy," as TWD closing level in Taipei doesn't represent real trading conditions. Trader says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may taper off its daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Tips pair to test 30.000 support today. Adds, investors ignore Taiwan legislature financial committee's proposal to impose management fees on speculative funds, as players expect management fee wouldn't stop foreign inflows amid global excess liquidity conditions. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan stocks off 0.1% at 8444.97, shares tipped consolidate in tight range around current level on uncertainty ahead of local mayoral elections. Some hot money from bonds entering equities market as Taiwan government tightens restrictions on some short-term local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  products, but slowing total foreign inflows, profit-taking on tech shares balancing out upside, says KGI Securities analyst Randy Chang. "Right now, no one expects the G-20 to solve anything. We don't see any kind of consensus, so we don't think it will have much impact on the market," he says. Tips Taiex in 8400-8480 range today. Acer (2353.TW) off 0.8% at NT$89.50 on weak October sales. Hon Hai (2317.TW) off 0.9% at NT$115.50, Formosa Plastics (1301.TW) off 2.0% at NT$92.00 on profit taking. Cathay Financial (2882.TW) +0.3% at NT$48.45, China Airlines (2610.TW) +0.7% at NT$23.30. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] China October CPI up 4.4% on year, vs 4.0% tipped in Dow Jones poll, September's +3.6%; growth fuels rising worries about inflation, feeding debate between analysts on extent of upward price pressures. China October food prices +10.1% on year, many economists believe food prices main driver, inflation will decline in coming months with autumn harvest. But others see inflation spreading to non-food items, believe it's being driven by ample money, credit conditions. Data come after China raises banks' reserve requirement ratio by 50 bps (effective from next Tuesday), 4th such hike this year, likely aiming to relieve some inflationary pressures; China also hiked interest rates for 1st time in almost 3 years in October to combat inflation, asset bubbles. Some economists say China entering cycle of more rate hikes. Adding to inflation concerns, PPI up 5.0% in October, vs 4.3% rise in September, Dow Jones poll tip of 4.6%, on rising commodity prices globally. (owen.fletcher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely higher, tracking gains in many Asian markets after shares in U.S. ended higher overnight, traders say; expect main index to top 3780 after shares ended +0.5% yesterday at fresh record high of 3756.967. But trader at First Asia Capital says "buying won't be as huge as in previous sessions as local funds may take profit after recent gains." Bellwether Telkom (TLKM.JK) may gain after ADRs +1.8% in NY, while car maker Astra (ASII.JK) may rise on bargain buying. Profit-taking likely hit nickel miner Inco (INCO.JK) on lower nickel prices. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] China's fixed-asset investment, industrial output data show economic growth steady after Beijing launched series of steps earlier this year to cool investment in real estate and other sectors with overcapacity, but inflation worry likely will prompt government to consider more tightening steps, such as further reserve requirement ratio hikes. But any tightening steps may have to be launched prudently to avoid hurting economic growth too much. Urban fixed-asset investment +24.4% on-year in January-October period, vs January-September's +24.5%, in line with economists forecast of +24.4%; October industrial output +13.1% on year, slightly slower vs September's +13.3%, expectation of +13.4%. See: =DATA SNAP: China January-October Urban FAI Up 24.4%; Expected Up 24.4% (victoria.ruan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] MKS Finance says gold market characterized by volatile and choppy trading. "We expect gold to remain choppy in the near-term, closely tied to the moves in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite renewed concerns over European sovereign debt." Spot gold at $1,402.60/oz, down 80 cents after bouncing off low of $1,383.55/oz overnight. Gold in euros at EUR1,015.70/oz, down EUR3.70 after again failing to surpass its June 7 record close of EUR1,020.50 yesterday. Precious metals wavering on some suggestions Fed's quantitative easing plans could be curtailed if U.S. economic data continues to improve; U.S. new jobless claims fell to better than expected 435,000 in week ended Nov. 6. However Fed's announcement late Wednesday it will purchase $105 billion in Treasurys during next month possibly more resonant for gold bulls. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippines shares tad higher, with investors starting to hunt for bargains after 4-session, 4.5% retreat; bellwether PSEi +0.1% to 4200.85 in light volume, with support pegged at 4150, resistance 4250; market breadth positive, with gainers beating decliners 46 to 23. Leading gainers, \uc2 Aboitiz Power\uc2  (AP.PH) +5.0% at PHP30.50, Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH) +2.0% at PHP11.02 and Metro Pacific Investments (MPI.PH) +0.5% at PHP4.03, which all recent decliners; retreaters led by PLDT (TEL.PH), down 0.1% to PHP2,416. "I expect a technical bounce today after the 4-day retreat. The market has fallen too fast, too soon," says SB Equities research head Erwin Balita. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open higher, led by coal miners, but concerns about further monetary tightening to rein in inflation, limits index upside. Shanghai Composite Index last +0.1% at 3119.49, may turn lower to test support at 3080 (near 10-day moving average) after release of October CPI due 0200 GMT. Expectations are that China's CPI likely rose 4.4% last month, "if confirmed, it will definitely intensify concerns about a further interest rate hike in the short term," says CSC International analyst Zhang Yuheng. Coal producers rise on expected higher seasonal sales in winter: \uc2 China Shenhua Energy\uc2  (601088.SH) +2.3% at CNY29.20, Yanzhou Coal Mining (600188.SH) +3.9% at CNY32.15. Shenzhen Index flat at 1390.00. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF says coupon on 5-year JGBs at auction later in day at 0.30%; Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities strategist Katsutoshi Inadome says tender "should meet strong demand." Says buying from Japanese banks with excess liquidity likely robust, also current yield levels of around 0.35% close to fair value, and that's attractive. Lead December futures last up 0.06 at 142.58, 10-year yield last unchanged at 0.990%, 5-year yield last down 0.5 bp at 0.345%.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] November HSI futures +0.5% at 24,520, indicating cash market may open higher after DJIA's modest rise. Still contract ends yesterday at 93-point discount, suggests HK market may witness further consolidation near-term. November open interest down 1,927 lots at 116,434. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR falls to 8,885, lowest level since June 6, 2007, vs 8,895 yesterday, as capital inflows continue, partly due to intact bearish sentiment on greenback globally, dealers say. 3 dealers note no indications of Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow pair's slide. "I expect Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to start buying the dollar at IDR8,800 to slow the rupiah's appreciation," dealer says; 8,880-8,900 range tipped for day. Unabating inflows of short-term capital have prompted Bank Indonesia to consider imposing further measures to avoid a sudden reversal of these funds, such as possibility of imposing longer a holding period than one month currently required for its money market securities. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY may fall to 112.50 if China CPI, PPI for October is strong, fueling speculation that China may take hawkish monetary policy soon, which in turn likely to weigh on share prices, riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as EUR, says Tomohiro Nishida, senior dealer at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking; China inflation data due 0200 GMT; EUR/JPY last at 113.41. In that scenario, EUR/USD may fall towards 1.3700; pair last at 1.3801.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com) [ 11-10-10 2119ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101111e6bb0001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
