\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesia Shares End +0.5%; 3800 Cap Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,558 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2010\par 10:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0920 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares end +0.5% at fresh record high of 3756.967 in moderate volume, boosted by foreign buying in most telecom, commodity-related stocks amid hopes for strong FY earnings, traders say; expect shares to continue to gain tomorrow, with main index likely testing 3800-resistance level. "Healthy foreign-buying was the main driver for the gain of the main index," says trader at Erdhika Securities. Foreigners net buyers of IDR205 billion worth of shares vs IDR150 billion net buys yesterday. Among gainers, steel maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Krakatau Steel\f28 \uc2 \f2  (KRAS.JK) +49% at IDR1,270 on debut, heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) +3.7% at IDR8,450, while nickel producer Inco (INCO.JK) +2.1% at IDR5,000. Profit-taking hits car maker Astra (ASII.JK) down 1.2% at IDR57,450. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0914 GMT [Dow Jones] Nomura expects EUR/CHF to trade higher and says it's therefore bought a three-month EUR/CHF 1.3700 call for 1.04% of EUR face value. "Our first target is a short term (one month) recovery in the cross back to its recent high above 1.38. With time value still in the option, this would broadly cover the premium (with current vol and skew if spot is at 1.38 in one month the call would be worth 1%, almost exactly covering the premium)," it says. "The next target is a move to 1.4150, the mid-point of the late May-early June range. This would provide a 3:1 payout. We allocate $10m within our $100m model portfolio," Nomura adds. Spot EUR/CHF trades at 1.3395. (martin.essex@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD slips back a little as investors continue to readjust their positions ahead of this weekend's G-20 summit in Seoul. Rising tensions, fueled by China reporting a larger than expected trade surplus for October, should help the USD as risk sentiment subsides. The EUR is a little higher, with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  getting some respite despite continued concerns over a euro zone debt default. The market is now waiting to see if a Portuguese debt auction later in the day fares as well as one did for Greece on Tuesday. The EUR is up at $1.3811 while the USD has fallen a little to Y81.68. The GBP is up at $1.6001 even though there are fears that the Bank of England will give a downbeat assessment of the UK economy in its quarterly Inflation Report later in the day. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0911 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares end higher to close at new all-time high; KLCI ends +0.1% at 1528.01."The FBM KLCI is now in uncharted territory. The mid-cap and lower liner stocks have also begun to move, making the current rise a very broadbased one," says Maybank IB. "We believe that the FBM KLCI should now head up towards the 1533 and 1553 target areas." Market breadth positive with 441 decliners vs 420 advancers; \uc2 YTL Corp\uc2  (4677.KU) +4.2% at MYR8.39, \uc2 Malaysian Resources\uc2  (1651.KU) +3.7% at MYR2.25. Decliners include \uc2 AMMB Holdings\uc2  (1015.KU) down 1.6% at MYR6.20, Genting (3182.KU) down 1.1% at MYR10.68, AirAsia (5099.KU) down 1.2% at MYR2.44. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0911 GMT [Dow Jones] There's been a subtle change in USD/JPY's fortunes says \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 's Nicole Elliott. A small spike low and a bullish engulfing candle hints that the spot will hold above its November low of 80.21 for at least another week or two. The spot is currently struggling with its 26DMA around 81.84, however, Elliott expects it to make a fresh interim high, however, she still favors small shorts from 81.85 with a stop above 82.55 for a first downside target of 80.85. USD/JPY now at 81.68 from the day's high of 81.91. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0910 GMT [Dow Jones] Irish and Portuguese bond yields continue to widen versus bunds Wednesday ahead of the Portuguese October 2016 and June 2020 bond auctions. "The disastrous performance of Portuguese bonds has everything to do with enormous doubts about the Portuguese Budget, both initially in terms of it being passed, and then in terms of its overall feasibility," says Marc Ostwald, Monument Securities strategist. "That the risks in Portugal have risen sharply in recent months is indisputable, but the chances of a debt rescheduling or default still look to be remote, and as such the heightened risks appear to be more than adequately priced into current yields, and a decent cover should be achievable." The Irish situation shows no signs of improving any time soon, with uncertainty of the December 7 budget passing through parliament adding to EFSF bailout concerns. The 10-year Irish/German yield spread is 4bps wider at 557.5bps and the 10-year Portuguese/German spread is 3.5bps wider at 439bps, according to Tradeweb. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0910 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank Indonesia's move to drop 3-month certificates, or SBI, in today's auction signifies \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's concerns on speculative flows, may extend minimum holding period of SBIs, says OCBC; move "is a bid to continue pushing investors to go into the longer-end papers, which in turn is meant to reduce speculative flows in the market." Adds, concerns on sudden outflows remain; "we would not be surprised if they were to implement a longer minimum holding period for SBI bills to 3 months from 1 month" currently. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0908 GMT [Dow Jones] Nomura still reckons GBP will outperform but prefers to remove the directional USD bias inherent in the short EUR/GBP trade it entered on November 2. It argues that EUR/GBP tends to be positively correlated with EUR/USD, so a renewed USD sell-off could hurt the trade. As a result, it is switching to a GBP basket trade, with two thirds invested against EUR and one third against USD. The EUR/GBP trade is closed for a profit of around 1.3% of the amount invested. "We enter this new basket trade at 0.8620 EUR/GBP and 1.5990 GBP/USD. We will run a 1.0% basket stop loss... We allocate $10m within our $100m model portfolio," Nomura says. (martin.essex@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT [Dow Jones] As tensions rise ahead of the G-20 summit starting this Thursday in Seoul, expect the USD to benefit, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . The bank notes that the whole issue of QEII, which had initially hit the USD, has now been pushed onto the back burner, while news of China's even larger than expected trade surplus will fuel discontent. "The USD often benefits if there is tension on the international scene. As a result EUR/USD might ease further initially," the bank says. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0905 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: China's October industrial production likely +13.4% on-year vs September's +13.3%, according to Dow Jones poll of 12 economists. Survey also shows urban fixed asset investment likely +24.4% in January-October period on-year vs January-September's +24.5%. Survey shows China's industrial output, investment remain relatively stable, despite cooling measures this year such as first ever rate hike in almost three years in October, continuous measures to cool property market. "Industry seems to have achieved a soft landing, but with industrial output growth slightly weaker than during the pre-crisis period," says Capital Economics economist Mark Williams. Data due Thursday. (li.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0901 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia's real GDP growth likely slowed to 5.6% on-year in 3Q vs +8.9% in 2Q, +10.1% in 1Q says RHB Research; 3Q industrial production growth slows to +4.3% on-year vs +11.1% in 2Q. "Going forward, we expect the global economy to expand at a more moderate pace in 2011," says RHB. Adds, in tandem with slowdown in global demand, country's exports may also slow in 2011 but domestic demand growth may stay resilient but grow at more moderate pace of 5.5% in 2011 vs +5.9% tipped for 2010. House expects real GDP growth to slow to 5.0% in 2011 from +7.3% forecast for 2010. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0856 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR lower at 8,895 vs 8,904 late yesterday in line with USD's weakness vs regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers say; no sign of Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent pair from sliding below 8,900, two dealers say. "I was surprised that Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't seem to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," one of them says; adds pair may slide further tomorrow, but profit taking evident in local bond market today could limit USD/IDR slide to 8,885; 8,910 resistance tipped. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com) [ 11-10-10 0420ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101110e6ba00068\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
