eper sell-off is on the cards," says Richard Adcock, \uc2 UBS\uc2  technical strategist. "The bearish themes are backed up by the momentum/trending relationship, with the MACD still below its zero line, and we will now look for a closing cross lower in stochastics over the next few days to confirm the resumption of the downside that can test the more important longer term support at 121.93/122.22." Support lies at 123.20, 122.22 and 121.93. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0849 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex trapped in narrow band, now flat at 20,915.29 on absence of fresh triggers, subdued cues from Asian peers. "Most of the major company results are already out and there are no major events to look forward to," says Prakash Diwan of Networth Stock-Broking. Another analyst tips index in 20,500-21,200 band near-term. Among actives, \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) +3.3% at INR1312 as 2Q net profit surged to INR22.23 billion, up vs INR217.8 million year earlier. However, \uc2 Hindalco Industries\uc2  (500440.BY), which posted lower-than-expected 2Q net profit late Tuesday, down 3.6% at INR223.45 and Cipla (500087.BY) down 1.7% at INR343.70. (swagata.gupta@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0846 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia's wider balance of payments data is likely positive for IDR and local bond market, analysts say. Indonesia's BOP stood at $6.96 billion in 3Q vs $5.42 billion in 2Q; contributed by $6.48 billion of capital and financial account (vs $4.37 billion in 2Q), $1.31 billion of current account (vs $1.8 billion in 2Q). "The erosion of the current account surplus is happening at a gradual pace...For the near future, portfolio inflows may continue to be rapid especially in the wake of the Fed's QE2," Bank Danamon's Helmi Arman says. ING shares similar view, but warns not getting too excited until inflation subsides. "We will turn bullish on government bonds after the threat of an above-6.0% inflation print has passed." (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0842 GMT [Dow Jones] The zloty is higher as foreign investors are moving into Poland with its relatively high interest rates, a trader says. Adds the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to test EUR/PLN3.8850 level, vital resistance toward further strengthening in anticipation of rate hikes. EUR/PLN is at 3.8901 from 3.9017, USD/PLN at 2.8190 from 2.8398. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones]USD/INR implied volatilities lower on moribund spot, but finding support at current levels from reviving concerns on health of global economy, says trader with foreign bank; 1-month ATMF at 7.50%/8.25% vs 7.75%/8.50% late Tuesday. Also, approaching G-20 meet also adding to demand for protection from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings, bolstering front-end of curve, says trader. Adds, some interest on 3-month to 1-year vega neutral spreads, likely to cover gap in risk bucket rather than play on view for curve. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0839 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP extend advance to 3rd straight session amid continued USD-buying; pair at 43.40 vs 43.70 late Tuesday amid heavy trade. "We expect more of the same tomorrow," says local bank trader; adds, pair may head towards 43.55 resistance Thursday, next support at 43.25. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0839 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD suffered a sharp decline in late trade Tuesday and that extended to a low of 1.3735 in Asia. Citi says they saw good size selling at the lows however amid talk of sovereign buying the spot rallied and their head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading in Singapore, Per Nor says there seems no reason to be short unless the 1.3730 level breaks. He favored a squeeze toward 1.3820-30 which is just about to happen. EUR/USD now trades at 1.3814. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD's 50-tick rally to the day's high of 1.3821 seems to have been triggered by strong buying out of the Middle East, says a London-based trader. The spot now trades at 1.3819, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  pegs nearest resistance at 1.3865. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD slightly lower late at 30.600 vs 30.630 yesterday, but off intraday low of 30.110 with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of USD-buying, say two local traders. "Right now what's important are foreign inflows. As far as we can tell, recent government restrictions on hot money will have no effect whatsoever," says one local trader. Yesterday, Taiwan government decides to tighten restrictions on foreign investments in some short-term local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  products. Trader adds, downward pressure on pair to continue in medium-term; pair to trade in 30.150-30.250 range tomorrow with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to step in again late to support pair near 36.000. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0836 GMT [Dow Jones] Short EUR/long G-10 commodity block \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still the post-QE trade, says Citi. The bank reckons that although risk sentiment may have fallen back, its decline has been limited. "Instead of taking risk completely off the table, investors are selective leaving their positions in commodities and commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  little changed. This could continue for as long as fears that global recovery may falter are negated by the recent Fed quantitative easing," the U.S. bank says. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com) [ 11-10-10 0356ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101110e6ba0005r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
