\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Lower, 8,900 Support Seen N/T\b0\par\par\uc2 1,364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2010\par 09:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0847 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR lower late at 8,903 vs 8,922 yesterday, tracking USD weakness against many Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with Bank Indonesia suspected of bidding around 8,900 level, two dealers say. "The pair is heavily biased down," dealer at foreign bank says; adds suspects Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  started \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  around 8,920, then suspected of bidding around 8,900 level; says 8,900 likely will become new support level. One dealer says suspects Bank Indonesia may have bought around $50 million-$100 million at IDR8,905 to stem IDR's rise.(andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0846 GMT [Dow Jones] Spain has remained relatively well insulated of late, while Ireland and Portugal continue to suffer from negative investor sentiment, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  ahead of Spain's EUR3 billion to EUR4 billion auction of the new 3.25% April 2016 bond. "We don't, therefore, expect the higher levels of volatility being witnessed in the weaker peripheral markets to have a significant bearing on Thursday's auction," \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says. Spanish auction results are due between 0930 GMT and 0945 GMT. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0840 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar is coming under heavy selling pressure Thursday following the FOMC's latest attempts to refloat the U.S. economy. EUR/USD trades above 1.42 for the first time since mid-January, GBP/USD trades at a fresh 9 month high of 1.6185, and USD/CHF falls to a fresh one week low of 0.9674. NZD/USD is soaring with the added help of strong employment data, and is now one cent up on the day at a fresh 29 month high of 0.7889. AUD/USD trades at a fresh post float high of 1.0087, and USD/CAD a three week low of 1.0047. USD/JPY is sidelined around 80.96. All this leaves the USD index trading at a fresh 2010 low of 75.983. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0840 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW down at a 6-month low of 1,107.5 late Thursday vs 1,110.2 yesterday on U.S. Fed's quantitative easing plan, but manages to stay well above psychologically important 1,100 mark largely due to suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of USD during domestic trade, according to 5 Seoul-based traders, with one of those traders estimating suspected buying to have been worth about $500 million. "I don't think the authorities are going to easily give way to the KRW1,100 support," says local bank trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0836 GMT [Dow Jones] India's banking system cash shortfall may touch INR550 billion by end November, despite RBI efforts to ease liquidity as large government balances, higher levels of cash in circulation during festive season drain liquidity from system, says ICICI Bank Global Markets in report. Notes, share sale of \uc2 Power Grid Corp\uc2 . may also drain out INR86 billion from already cash-strapped system (IPO opens Nov. 9). But house expects RBI to infuse between INR160 billion-INR180 billion in November to help ease cash crunch. RBI to buyback INR120 billion of bonds later in day while buyback of INR264 billion still outstanding. Still, house recommends paying 1-year overnight indexed swaps, expects it to rise 6.85% level (last 6.60%/6.62%) in near term with further flattening in swap curve as cash is tipped to remain tight. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0835 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR lower late at 3.0820 vs 3.0840 late yesterday after FOMC decision overnight. Two dealers say \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of buying dollars at 3.0820 earlier today. One dealer says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of buying dollars since morning session to limit pair's fall, expects pair will continue to decline Monday but says size of decline may depend on "how much \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will let it fall." Malaysian markets closed Friday for local holiday. Tips support at 3.0820, then 3.0800. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0834 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP slides to fresh 30-month low, dragged down by continuing stream of remittance from overseas Filipino workers amid broad USD weakness. However, pair's retreat tempered by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounting to around $200 million-$300 million at 43.50-43.51 level, say local bank traders. "The upside of the peso is (suspected of) being challenged by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , thus, the peso is being a laggard compared with other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But if we continue to experience heavy inflows, then there's no way (for the pair) but down. The objective of the (suspected) \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (moves) would only be to moderate" the peso's appreciation, says one dealer. Pair 42.53 from 42.59 late Wednesday amid heavy trading; pair tipped to stay within 42.40-42.55 Friday with downside bias. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0832 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD has breached resistance at 1.4080 and the October high of 1.4160, and this clears the way for a move toward the 2010 high of 1.4445 and its two year resistance line at 1.4580, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 's Karen Jones. Now at 1.4177, she pegs immediate resistance at 1.4217 and 1.4264. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0830 GMT [Dow Jones] The U.S. economic model "looks broken" and policy makers "are running out of levers to pull," leaving the Federal Reserve trying to save the day single-handedly, says John Stopford, co-head of fixed income at \uc2 Investec Asset Management\uc2 . The Fed can't do much to influence real U.S. growth, but can try to push up the trend rate of nominal growth through higher inflation, so despite Fed bond buying pushing yields down, in the longer term, "the reflationary bias of U.S. monetary policy should ensure that the 30 year bull market in government bonds comes to an end, and QE2 may herald the start of a new bear market." (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0830 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts move lower in early trading Thursday, following US Treasurys and bunds lower after the FOMC decision to restart QE. The market now awaits the BOE monetary policy decision due at 1200 GMT and US nonfarm payrolls Friday. Consensus expect policy to be left unchanged. Halifax's UK October house price survey showed a 1.8% rise on the month and a 1.2% rise on the year Thursday rebounding from September's record slump. At 0945 GMT, Chancellor Osborne will be questioned by the Treasury Select Committee on the Comprehensive Spending Review. December gilts are down 0.13 at 123.98 and the 10-year gilt is down at 114.097, yielding 3.005%. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0828 GMT [Dow Jones] So what does the Bank of Japan do now that the Fed has announced its QEII? The USD has shown only minimal losses against the JPY so far, with FM Noda warning that Japan will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if necessary. "We think the policy board will more than likely refrain from further easing at this stage, and focus instead on finalizing the design of the new asset purchase facility which is not yet \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . The BOJ is expected to announce its decision at about 0400 GMT Friday. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0823 GMT [Dow Jones] The lack of concession in French OAT government bonds ahead of Thursday's EUR7.5 billion to EUR9 billion auction may indicate a large short base in France due to its position as consensus short in the highly rated euro zone core segment, and this should be supportive of the auctions, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 's strategist Khrisnamoorthy Sooben. Separately, \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  says that the value of convexity will support demand for the 4% April 2060 OAT, one of the three OATs on offer, and the longest-dated bond in the euro zone. Results are due at 0950 GMT. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com) [ 11-04-10 0447ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101104e6b40005h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
