\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Bank Indonesia Keeps Rates Steady; Inflows Bigger Concern\b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2010\par 10:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , economist on liquidity management, inflation)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Farida Husna and Andreas Ismar \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Bank Indonesia Thursday kept its key interest rate unchanged at a historic low of 6.50%, and reiterated that it views a wall of foreign capital inflows as a greater threat than price pressures.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stance strengthens market views that it may keep rates unchanged for the rest of this year, while potentially moving to further strengthen liquidity management after already introducing several measures.\par \par \uc2 The rate decision was in line with market expectations as slowing inflation is providing room for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep borrowing costs low. All 13 economists in a Dow Jones Newswires poll expected Bank Indonesia to keep the overnight policy rate at 6.50%, the level it has been at since August 2009, to help spur growth in Southeast Asia's largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's consumer price index edged down to 5.67% on-year in October from 5.80% in September, the official Central Statistics Agency said Monday. On-month inflation eased to 0.06% from 0.44%.\par \par \uc2 "The Board of Governors underlines that liquidity management is more important amid huge capital inflows and excess liquidity," Bank Indonesia said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia has ordered commercial banks to raise their reserves by 3 percentage points from Nov. 1 to absorb excess funds and help reduce inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 The move was taken after it issued a set of policies in July, including instituting a one-month holding period for its short-term securities, or SBIs, in a bid to curb hot money flows and reducing risk of sudden capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 Several central bankers in recent weeks have also aired the idea of further lengthening the minimum holding period of SBIs and term deposits.\par \par \uc2 "The possible implementation of quasi capital controls is likely to be done in a very careful and well communicated manner, so as to keep the stability of the domestic financial conditions intact," OSK DMG economist Enrico Tanuwidjaja said.\par \par \uc2 "Our end-2010 forecast for the Bank Indonesia rate remains unchanged at 6.50% and possible rate hikes might only start to materialize around the second half" of next year, Tanuwidjaja added. He forecast the policy rate to reach 7.50% by the end of 2011.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia also announced Thursday that its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves jumped to $91.8 billion at the end of October from $86.5 billion in September.\par \par \uc2 "If we look at the significant jump in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for October, it is also clear that Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 INdonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  of late, and this is again another sign of concerns over capital inflows," OCBC economist Gundy Cahyadi said.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia has said inflows totaled IDR115 trillion ($12.92 billion) between January and September.\par \par \uc2 -By Farida Husna and Andreas Ismar, Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 39831277; andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com [ 04-11-10 0919GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101104e6b4000zj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
