\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Higher; 8,920-8,935 Tipped Tuesday\b0\par\par\uc2 1,424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2010\par 11:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0930 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR late higher at 8,930 vs 8,920 yesterday as local players take profit after Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's suspected active \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recently, showing its resolve not to let greenback fall below IDR8,920. "Market expects Bank Indonesia to continue buying around 8,920 in the near term, providing support for the pair," dealer at local bank says; adds pair remains biased down partly due to robust foreign appetite for local stocks, bonds; tips pair to trade in 8,920-8,935 range Tuesday. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0929 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds higher in lackluster trade. Mild buying demand seen in short-dated notes in early trade. Players hibernating ahead of \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 's policy rate decision Wednesday, though they increasingly believe \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep benchmark rate at 1.75%, says local dealer. Adds, if absence of fresh drivers persists, sentiment likely to remain in doldrums Tuesday. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.43%/2.40% vs 2.44%/2.40% late Friday, November 2016 at 2.55%/2.50% vs 2.58%/2.55%, June 2019 at 2.78%/2.72% vs 2.81%/2.78%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0919 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares end down 0.8% at 3566.918 in light volume, but off intraday low of 3557.371, as weaker Asian markets inspire local funds to continue to take profit, traders say; expect shares to continue to fall tomorrow; tip support at 3530. "The selloff in most consumer- and select commodity-related stocks drove the main index lower despite healthy inflows," says fund manager at state-owned bank. Profit-taking hits food maker \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) down 2.5% at IDR4,957, consumer goods producer \uc2 Unilever\uc2  (UNVR.JK) down 4.4% at IDR17,250. Bucking trend, gas, oil producer Medco (MEDC.JK) +15% at IDR4,225 on news \uc2 Pertamina\uc2  will buy a 27.9% stake in Medco. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0914 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shares end down as investors take profits after recent highs as 2011 budget turns into "non-event" with no major positive surprises; KLCI down 0.6% at 1,480.70; "From the market's standpoint, Budget 2011 is again a non-event," AmResearch says; but adds upside remains as liquidity may continue to flow into emerging markets as additional monetary easing by central banks of developed markets may continue. Dealer says market likely to take cues from external markets for now; "next catalyst on the local front will come from corporate earnings, which will pick up pace in November," adds dealer; tips range-bound trading Tuesday with negative bias. Market breadth negative with 468 decliners vs 299 advancers; decliners include PLUS Expressways (5052.KU) down 2.7% at MYR4.34 after analysts say UEM, EPF's MYR23 billion takeover offer "unattractive;" SP Setia (8664.KU) down 3.1% at MYR4.73, \uc2 Malaysian Resources\uc2  (1651.KU) down 2.9% at MYR2.02; plantation stocks leading gainers with Batu Kawan (1899.KU) +5.7% at MYR15.58.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR extends gains mirroring EUR's retreat vs greenback while demand from oil importers also supports, dealers say. Pair at 44.37, off low of 44.17 intra-session, 44.12 late Friday. Trader with state-run bank says, pair driven higher as some participants covering short dollar positions despite USD inflows directed at \uc2 Coal India Ltd\uc2 .'s up to $3.5 billion share sale, which opened for subscription Monday. Trader tips 44.40 as key resistance for pair. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0858 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD up late at 30.885 vs 30.793 Friday supported by steep drop in local stock market today, Taiex ends off 1.8% at 8060.54. "Regional weakness (in stock markets) and foreign investors selling Taiwan shares today led to a temporary halt in the Taiwan dollar's appreciation," says local trader. Foreign investors sold net NT$9.71 billion in Taiwan shares today. Adds, he expects pair to continue to fall in the coming week, tips 30.80-30.90 band for tomorrow. (paul.mozur@dowjones.net)\par \par \uc2 0855 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/USD has reached an extremely overextended position and risks are now on the downside says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . Fed chairman Bernanke's more cautious approach to QE on Friday triggered a USD correction and puts GBP/USD, which has been a main gainer on the prospects of more US liquidity, as most at risk of a correction. BNP also notes a deterioration in UK fundamentals which will likely lead to both the BOE and governments revising their growth forecasts lower. GBP/USD now trades at 1.5870 and the bank would use any uptick to 1.60 to short with a target of 1.55 and a protective stop around Friday's high of 1.6110. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0853 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/HUF climbs higher as markets receive details of Hungary's planned extraordinary tax package for the telecommunication, energy and retail sectors, but market participants expect further details to come later Monday. EUR/HUF is at 276.76 from 273.9 Friday. \uc2 MKB Bank\uc2  expects hectic trading during Prime Minister Viktor Orban's speech in parliament at 1100 GMT, adding the pair could rise to 279 if it breaks resistance at 276. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says markets especially gauge any news in the speech on long-awaited structural reforms; sees the cross trading between 274.50-278.00. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CZK trades higher slightly at 24.529-24.549 from 24.51 late Friday as EUR rebounds from last week's losses, in part driven by the weakening of Czech stocks, a local trader says. The cross also follows similar moves on other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region, the trader says, adding that in all the EUR/CZK is likely to stay in its current range just around 24.500.(leos.rousek@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0836 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate inches up by 60 bps Monday as banks scramble to cover cash positions in a week witnessing country's largest share sale plus 3 retail bond sales, says trader with state-run bank. Overnight call rate at 6.75% vs 6.15% Friday close, collateralized lending and borrowing obligation rate at 5.75% vs 6.00% at open. Trader says call rate may gradually move much higher this week as individuals may withdraw heavily to subscribe to share, debt sales. Notes, most lenders borrowed from interbank call market sparingly, instead meeting cash shortfall from RBI's discount window at fixed 6.00%. Banks borrowed INR894.55 billion from RBI Monday. Tips call rate in 6.50%-6.90% band rest of session. \uc2 Coal India\uc2 's IPO, which aims to raise up to $3.5 billion, opened for subscription Monday. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0835 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher late Monday, tracking EUR retreat against USD. Pair at 43.23 from 43.17 late Friday. Pair likely to stay within 43.15-43.45 range Tuesday with upside risks as market likely to follow EUR movement. "The peso may retreat further as euro might sustain its reversal," says local bank trader. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0833 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia shelves painful fiscal reforms for now as government prepares for an election, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 ; election has to be called by 2013. "The fact that the new budget proudly includes a plan to construct a MYR5 billion 'Warisan Merdeka' tower--which would trump the already unnecessarily tall \uc2 Petronas\uc2  Twin Towers--speaks volumes about just how focused the government is on this front," says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . Adds government target of cutting budget deficit to 2.8% of GDP in 2015 (from 5.6% this year) "would now appear ever more ambitious." Says subsidy cuts so far "largely symbolic" with 2011 subsidy bill at MYR23.7 billion, just over MYR1 billion less than 2010's expected costs; GST implementation postponed without new timeline. "The now-best case is that the government's gamble pays off: that the near-term dangling of carrots (plus postponement of sticks) may just deliver a strong mandate for tough reforms after the elections." (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com) [ 10-18-10 0530ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101018e6ai0005t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
