\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Tad Higher; 8,920-8,930 Range Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,399 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2010\par 03:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR edges up slightly at 8,925 vs 8,920 as local players take profit after Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's supected active \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recently, showing its resolve not to let greenback fall below 8,920. But, so far this morning \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hasn't heard buying pair as yet. Pair tipped in 8,920-8,930 range for session. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD tad lower at 30.750 vs 30.793 Friday, but off early low of 30.670 due to Taiex's 1.0% fall, importers buying, says foreign bank trader. "The pair has rarely gone below 31.000 over the past 10 years," so current price attractive to investors, she says. Adds, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step in to slow pair's rise later; as it was suspected of doing so recently. Tips pair in 30.700-30.800 band for day. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0144 GMT [Dow Jones] HK blue chips' pre-open showing HSI's correction may be 1-day thing, after index ended Friday down 0.4%. Gainers outnumber losers at pre-open; those bid up at least 2% include Swire Pacific (0019.HK), \uc2 Hang Lung Properties\uc2  (0101.HK), Sinopec (0019.HK), Shenhua (1088.HK), China Coal (1898.HK); while \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) lone blue chip quoted more than 1% lower. HKEx (0388.HK) indicated flat at HK$180.00 pre-market, while stock very overbought after 47.7% surge since September, any pullback unlikely significant given robust HK market volume. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI little changed at 1490.40, supported by gains in plantation stocks. Broader market mixed, with advancers, decliners evenly matched. 2011 budget unveiled by PM Najib Friday failing to make any impression among investors. "Broadly speaking, we see little instantaneous impact from Budget 2011 on our domestic stock exchange as the government's master plan to overhaul the Malaysian economy...may only begin to bear fruit in the medium to longer term," HwangDBS Vickers Research says; pegs immediate resistance at 1495, support at 1465. Among gainers, plantation stocks extend gains amid strong palm oil prices, with Batu Kawan (1899.KU) +7.2% at MYR15.80, Genting Plantations (2291.KU) +0.4% at MYR8.40; among decliners, PLUS (5052.KU) down 0.9% at MYR4.42 as some analysts deem UEM, EPF's MYR23 billion takeover offer unattractive. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares likely higher, expected to test 1000 as fund inflows continue. If benchmark index rises above that psychological level, it may even extend gains to 1020 technical resistance, says Globlex Securities' Head of Equity Investment Strategy Chakkrit Charoenmethachai. "As the dollar remains bearish, it's likely that the fund relocation to the Asian markets would continue," he says. Expects energy, financial plays to lead advance, though gains likely limited on profit-taking; investors think \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  may hold its benchmark rate unchanged at 1.75% at Wednesday policy meeting. Support put at 987. SET Index ended +0.4% at 997.15 Friday. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares slightly higher; STI +0.3% at 3215.13, off 3197 early low, with gainers beating losers 1.7 to 1. Muted gains reflect caution ahead; "(STI) upside is probably limited after the good run we've seen and the market is starting to look a bit tired. The main question is 'did we do enough to warrant the spike above 3200?' I think that question will be answered during the earnings season," says analyst at local brokerage. GLP (MC0.SG) opens at S$2.16 on debut, +10% vs IPO price of S$1.96, gains largely expected after offer 12X subscribed; stock tops volume list. SGX (S68.SG) down 0.8% at S$10.04 after 1QFY11 net profit falls 21% on-year, hurt by lower securities revenue; down 17.1% on-year. China New Town Development (DN4.SG) +5.8%, second most traded stock, on news dual listing in Hong Kong by way of introduction to commence trading October 22. STI resistance tipped at May 2008 peak of 3,267, support at 3,142 last week's low. 271.5 million shares traded so far. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD higher at 7.7594 vs 7.7585 Friday tracking higher USD/CNY fixing, says trader at UK bank. Tips pair in 7.7570-7.7620 band near term. "The performance of the local equities market remains the key driver. If Hong Kong stocks extend their recent gains, this could lure more inflows and be supportive to the Hong Kong dollar." HSI tipped to consolidate today, with any profit taking expected to be modest amid ample liquidity. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped lower, with DJIA decline, lack fresh market leads likely to undermine stocks; benchmark PSEi may slip to 4200 initial support after ending down 0.4% to 4216.35 Friday, with next stop at 4180, resistance 4250; "Technically we're still overbought, so the market will have to consolidate further. Some earnings reports may start to trickle in and that could provide market support," says Astro del Castillo, managing director of First Grade Holdings; utilities such as \uc2 Manila Electric\uc2 . (MER.PH), PLDT (TEL.PH) and \uc2 Globe Telecom\uc2  (GLO.PH) may be in investors' focus. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] Following bear steepening in U.S. Treasury yield curve last week, market may remain prone to uncertainty this week, as expectations for further Fed easing continue to clash with any further positive surprises in economic data. For now "we forecast continued room for uncertainty," with market easily swayed by moves in long end, says Mizuho Securities chief market economist Yasunari Ueno. Benchmark 10-year Treasury yield up 6.1 bps at 2.567% late Friday in New York. Rise came after stronger-than-expected retail sales, manufacturing outlook for New York region dented expectations for bold Fed move, despite earlier speech by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Bernanke Friday emphasizing readiness for more monetary easing. FOMC meets Nov. 2-3. Ueno says Treasury market "may not see a calm spot until after the early November FOMC meeting passes."(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0120 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may consolidate, as fears of a sharp correction (HSI up 7 consecutive weeks for 15.3% gain) battle strong momentum-induced voracity to buy. Still, any profit-taking "at the present stage would only be modest," given ample liquidity, says Core Pacific-Yamaichi's Belle Liang. Valuation another useful tool to gauge where market is - according to Daiwa, MSCI China Index trading at 12-month forward P/E of "only 12.2X"; believes investors likely to increase allocations further to China over next few weeks. HKEx (0388.HK) may need to take breather after 47.7% surge over past 7 weeks. HSI down 0.4% to 23,757.63 Friday, but up 3.5% for week. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to consolidate after recent sharp declines, report from U.S. Treasury on whether China manipulates its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been delayed. Pair tipped in 6.6400-6.6500 range. "Though the PBOC set a string of record-low dollar/yuan central parities last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is controlling the pace of yuan's rise well," says Shanghai-based trader at foreign bank. USD/CNY central parity may be set above 6.6500 vs Friday's 6.6497. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may extend gains after ending at another 5-month high Friday, but benchmark Shanghai Composite Index will need to consolidate after testing near-term resistance at 3000. "The stock index may have momentum to rise further as investors's passion for heavyweight blue chips hasn't cooled," says Wei Daoke at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. But notes index also faces pressure from rebound in Dollar Index, fall in gold prices. Shanghai Composite Index ends +3.2% at 2971.16. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com) [ 10-17-10 2150ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101018e6ai00013\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
