\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Lower But Well Supported At 8,920\b0\par\par\uc2 1,418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2010\par 10:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0819 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR lower but well supported at 8,920 vs 8,930 late Friday on suspected Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dealers say. 3 dealers estimate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have bought around $50 million at 8,920 to stem IDR's rise. Volume thin due to holidays in Japan, U.S. 8,915-8,935 range tipped for Tuesday; traders reckon Bank Indonesia may continue supporting greenback near 8,920. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0819 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia government bonds mostly higher as local bond yields remain attractive (vs global bond yields), continue to draw foreign interest, dealers says. "We have been wrong many times for predicting that bond prices would correct lower as foreigners continue to accumulate government securities," dealer says; 10-year yield down at 7.09% vs 7.7.22% late Friday; 15-year yield down at 7.51% vs 7.67%; 20-year yield down at 7.86% vs 8.12%; and 30-year down at 8.24% vs 8.44%. Analysts say foreigners may continue buying Indonesian IDR bonds as growth in advanced economies still look anemic. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0818 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF may struggle to climb above the 1.35 level as there is strong selling interest at 1.3475, according to BayernLB. "With the US holiday Monday, and a lack of franc-related data this week, we are expecting the pair to move in a tight range between 1.33 to 1.35," says trader Roland Raczek. EUR/CHF at 1.3414 from a high of 1.348. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0818 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR's reversed an early rally. Profit-taking appeared as the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  once again rose close to $1.40. Late last week, euro group President Juncker expressed concerns over the EUR's recent rise. Nevertheless, downward pressure on the USD is expected to continue as last Friday's weak US payrolls will keep the market speculating about more QE from the Fed. Also, the failure of ministers at last weekend's IMF meeting to come up with any greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  coordination means that current pressures in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will continue. The EUR is virtually flat at $1.3930 after rising as far as $1.4019. The USD is also hardly changed at Y82.10, after falling as low as Y81.37. There was still no sign of new Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the JPY's rise. However, both Japan and the US are on holiday Monday. The GBP is a little lower at $1.5949. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0814 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungarian forint firms, and looks to find new, relatively stability around 275 against the EUR, following the steady firming it posted over the past two weeks, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . EUR/HUF is at 273.90 after 276 Sunday evening. HUF could firm further this week if the government discloses the corner stone target figures of next year's budget by Friday, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. If the corner stone figures turn out to be convincing for markets, and if global sentiment remains supportive, the EUR/HUF could even come close to 270 again, it adds. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0813 GMT [Dow Jones] Last week's rally in risk assets supported the European asset-backed securities market, with spreads ending the week unchanged or moderately tighter and flows focused on vanilla asset classes like prime UK RMBS, says RBS. "There does not appear to be any meaningful selling into the recent rally as yet, unlike the greater offer-side interest coincident with other periods of asset-backed pricing strength that we've seen since last year," the bank says. (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0809 GMT [Dow Jones] French August industrial production demonstrates the recovery is gradual and confirms that economic activity in France will be less buoyant than in 2Q, when GDP rose 0.7% on month, says Nicolas Bouzou, economist at financial consultancy Asteres. Industrial output was unchanged in August from July, official data shows Monday. "The pause in August could prefigure a difficult end of the year for industry," says Bouzou. He notes the recent rise of the euro against the dollar will harm French price competitiveness outside of the euro zone, at just the moment when France needs stronger exports to emerging markets. (william.horobin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0808 GMT [Dow Jones] Although 1.60 is proving a psychological hurdle for GBP/USD there have been no meaningful bearish signals, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 , therefore the bank looks for a slow advance with an eventual daily close above 1.60 leading to a test of the 1.6440 area later in the 4Q. Now at 1.5940; BarCap expects any pullbacks to find support in the 1.5730-1.5670 area. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0805 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR lower late at 3.1030 vs 3.1130 Friday; local dealer says MYR rebounding on broad USD weakness vs Asians. Still, pair trading in tight 3.0980-3.1030 range for most of day. "Selling interest (on the dollar) has moderated, but there are also currently no catalysts for further upside on the dollar," says dealer. Tips 3.0900-3.1100 range Tuesday. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0804 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex last +0.3% at 20,299.86, off 20,461.27 (+1.0%) early high, as investors cautious, reluctant to buy at these levels; Sensex +13% since Sept. 1. 17 index issues up, 13 down, with \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) +3.0% on hopes of strong global sales due later this week while Bharti (532454.BY) off 1.6% at INR345.20 on profit-booking. "The markets opened gap up purely on the back of strength in regional markets. But, have failed to hold onto its gains yet again due to recent outperformance and expensive valuations;" Sensex trading at over 19X this FY earnings, keeping investors on sidelines. Says, every high level is being used by short-term traders to pare positions. Expect markets to trade rangebound, but with downward bias near-term. Tips Sensex in 20,000-20,500 range, with 2Q earnings, starting with \uc2 Infosys (500209.BY) \uc2 due Friday - providing future direction. \uc2 Sterlite Industries\uc2  (500900.BY) +1.9% at INR177.10, \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) +1.3% at INR1,062.30. TCS (532540.BY) down 1.0% at INR932, \uc2 Infosys\uc2  down 0.5% at INR3,061.35. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0803 GMT [Dow Jones] The market is only a few months old and still very shallow, but offshore yuan market can now do things Beijing still doesn't allow onshore. Friday, \uc2 HSBC Holdings\uc2  said it had traded yuan options, something onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have been waiting for clearance to do for years. Nondeliverable yuan options with contract settled in dollars have been available for years. But with HKMA, PBOC agreeing in July to allow banks in Hong Kong to trade yuan for reasons other than trade settlement, options can now be settled in yuan. Economists have long said major barrier to Beijing's plans to encourage overseas firms to settle China trade with yuan is lack of tools for hedging \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. Now seems overseas firms will soon be able to trade in a raft of yuan-denominated financial products, largely free of Beijing's oversight. That won't be enough to convince most exporters, importers to give up USD as settlement \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it's a good start. See: =WSJ BLOG/China Real Time: New Option on the Yuan (dinny.mcmahon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0800 GMT [Dow Jones] Sell EUR/HUF at 275, with a 265 target and 281 stop-loss, says \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . Expects EUR/HUF to end 2010 at 270 and to fall to 260 by end-2011. EUR/HUF is at 274. Says sentiment toward Hungarian assets has improved on government confirming commitment to below-3% of GDP 2011 budget goal, but positioning remains light. However, there are still concerns such as 2011 budget details and how much the bank sector's extra tax will curb credit growth and slow economic recovery, also how the government's disagreement with the European Commission over the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor's pay cap will be resolved. (margit.feher@dowjones.com) [ 10-11-10 0419ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101011e6ab00056\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
