\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesia Shares Likely Higher; 3620 Cap Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,461 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2010\par 04:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely slightly higher, helped by steady IDR, expected further healthy inflows, traders say; tip index in 3590-3620 range after shares yesterday end up 0.3% at record high 3603.404. "I think the market will continue to consolidate, with the main index likely moving in a limited range to reflect caution after recent sharp gains," says trader at First Asia Capital. Tin miner Timah (TINS.JK), nickel miner Inco (INCO.JK) may lead gains on hopes for strong 9-month earnings, but car maker Astra (ASII.JK) may fall on profit-taking. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares likely to extend gains on persistent capital inflows. Big-cap energy plays may be buoyant, while banking stocks also eyed to rise on hopes for strong 3Q earnings. "I don't see any sign that capital inflows will reverse soon as the U.S. dollar, which is an indicator of fund flows, continues to weaken against the baht," says Globlex Securities head of strategist Chakkrit Charoenmethachai. Adds SET index likely to test 1,000 mark again this week but won't be able to hold above that level as profit-taking likely to emerge. USD/THB slides to 29.86 earlier vs 29.93 late yesterday when pair slipped through 30.00 mark for first time since July 1997. Resistance tipped at 986, support at 971. SET index yesterday ends at day's high of 979.00, up 1.0%. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0150 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 30.789 vs 31.132 late yesterday, touches fresh 2-year low of 30.752 earlier, weighed by continued foreign fund inflows as global USD remains weak, says foreign bank trader. "The new Taiwan dollar is likely to test a new high" in near term if global USD stays weak, he says. Adds, downside limited as some importers buying at lows. Tips pair in 30.700-30.900 band for day. Another trader says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may buy USD late to slow pair's drop, but doesn't seem to be defending a particular level, judging from pair's recent sharp decline. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW rebounds on bargain hunting, short-covering after slipping to its lowest in more than 5 months at 1,113.5 near market open, while Kospi's moderate 0.3% fall also weighing on KRW, traders say. Pair now tad higher at 1,120.2 vs 1,118.0 late yesterday in Seoul. "The pair made a swift rebound from its earlier low to above 1,120," with help from buying by some offshore players, says local bank trader. Adds, pair's quick rebound shows quite a number of investors willing to grab USD on dips near low-1,110s region. Tips pair in 1,115-1,125 range for rest of session. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] October HSI futures +0.1% at 22,953, as cash market expected to consolidate. Still, contract ended yesterday at 40-point premium to cash, after ending Tuesday at big 61-point premium, continuing to signal unmistakable positive bias in market's near-term direction. October open interest little changed at 111,339 lots. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot silver at $23.19/oz, flat from NY close after spiking to 30-year high of $23.26 in early Asia trade. Since hitting intraday low of $17.75 on August 24 silver has enjoyed remarkable 31% run with no real correction in that timeframe. White metal has been fuelled by same drivers as gold with many traders selling the gold/silver ratio in belief silver was relatively undervalued vs gold, says Mark Pervan, commodity strategist at \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  in Melbourne. Gold has added 11% since August 24 when ratio was 68; current ratio is 58. Pervan says some signs that people will start looking for exit points soon to take profits, but strength of INR a supportive factor with Indian silver buyers cushioned from rally by that. "Everything is heavily skewed towards what's happening in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at the moment," he says. Any rally in USD likely to be obvious trigger for some silver liquidation, he says. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0144 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR steady at 8,920 as Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of continuing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow pair's slide. Three dealers estimate \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to have bought around $30 million at 8,920 as pair remains under selling pressure. 8,915-8,935 tipped for today. Analysts expect Bank Indonesia could eventually allow pair to drift lower if selling interest persists as it doesn't want to flood market with rupiah. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI little changed at 1478.98, trapped within narrow 2-point range so far; broad market mixed, with decliners, advancers about even. Dealers say market lacking catalysts, following mixed performance on Wall Street overnight, absence of fresh developments domestically. "Given the somewhat cautious sentiment, the KLCI may continue treading a tight range, but with a slight negative bias due to profit-taking after yesterday's rise," dealer says. KLCI had reached 33-month high yesterday; key index tipped to remain in 1475-1485 range rest of day. Among gainers, Dialog Group (7277.KU) +2.7% at MYR1.16 after clinching MYR61 million contract, Mudajaya (5085.KU) +2.0% at MYR4.53; among decliners, Supermax (7106.KU) down 1.2% at MYR4.21 on concerns over slower earnings growth for glove makers. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT