pped through 30.00 mark for first time since July 1997. USD under selling pressure vs majors, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as weak U.S. private-jobs data further stokes expectation of new round of quantitative easing measures by Fed to stimulate U.S. economy. Sustained capital inflows, export settlements also likely to continue supporting local unit. Still, pair off 29.85 intraday low as BOT was suspected of buying no more than $100 million early, dealers add. "We must keep watch on how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will handle the situation as the baht is growing stronger too fast," one dealer says. Support tipped at 29.80, resistance at 29.90. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0134 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks lower in early trade as investors opt to preserve gains amid lack of firm leads from Wall Street overnight. STI off 0.3% at 3179.18, expected to hold above 3150. "A pullback at this stage is not a bad thing, bearing in mind there could be room for more upside as we head into the earnings season," says dealer at local brokerage. Small caps most active in market, led by \uc2 Genting Hong Kong\uc2  (S21.SG), off 2.2% at US$0.445, Sino Grandness Food (JS5.SG), +3.6% at S$0.43, \uc2 Kreuz Holdings\uc2  (5RK.SG), +6.7% at S$0.48, Hoe Leong (H20.SG), +5.4% at S$0.39. Among blue chips, key decliners include yesterday's top performers like \uc2 Sembcorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG), off 1.2% at S$4.28. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0128 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD higher as major UK bank, also U.S. bank buying on bargain hunting, says senior trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair at 7.7550-7.7580 band near term. "I spotted the UK bank and the U.S. bank buying the U.S. dollar at HK$7.7550, with the total amount about US$200 million. Still, I don't expect a big rise in the pair due to the broad weakening trend of the U.S. dollar." Pair last at 7.7561 vs 7.7552 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0128 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped higher, likely carried by positive outlook on economy, corporate earnings; benchmark PSEi may top 4200 after ending +0.6% at 4196.73 Wednesday, a new record closing high, with 4120 as immediate resistance, support at 4150. Property developers such as Megaworld (MEG.PH) and Filinvest Land (FLI.PH) and power generation firms as First Gen (FGEN.PH) and Energy Development (EDC.PH) likely to lead advance; "Liquidity and positive economic and corporate outlook will driven market momentum. But we're prone to profit-taking," says Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0127 GMT [Dow Jones] 10-year JGB auction likely unexciting as coupon expected to be set at less attractive level, but demand for 10-year JGBs could remain solid in secondary market, says Katsutoshi Inadome, fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Notes, many investors still eager to buy long-term JGBs on back of BOJ's aggressive monetary easing, so even if 10-year JGB prices fall after auction, "that could provide a window of opportunity for investors to buy (10-year JGBs) on dips." Tokyo traders also say 10-year JGBs could gain favor as mid-term bond yields have already fallen to very low levels. 10-year yield now at 0.825%, down 1 bp. Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  slated to sell Y2.2 trillion worth of 10-year bonds with expected coupon of 0.8% or 0.9%. Auction results due out 0345 GMT. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] After adding 49,500 jobs last month, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  doesn't expect annual pace of Aussie jobs growth to lift much, though bank's strategists don't expect it to fall off either. "We do expect the unemployment rate to move below 5% - most likely before years end," says \uc2 Westpac\uc2 . "The current average monthly gain of 32,000 is comfortably above labor force growth." Aussie unemployment rate currently at 5.1%. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI likely to consolidate, as profit taking sets in after HK market up in 20 out of last 24 trading sessions, for cumulative gains of 11.4%. UOB KayHian's Steven Leung says blue chips overbought, with HSI's 14-day RSI at 77.76, "a level for consolidation." Still, expects more market upside after consolidation, house's year-end target kept at 26,500 for HSI, 15,500 for H-share index. Any pullback in resources stocks such as Cnooc (0883.HK), \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK), Shenhua (1088.HK) may be entry opportunities given strong sector momentum. HSI +1.1% at 22,880.41 yesterday, a new 2010 closing high; volume finally breaches HK$100 billion mark at HK$104.8 billion. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0111 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD sharply higher as Aussie economy adds 49,500 jobs in September vs expected 20,000, with new post-float high likely soon, says Sean Callow, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Westpac\uc2 . After trading around 0.9765 just before jobs report, AUD/USD last at 0.9840, its highest level since reaching a post-float high of 0.9850 on July 15, 2008. "In this bounce, we took out 0.9790 and other interim resistance. Beyond this, you're mostly looking at round figures like 0.99 and parity. The only other level watch for resistance is 0.9870," says Callow. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped lower as other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  advance vs USD on expectation of further easing measures by the Fed; pair may slide to immediate support of 43.30 after settling at 43.505 late yesterday, a new 28-month low; resistance seen at 43.75. Local bank trader says "only the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands in the way of further peso gains." (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely higher, tracking overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys amid growing expectations Fed, other central banks will loosen monetary policy further. "The rally in U.S. and some European bonds is likely to push Singapore government bonds higher. The market is currently driven entirely by external factors," says trader at local bank, tips yields to fall 2-3 bps. Yesterday, 10-year government bond yield fell 3 bps to 1.96%, 2-year unchanged at 0.41%. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com) [ 10-06-10 2144ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101007e6a700016\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
