\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Steady; 8,910-8,930 Range Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 03:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR steady at 8,920 despite broad USD weakness vs regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as players see chances of Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increasing after IDR's recent sharp gains, dealers say; "But, the tone is still bearish on the dollar because foreigners may still need to buy the rupiah to settle their purchases of Indonesian stocks and bonds done Friday," dealer with foreign bank says. 8,910-8,930 range tipped. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0113 GMT [Dow Jones] Stock selection "makes all the difference" in September, will again be important in October, says local fund manager; notes HSI up 9% in September, which impressive, but even more impressive are \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK), HKEx (0388.HK), \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) racking up 26%, 25%, 23% rises last month, vs China Life (2628.HK), Esprit (0330.HK) at other extreme, up 3% and down 3% respectively. Thinks gains will moderate in October, strategy is to buy "strong stocks" on pullback rather than betting on laggards to catch up. Tips HSI to open modestly higher today on Wall Street cues, although profit-taking likely to set in early part of week, if not today. HSI ends Thursday down 0.1% at 22,358.17. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] 5-year JGB yield briefly falls to 0.240%, lowest level since June 2003, amid growing expectations that BOJ will loosen monetary policy early this week, says trader at Tokyo securities house; adds if speculation persists that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will do more to curb trend toward strong JPY, 5-year yield could extend declines, possibly to 0.200%. Adds demand for longer-term JGBs also rising amid persisting concerns that Japan's economy will likely remain sluggish for long time, making yield curve flatter. 5-year yield now at 0.245%, down 0.5 bps; 10-year down 1.5 bps at 0.940%, 20-year at 1.670%, down 2.5 bps. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0109 GMT [Dow Jones] Profit-taking clips EUR's wings after ascent earlier in morning to multi-month highs vs USD, JPY, but EUR may avoid any serious tumbles this week as investors take comments from Chinese PM Wen over weekend as show of support for common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Yuzo Sakai, manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  business promotion at Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  & Ueda Harlow. In Greece on Saturday ahead of 2-day Asia-Europe Meeting summit starting today, Wen says China will "make a big effort" to stand behind euro zone, continue to support a stable EUR, and will keep buying Greek bonds. "With all the attention that's on Chinese policy, remarks like this can't help but give the euro some support." Adds EUR also likely to get support as recent rises in risk-sensitive commodities prod investors to turn to higher-yielding units such as common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . EUR/USD last 1.3752, after earlier hitting 1.3809, highest since March 17, while EUR/JPY up to 114.98, highest since May 18. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after hitting 2-month high $81.87/bbl this morning, provided support at Friday's $79.70 low doesn't break, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart positive-biased as 5-day & 15-day moving averages still rising; meanwhile, MACD & stochastics bullish, but latter at overbought. Resistance at psychological $82.00, then at then $82.97 (Aug. 4 top); breach would expose upside to psychological round-numbered levels from $84 up to $87.00, then $87.15 (May 3 peak). Fall below $79.70 support would temper near-term positive outlook, opening way down $78.04 (previous cap set Sept. 13), then $77.55 (Thursday's low, near 200-day moving average), $76.69 (55-day moving average) and $75.52 (Sept. 27 low, matching 100-day moving average). November crude last down 4 cents at $81.54/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB lower at 30.16-30.18 vs 30.20 late Friday in thin trade amid continued bearish view on greenback. Pair biased down with fund inflows likely to continue. Market expects BOT to step in to curb sharp THB rise. "(The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) may not be able to reverse the trend but the market is still hoping it will stem the pace of baht appreciation," says local dealer. Adds panic selling by exporters may ensue if pair slips too quickly early in week, sliding through key 30.00 support. Initial support pegged at 30.10; resistance at 30.22. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP likely lower, tracking USD weakness vs regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early Asian trade; pair may slide to 43.75 after settling late Friday at 43.88; support tipped at 43.60, resistance at 44.00. Manila-based trader says if \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrains from buying, USD/PHP will likely hit new year-to-date low today. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to rise a tad in 8200-8300 range after ending +0.1% at 8244.18 on Friday, boosted by foreign fund inflows on hopes local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue rising, says KGI Securities trader Randy Chang. USD/TWD at 31.310 late Friday, off 2-year intraday low. Still, adds volume likely moderate today on lack of firm cues, with China markets shut for National Day holidays. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0055 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi +0.5% at 1886.33 on Wall Street's gains Friday, with foreign investors extending buying-streak to 14 sessions. "Investor sentiment remains positive as foreign funds are flowing into emerging markets amid a weak U.S. dollar," says HI Investment & Securities' Kim Seung-han. Hopes of more Fed action to stimulate U.S. economy, strong China data, expectations of strong 3Q (local companies') earnings will continue to support market sentiment, analysts say; think Kospi may touch 1900 sometime this week. Recent KRW strength may hurt some exporters, but energy, airline stocks may get boost, analysts say. "Tech shares are under pressure due to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk (strong KRW) but valuation-wise, it's a good timing to snatch those up," Kim adds. Banks, securities also up on brighter 3Q earnings outlook, recently active stock trades, respectively, analysts say. KB Financial (105560.SE) +1.7% at KRW50,800, \uc2 Hana Financial\uc2  (086790.SE) +2.1% at KRW36,000. SK Energy (096770.SE) +2.8% at KRW148,000, \uc2 Korean Air Lines\uc2  (003490.SE) +1.0% at KRW78,800, \uc2 Asiana Airlines\uc2  (020560.SE) +1.4% at KRW9,630. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0051 GMT [Dow Jones] ACCC Chairman Graeme Samuel says competition sensitivity is high in Australian finance sector due to the global financial crisis. "That is an area of constant focus for us in terms of examining potential mergers on a very vigorous basis, and is made even more difficult by not knowing exactly what we are going to see over the next year or two years," he tells the Wall Street Journal. Uncertainties include whether non-bank financial institutions re-enter market with vigor of 2007, and whether foreign banks return despite more stringent capital requirements. ACCC recently blocked \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 's (NAB.AU) bid for AXA Asia Pacific (AXA.AU), after copping criticism for allowing takeover of BankWest by Commonwealth Bank (CBA.AU), St. George Bank (SGB.AU) by \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) during crisis. But Samuel says that criticism misses the point; increased dominance of mortgage market by big four banks is due to drying up of securitization market that funded smaller, non-bank competitors. "I don't think there is anything that a competition regulator can do to change that or that any government can do. We have got to let this shock of the GFC get out of the system," he says. See "Regulator Weighs Australia's Future" (rebecca.thurlow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones]EUR/USD, EUR/JPY down as short-term investors in Asia take profits, says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major brokerage in Japan. EUR/USD earlier hit 1.3809, highest level since March 17, while EUR/JPY rose to 114.98, highest since May 18. "Those rises put the euro at an attractive level for profit-taking, which should put the brakes on further gains for the rest of today." Says EUR/USD could fall to 1.3710 vs last at 1.3755, EUR/JPY to 114.20 vs last 114.66. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.2% at 9385.02, after opening in positive territory, as investors stay sidelined ahead of BOJ policy board meeting (Monday-Tuesday). Investors widely anticipate additional monetary easing measures to be announced; today's Nikkei range seen at 9350-9500, analysts say. "It's a chance for the BOJ to take steps before the U.S.," says Cosmo Securities equity strategist Toshikazu Horiuchi, referring to upcoming November FOMC meeting. "But market players are not quite sure about what, specifically, the BOJ can do when it comes to 'stepped-up' measures." 25/33 Topix subindexes lower; banks off 0.9%, consumer finance firms off 0.9%. Among heavyweight shares, \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) off 1.1%. Inpex (1605.TO) up 2.2% at Y412,000 on crude gains. \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  (8801.TO) up 2.1% at Y1,490 on continued BOJ easing hopes after sharp Friday gains. \uc2 Shinsei Bank\uc2  (8303.TO) up 3.4% at Y60 on \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  rating hike. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 10-03-10 2126ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101004e6a40000x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
