\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: KLCI +0.1%; 1460-1466 Range Tipped Until Close\b0\par\par\uc2 1,666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 08:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0651 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.1% at 1461.42; trading activity remains subdued, dealers say absence of fresh fundamental leads continues to limit KLCI to narrow range. OSK Research analyst Shin Kao Jack says near-term direction unclear; breach of recent high of 1479 needed to confirm resumption of market's bullish trend. Dealers tip morning session range of 1460-1466 to persist until close. "With no major leads expected until the 2011 budget, the market can be expected to move largely in line with overseas markets for the next 1 to 2 weeks," dealer says. Among gainers, nursing college operator Masterskill (5166.KU) +7.3% at MYR3.25 after getting government approval to open new campus in east Malaysia, Sime (4197.KU) +2.0% at MYR8.56; among decliners, AirAsia (5099.KU) down 2.8% at MYR2.08. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0651 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro zone is still bearing the weight of event risks Wednesday, with the cost of the Anglo Irish bailout and a \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgrade of Spanish debt both expected this week. "Any combination of worse than one notch downgrade of Spain with stable outlook should put pressure on SPGBs," says David Schnautz, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  strategist. However, the recent Irish and Portuguese weakness has led Italian BTPs to cheapen ahead of Wednesday's auction of BTP 2% June 2013, BTP 3.75% March 2021 and CCTeu December 2015 providing a good levels to implement the long BTP versus SPGBs \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  strategy. The 10-year Italian/Spanish yield spread is at 24 bps while the 10-year Irish/German spread is at 457.5 bps, Portuguese/German at 428 bps, Greek/German at 854 bps, Spanish/German at 194 bps and Italian/German 170 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0650 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite rupiah bond yields already at historical lows, foreigners likely to continue buying Indonesian local bonds in anticipation of ratings upgrade expected within 12-24 months. "Our economic growth, generally sound policies and high-yielding assets, especially with the prospect of a credit upgrade, certainly attract investors," says Ramdan Ario, head of fixed income at local brokerage firm Anugerah Securindo Indah; analysts predict local bonds may correct in 4Q on expectations of higher inflation next year, but say sharp reversal unlikely. See "=FOCUS: Foreigners To Continue Buying Indonesian Govt Bonds" (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares generally still holding up, with lower liners, S-chips among better performers. FTSE ST Small Cap Index +1.2%, FTSE ST Mid Cap Index +1.1%, FTSE ST China Top Index +1.4%. Gains by blue chips less robust, with STI +0.3% at 3107.22, suggesting recent 27-month high of 3125 unlikely to be challenged. "I still think the market is due for a correction," says dealer at local brokerage. Notes penny stocks have run up substantially in short time, while S-chips active as investors banking on dual-listing theme to push prices higher; "we all know interest in such thematic plays don't last long." China-based Sound Global (E6E.SG) +11.1% at S$0.90 ahead of its debut in Hong Kong tomorrow by way of introduction; Midas Holdings (5EN.SG) +5.2% at S$1.01 as train parts supplier in final stages of selling new shares in HK. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are little changed Wednesday, with stocks also expected to open largely flat. Trading has been choppy in the last few sessions, with markets continuing to fret over the sovereign debt troubles in peripheral euro zone countries, notably Ireland and Portugal. The Irish government is set to detail the additional cost of injecting capital in \uc2 Anglo Irish Bank\uc2  only on Thursday. "Given this, bunds are likely to trade well supported as the uncertainty continues," says WestLB. In the primary market, Italy sells bonds for up to EUR8Bln while the ECB conducts its 3-month liquidity facility with EUR225Bln maturing, and the take-up will be eyed for clues on the amount of surplus cash in the financial system. Euro zone retail PMI is due 0800 GMT while EU business and consumer confidence indicators are out at 0900 GMT. December bunds are unchanged at 131.78 and the 10-year bund is up at 100.10 to yield 2.238%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0632 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 0.2% at HK$2.392 billion at interval, 5.2% of market total vs 5.7% Tuesday's half-day, as bears slightly deterred with HSI's 1.3% rebound. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) leads with HK$415.74 million, then \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) with HK$285.87 million, \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  (0762.HK) with HK$114.86 million, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$108.83 million and Henderson Land (0012.HK) with HK$92.34 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0630 GMT [Dow Jones] The two important indicators of Swiss consumer confidence, the KOF barometer and retail sales, due Wednesday and Friday, are likely to print solid readings for another month, according to \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "In particular where the KOF business climate in the retail sector is concerned, we are looking for yet another improved headline figure," says economist Reto Huenerwadel. "The close to perfect correlation between the Swiss employment situation and consumer confidence, suggests we don't have to prepare for faltering private consumption in Switzerland," he adds. The September KOF data are due at 0930 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0624 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly higher; Nikkei +0.7% after tankan survey tops view; HSI +1.3%, STI +0.4%, Kospi +0.6%, Taiex +0.6%, SET +1.1%, Shanghai Composite +0.2%; but S&P/ASX down 0.5%, NZX-50 down 0.1%, Sensex down 0.3%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.3589 vs 1.3590 late Tuesday in NY, EUR/JPY at 113.66 vs 114.00, USD/JPY at 83.63 vs 83.89. Traders say central banks in Korea, Singapore, Thailand, \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to contain their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' gains. USD/CNY at 6.6862 after touching 6.6844, lowest intraday level under current system started in 1994 after central parity rate set at record low. BOJ's September tankan survey showed sentiment among Japan's big manufacturers picked up for 6th-straight quarter at faster-than-expected pace, but companies outlook turning more cautious; headline diffusion index at 8 in September, highest since March 2008 vs 1 in June survey, forecast for 6. But companies expect index to fall to minus 1 in December survey. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  September China PMI rose to 5-month high of 52.9 vs August's 51.9, likely reassuring markets China isn't facing rapid slowdown. Korea August current-account surplus at $2.07 billion vs July's $5.82 billion, year-earlier's $1.92 billion, lowest in 4 months, but full-year surplus expected to easily surpass BOK's $21 billion estimate. NZ August goods trade balance at NZ$437 million deficit after weaker-than-expected exports. Spot gold at $1,309.80/oz, up 80 cents vs NY close after touching 30-year high of $1,311.90. November Nymex crude oil futures up 22 cents at $76.40/bbl. (leslie.shaffer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0620 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +0.6% at 8240.89, hitting 8-month high of 8286.93 earlier on gains in U.S. shares, says Grand Cathay Securities analyst Mars Hsu. Expects Taiex to sustain gains short term, but notes "the upside for the Taiex is limited as both Taiwan and U.S. shares are close to their year-to-date high levels." Tips Taiex in 8200-8300 band Thursday. \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) +5.1% at NT$32.80, Chimei (3481.TW) +3.3% at NT$42.15. China Airlines (2610.TW) +4.1% at NT$23.00. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0619 GMT [Dow Jones] The Swiss Purchasing Managers' Index for September is expected to slip to 60.3 from 61.4 in August, according to an average of four economist estimates compiled by DJN. The PMI is likely to slip for a second straight month after reaching a record level of 66.9 in July, confirming expectations for an economic cooling heading into the year-end. The data are due October 1 at 0730 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] December bunds are unchanged at 131.79, although WestLB reckons that near-term technical outlook looks constructive, after as the daily MACD is established above the signal line. Initial resistance is at 131.99 ahead of strong resistance at 132.14 and 132.29. Support is at 131.45, while a break below 131.22 would subdue the latest upward momentum, WestLB says. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0616 GMT [Dow Jones] Nomura expects Malaysia \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep policy rates on hold until 1Q11 as it waits for more clarity on global outlook and given Bank Negara's "moderate inflation" projection through 2011. "In our view, (a) policy pause reflects rising global uncertainty and already tighter monetary conditions," house says. Robert Subbaraman, chief economist, Asia ex-Japan, says, "There is a risk, with interest rates so low in the west, that Asia attracts too much inflows." Says this will be a "big challenge" for central banks in Asia, including Bank Negara. Bank Negara kept rates steady at 2.75% at its Sep. 2 meeting; bank to meet once before end of this year on Nov. 12. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com) [ 09-29-10 0251ET ]\par \par \