\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:USD/IDR Tad Lower; Ctrl Bk Stood At 8,950 -Dealers\b0\par\par\uc2 1,475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2010\par 03:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR still biased lower, now at 8,950 vs 8,952 late yesterday, though suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  limiting pair's slide, dealers say; \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  likely bought around $20 million so at IDR8,950 far after an estimated $200 million-$300 million total \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yesterday. "Trading volume is lower this morning compared with yesterday as market players see little room for speculation with Bank Indonesia supporting the dollar at the current level," dealer says; 8,945-8,965 range tipped for today. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0113 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB tad higher in tepid trade at 30.59 vs 30.58 late yesterday, tracking marginally stronger USD/regionals. "The rise in the U.S. dollar will be only short-lived as a flood of funds still flow into Asia including Thailand due to the rosy economic outlook," says Bangkok-based dealer. Notes, MOF Monday revised up Thailand 2010 GDP growth forecast to 7.5% from 5.5%; while SET index hit fresh 14-year high. Resistance tipped at 30.65, support at 30.50-30.52 band. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0109 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. Treasurys buoyed by lingering concerns over European fiscal health, speculation that Fed will conduct further quantitative easing ahead, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  in research note. Cites WSJ report that Fed considering new tactics for purchase of long-term U.S. Treasury securities to bolster a disappointingly slow recovery. Adds weak equities, strong 2-year Treasury auction overnight also positive for Treasurys. 10-year yield down 8.6 bps at 2.521% late yesterday in NY. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely in 31.40-31.50 range today vs 31.37-31.45 range most of yesterday, largely on rebound of USD offshore, says local trader. Pair yesterday closes at 31.47 after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected buying of around US$50 million in late session. Says investors awaiting fresh trading cues on TWD, interest rate outlook by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, when bank will meet to discuss key interest rate; market widely expects hike of 12.5 bps, but unsure if it will continue to hike in near future, as higher rates may attract more inflows, adds pressure to TWD to appreciate. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely higher, tracking overnight moves by U.S. Treasurys, anticipated correction in local stocks, trader at local bank says. "Treasurys rose overnight and we expect a bit of caution as the recent rally in growth assets such as stocks and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have been a bit overdone. It's probably time for a small correction," trader says. Tips yields down 2-3 bps. Yesterday, yield on 2-year Singapore government bond down 2 bps to 0.40%, 10-year unchanged at 2.02%. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0055 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi down 0.3% at 1854.83 as financials, auto stocks in negative territory after recent gains; "bank shares seem to be affected by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  move to downgrade the debt of \uc2 Anglo Irish Bank\uc2  by three notches that weighed on financial stocks overnight on Wall Street," says Bae Sung-young at \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2 . Tips 1850-1860 range for morning session. \uc2 Shinhan Financial Group\uc2  (055550.SE) down 1.0% at KRW43,400, \uc2 Korea Exchange Bank\uc2  (004940.SE) off 1.4% at KRW14,000, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) down 2.8% at KRW157,000. "The recent news of its recall of Sonata sedans in the U.S. and its plan to acquire \uc2 Hyundai Engineering & Construction\uc2  (000720.SE) may be pushing down shares of \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2 ," Bae says; though adds, \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  financially healthy, no big fall in share price expected. Shipbuilding, steel stocks up; \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  (009540.SE) +0.6% at KRW330,500, Posco (005490.SE) +0.4% at KRW511,000. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0051 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely down on weakness in U.S. shares overnight, says Taiwan International Securities Assistant Vice President Andrew Teng. Tips Taiex support at key 8150 level; notes "Taiex continues to see strong resistance at 8200 as there is a lack of fresh leads." Says strong TWD may hurt electronics exporters such as TSMC (2330.TW), HTC (2498.TW), \uc2 Compal Electronics\uc2  (2324.TW) and Quanta Computer (2382.TW). Taiex ends +0.3% at 8191.54 yesterday. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW rebounds following declines in previous two sessions on offshore buying amid caution over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by authorities, expected demand from public firms. Pair at 1,149.4 vs 1,148.2 yesterday in Seoul; "given the moves of the (Japanese) yen and (Chinese) yuan, the direction (for USD/KRW) is clearly downward, but participants aren't betting recklessly to play short, risking authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says local bank dealer. Adds renewed concerns about euro-zone's financial health also provides support. 1,147-1,152 band tipped for day. (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to consolidate after yuan rose for 10th consecutive session vs dollar late Monday. "The yuan has risen too much in the past few days and it's time to take a breather," says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. Yuan has risen 1.3% since Sept. 8. Central parity may be fixed around 6.7000 vs 6.7098 yesterday. Pair at 6.6923 late Monday vs 6.7079 Tuesday last week; China's financial markets were closed Wednesday to Friday for the Mid-Autumn Festival. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may consolidate with downward bias, tracking losses in U.S. stocks on concerns over health of financial stocks. Shanghai Composite Index tipped to trade around 2600, after ending +1.4% at 2627.97. "The technical rebound following last week's holiday looks complete. Further gains will be difficult to achieve as blue chips lack upward momentum, and volume is thin ahead of the next holiday," says Qian Qimin at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. China to go on National Day holiday break October 1-7. China's 2nd largest port operator Ningbo Port (601018.SH) likely to make lackluster debut due to limited potential of company, slowing growth in port industry, says Li Lei at China Securities Research. Shenzhen index ends +2.3% at 1170.54. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0042 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 opens slightly lower after a negative lead from Wall Street, down 6 points or 0.1% at 4669.4. "Today we are having a very classic pull back," says Stuart Smith, senior client adviser at Bell Potter in Brisbane, noting the late pull back in U.S. markets after four weeks of gains. He notes there are no particularly strong leads either way from Wall Street for Asian markets. Still, Smith sees opportunities in the Australian market, although today's trade is likely to be "steady as she goes". "I am quite happy for clients to buy. In other words, we have value and I think it is a buying day," he says, adding he expects the market to trade higher in future. "I am looking for an upward trend that will take us to 4820," Smith says. Among key stocks, BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) down 0.4%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) down 0.2%, ANZ (ANZ.AU) up 0.2%, Qantas (QAN.AU) up 1.1%. Nufarm (NUF.AU) is down 4.9% at A$3.90 ahead of full year results and after it secured debt covenant waivers yesterday, although clearly concerns remain. (lyndal.mcfarland@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.6% at 9546.42 with high-yielding pharmaceutical shares underperforming after many stocks turned ex-dividend. "Speculation on more BOJ monetary easing measures is supporting the market and fueling hopes that the yen will not strengthen further," says Cosmo Securities equity strategist Toshikazu Horiuchi. Nikkei reported Tuesday BOJ will discuss taking additional easing steps at its October policy board meeting. Horiuchi puts Nikkei range for today at 9450-9550. 30/33 Topix subindexes lower; SMFG (8316.TO) down 2.2% at Y2,563, MUFG (8306.TO) down 1.0% at Y403, following decline in U.S. financial stocks after Moody's Investors Service downgraded \uc2 Anglo Irish Bank\uc2  debt by 3 notches. Eisai (4523.TO) down 3.8% at Y2,988. Acom (8572.TO) up 1.8% at Y1,368 following yesterday's 10% plunge on Takefuji (8564.TO) bankruptcy preparation report; Takefuji ask-only at Y131. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 09-27-10 2121ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100928e69s0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
