\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Slips; BI Suspected Supporting - Dealers\b0\par\par\uc2 1,443 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 03:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR tad lower at 8,950 vs 8,955, with suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slowing pair's slide, dealers say. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  likely to have bought around $50 million at 8,950 via state banks, 3 dealers say. "Besides (suspected) Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar seems to be getting support around the current level, from demand from importers," dealer says; 8,940-8,960 range tipped. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.5% at 1458.35, boosted by gains in U.S. stocks, regional markets; but further upside limited by dearth of fresh leads, dealers say. "The KLCI will probably consolidate in the 1450 to 1465 range for now as players wait for clearer direction." Among gainers, Gamuda (5398.KU) +2.1% at MYR3.92 on optimism it may clinch government approval for major rail project, AMMB (1015.KU) +2.1% at MYR5.78, Genting (3182.KU) +1.1% at MYR9.86. Among decliners, Gopeng (2135.KU) down 3.0% at MYR1.29 after agreeing to sell its stake in cement company Perak-Hanjoong - a major profit contributor - to \uc2 YTL Cement\uc2  (8737.KU). (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea government bonds, bond futures up as KRW's rise to highest level in more than 4 months spurring hopes of more foreign interest in local bonds, participants say; 3-year yield quoted down 5 bps at 3.39%, 10-year yield down 1 bp at 4.24%, December futures +16 ticks at 112.28. Also, "local bonds are still playing catch-up on the sharp gains that U.S. Treasurys posted during the Chuseok holidays (last week)," says bond manager at local securities firm. Eyes on monthly industrial output data, consumer price data due later this week; notes "after the BOK kept the key policy rate on hold earlier this month, some expected a hike to come in October instead. But if inflation readings aren't that high," rate hike expectations likely to ease. Pegs cap for December futures at 112.30. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP moving lower, weighed down by weak sentiment for USD amid readiness of U.S. Fed to undertake quantitative easing; pair last at 43.93 in light volume after settling late Friday at 44.09; support at 43.88, resistance 44.20; "The market is watching out for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It's fairly quiet so far," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks in broad-based advance following Friday's U.S. rally, with 11 gainers for every decliner. STI +0.9% at 3119.67, expected to meet resistance at 3146 (higher end of breakdown gap formed June 2008). Lower liners also holding well, with FTSE ST Small Cap Index +1.2%, FTSE ST Catalist Index +0.9%. "The market still has some momentum. The key question is 'How long more will this last?' Until there's solid evidence pointing to a protracted slowdown, it looks like prices will keep heading north," says dealer at foreign brokerage. Among blue chips, CityDev (C09.SG) +2.8% at S$12.44, \uc2 Jardine Strategic\uc2  (J37.SG) +2.6% at US$27.26, \uc2 Jardine Matheson\uc2  (J36.SG) +2.0% at US$46.30. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0125 GMT [Dow Jones] HK's inflation risk "is tilted to the upside," may surprise market over next 18 months, warns \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 ; expects higher imported food prices from China (1% change in China food CPI estimated to contribute 0.25 ppt to HK's headline CPI inflation), among others, to push up CPI in months to come - raises end-2011 CPI inflation forecast to 5.3% on year vs 4.2% previously. Adds higher inflation expected to turn real interest rate even more negative in future, pushing HK's deposit base into asset markets, in particular property market. Without ability to alter HK's monetary policy, house believes government has to do a lot more administratively to get property market under control, but "there is no guarantee that these measures would be successful." (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says coming weeks warrant attention on U.S. corporate profit warnings; notes firms typically publish profit warnings during several weeks around end and beginning of a quarter, while a series of earnings downgrades often trigger sell-offs in shares. "As such, our eyes are on announcements from U.S. companies over the coming weeks," writes analyst in report, alluding to possible subsequent fallout on Japan shares. Adds, however, weakness on profit warnings might provide good entry opportunity (for equities generally), given stocks tend to bottom during earnings season as negatives price in. Topix up 0.7% at 843.84, up 0.3% for quarter ending September, Nikkei up 1.2% at 9585.28, up 2.2% for July-September.(bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI set to rise after Wall Street rally Friday, with index expected to test April high of 22,388 near-term, if not today, says Mark To at Wing Fung Financial; index closed +0.3% at 22,119.43 Friday. Expects volume to increase in run-up to end of 3Q due to window-dressing activities, continued liquidity inflows. Tips index may rise further in October to test above 23,000. Expects \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) to rise as uncertainty cleared after power struggle for bank's top post ends with current CFO Douglas Flint to succeed Stephen Green as chairman. Tips stock may test HK$82.50 within days after closing Friday down 0.6% at HK$80.60. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped higher, with sentiment likely buoyed by strong overseas cues; pair may initially pierce 4100 barrier after ending 0.3% higher to 4078.87 Friday, next resistance likely at 4125, index's record high. Support pegged at 4050. "The Dow closed strongly Friday and gold prices are at record highs," says Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities. Likely to again lead market rise are mining stocks, particularly \uc2 Philex Mining\uc2  (PX.PH), country's most profitable gold, copper producer. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] JGB yields should keep falling for now as additional JGB issuance for this fiscal year unlikely at least at this point, also BOJ tankan survey Wednesday may suggest Japan's deflationary pressure strengthening again due to rising inventories, says RBS Securities chief bond strategist Akito Fukunaga. Adds, investors should also watch if government increases pressure on BOJ to ease policy further; 10-year yield not traded yet, closed at 0.995% Friday; December futures last down 0.04 at 142.84.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0110 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after hitting 9-session high $76.85/bbl this morning, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode; meanwhile, 5-day moving average staging bullish crossover against 15-day moving average. Resistance at $77.47 (200-day moving average); breach would expose upside to $78.04 (September 13 reaction high), then $78.30 (61.8% Fibonacci correction of $82.97-$70.76 August 4-August 25 decline) and psychological $80.00 (just beneath previous base set Aug. 6). Support at $75.36 (hourly chart), then at $74.66 (Friday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to $73.58 (Thursday's low), then $72.75 (September 17 reaction low), $72.63 (September 7 low) and $71.53 (August 31 reaction low). November crude last up 31 cents at $76.80/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB lower at 30.68 vs 30.70-30.72 late Friday in thin trade, tracking broader USD fall vs Asians. Pair biased downward on continued risk appetite, players seeking higher yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while more fund inflows expected as Thai stock market tipped to rise, says local dealer. "Look at the yen (which is still stronger against the dollar despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )...hopefully, the baht won't slip as quickly as previously"; expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be in market only to curb volatility. Pair tipped 30.60-30.70 band. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com) [ 09-26-10 2145ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100927e69r00018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
