\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Sees Benefit From Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2010\par 15:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments, background)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    I-Made Sentana and Andreas Ismar \par    of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Indonesia will benefit from the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to weaken the yen as it makes the country's yen-denominated debt more manageable, Bank Indonesia Deputy Governor Hartadi Sarwono said Friday.\par \par \uc2 Sarwono said Indonesia has "quite a large amount of yen-denominated debt," in a meeting with reporters that was also attended by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor.\par \par \uc2 Data from the finance ministry show Indonesia had Y2.68 trillion in yen-denominated debt as of April 30--about 16% of the country's total debt. The country had 22% of debt denominated in the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia Governor Darmin Nasution suggested that Indonesia won't follow in the footsteps of its Japanese counterpart, saying Bank Indonesia will only act to smooth out volatility in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to curb the rupiah's gains.\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah has climbed nearly 5% against the dollar so far this year, making the country's exports more expensive for overseas markets. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  played down the impact on the export sector.\par \par \uc2 "At the moment, the rupiah isn't the only \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in the region) which is strengthening against the dollar. (Thailand's) baht and (Malaysian) ringgit have gained even more, so let's not say that Indonesian exports are no longer competitive," said Nasution.\par \par \uc2 Policy Combination To Curb Inflation\par \par \uc2 Sarwono also explained why, despite mounting inflation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its policy interest rate unchanged at 6.50% earlier this month, a level it has been since August 2009.\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices rose 6.44% from a year earlier in August, the fastest in 16 months, leading the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to acknowledge earlier this week that its full-year inflation target of 6.0% would be "hard to achieve."\par \par \uc2 "A rate hike will further attract capital inflows and add more liquidity into the market," said Sarwono, adding that the authorities will not depend solely on adjusting interest rates to tame inflation.\par \par \uc2 Investors have been flocking to Indonesian stocks and bonds, attracted by the country's political stability and strong economic growth. But some of those foreign funds flow out whenever global markets get jittery, pressuring the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a bid to minimize the damage that could arise from such sudden reversal of fund flows, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued a set of policies in June, including a month-long holding period for Bank Indonesia certificates and the introduction of longer maturities for these securities.\par \par \uc2 "That's why we haven't raised the rate but instead issued new policy on primary reserves requirement," he added.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said commercial banks must, from Nov. 1, increase their primary reserves at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 8% of their third-party-funds from the current 5%. Bank Indonesia said the move will likely absorb IDR50 trillion of excess liquidity from the market.\par \par \uc2 Nasution also said that a rate hike will be counterproductive for the economy, cementing the view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep its policy rate unchanged for as long as it can.\par \par \uc2 "I think their move on the reserves requirement is quite aggressive in terms of curbing liquidity," OCBC economist Gundy Cahyadi said.\par \par \uc2 "At the moment we still maintain our forecast for the BI rate to be raised by 50 basis point this year, but their latest move certainly opens up possibilities that they may not need to raise the rate this year," Cahyadi added.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By I-Made Sentana and Andreas Ismar, Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 39831277;andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 17-09-10 1304GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100917e69h000wb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
