\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Higher; 8,975-8,995 Range Tipped Friday\b0\par\par\uc2 1,399 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2010\par 12:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1041 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR higher at 8,985 late vs 8,975 yesterday; foreign investors are probably repatriating funds after taking profit in local stocks, bonds, dealers say. "We will likely see the pair move higher tomorrow, but Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  around IDR8,990," senior dealer at foreign bank says. 8,975-8,995 range tipped Friday. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1028 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR forward premia steady despite comfortable liquidity, which could be read as trigger to pressure rates, says trader with state-run bank; 1-year forward premium at 1.85 vs 1.845 late Wednesday. But market positioning, technicals read for rally rather than correction, with premia for 1-year contract repeatedly foiled by 1.82 support. 1-year contract may even bounce back up to as high as 2.00 as liquidity expected to tighten again in mid-September when advance tax payments begin, trader says. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1023 GMT [Dow Jones] India interest rate swaps steady as market still grappling with permutations on liquidity over next few months, leaving front-end swaps directionless, says trader with foreign bank; 1-year OIS at 6.14%/6.16% vs 6.14%/6.17% last close, 5-year OIS at 7.06%/7.09% vs 7.05%/7.08% last close. "The problem is that we're probably going to be seeing liquidity swinging between the repo and reverse repo rates," says trader. Still, until liquidity tightness begins mid-September when advance tax payments begin, steepeners may be most lucrative bet as government bond yields expected to rise while liquidity operates at lower end of discount window.(bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1023 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares end +0.2% at 3145.14 in moderate volume on expectations for strong 3Q corporate earnings, while gains in regional stock markets lift sentiment, traders say; tip benchmark index in 3120-3160 range Friday. "Foreign investors are still net buyers" picking up IDR22 billion worth of shares today, trader says; locals bought commodity, consumer-related stocks. Coal miner Bukit Asam (PTBA.JK) +7.5% IDR17,950 after company signs $1.6 billion coal railway project with Adani Group; \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) +3.5% IDR4,500 on strong 1H earnings. Profit-taking hits Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK), down 1% at IDR9,700, Bumi (BUMI.JK) down 2.7% at IDR1,780. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1014 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as expected, keeps overnight rates unchanged for 13th month at 4% for borrowing, 6% for lending; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped hints it may keep rates unchanged for longer, noting stronger-than-expected economic growth isn't stoking inflationary pressures. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says latest economic data showed inflation will settle within target of average between 3.5% and 5.5% for this year, between 3% and 5% for 2011 even as economy expands at stronger-than-expected pace. "The (monetary) board observed that the recent strengthening of capital formation could have added to the economy's productive capacity, thus moderating additional price pressures," says Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas Governor Amando Tetangco. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1007 GMT [Dow Jones] Another period of economic weakness could find the world's developed economies short of the sort of fiscal weapons they deployed quite effectively in round one, says Thursday's Taking Stock column. (david.cottle@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0958 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares end +0.2% at 886.10 in active trade, but market breadth neutral with number of gainers, losers about even; gains contained as investors take profit in banks, other recent outperformers, while foreign fund inflows slowed. "I guess it's sort of healthy; I kind of like the fact that we've got this bit of a correction and not outperforming too much," says Andrew Yates, head of sales for international equities at Asia Plus Securities. Bourse lacking drivers at moment, with buying mostly in plays with stock-specific news. Resistance at 890, support at 885. Among top actives, \uc2 Italian-Thai Development\uc2  (ITD.TH) +6.9% at THB3.42, PTT (PTT.TH) +0.4% at THB258, Thaicom (THCOM.TH) +7% at THB8.45 on optimism of new China deal soon, \uc2 Charoen Pokphand Foods\uc2  (CPF.TH) +1% at THB25.25, but \uc2 Jasmine International\uc2  (JAS.TH) down 6.1% at THB1.54 after recent run-up, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) down 1.9% at THB103.50. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0958 GMT [Dow Jones] Dealers betting Malaysian government may soon lift ban on offshore trading of MYR may be in for disappointment; participants have been speculating Bank Negara may do away with ban after bank last week further liberalized \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules; but relaxation is administrative in nature, modest step to help companies reduce their exposure to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations and lower \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  conversion costs. Bank may also want to assess impact of latest rule changes on market before moving towards full \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  convertibility, which existed before 1998. If history is any guide, markets shouldn't expect further relaxation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading rules for a few more years. See: =MONEY TALKS: Malaysia Not Ready To Let Ringgit Trade Offshore. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0957 GMT [Dow Jones] Most Thai government bonds down on profit-taking by some foreign banks after recent steep gains. However, offshore players continue to seek medium and long-term notes, limiting downside for day. Foreign investors parked funds in bonds after they piled up THB positions, pushing THB to fresh 28-month high. "Despite profit-taking by some players, persistent capital inflows will continue to keep bonds well-bid and this likely to prevent any sudden yield reversal," says bond trader. Yields expected to move in tight range tomorrow. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.70%/2.67% vs 2.70%/2.65% late yesterday, November 2016 at 2.77%/2.72% vs 2.73%/2.70%, June 2019 at 2.82%/2.75% vs 2.79%/2.75%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0957 GMT [Dow Jones] The spread between Irish and German bond yields remains stable after the Irish government debt agency, NTMA, sold EUR200 million worth of six month T-bills and EUR400 million eight-month T-bills at average yields of 1.978% and 2.348%, respectively. Coverage is 10.1 times in the six-month maturity and 4.1 times in the eight-month maturity. The 2-year Ireland/Germany yield spread is up 8 bps at 264.5 bps, 5-year spread 2 bps tighter at 286.5 bps and 10-year spread unchanged at 344 bps, all are around the same levels they are at before the T-bill auction. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0955 GMT [Dow Jones] Pakistan shares up on gains in refinery, cement stocks with benchmark KSE-100 index +0.4% at 9589.37. "Cement stocks are in the limelight on expectations that the government will soon start constructing homes, bridges, roads and highways in flood-hit areas, which will boost demand," says local dealer. Says market sentiment aided by foreign investments as data show offshore fund houses invested $1.1 million in equities Wednesday. "There is a likelihood that the IMF (in response to floods) could relax conditions tied to the release of loan installment of $1.56 billion to Pakistan, out of the total aid package of $11.2 billion approved in November 2008," adds local analyst. National Refinery (NRL.KA) +3.9% at PKR192, Attock Cement (ACPL.KA) +1.3% at PKR65.85. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0954 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB slips to fresh 28-month-low of 31.36 vs 31.48 late yesterday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of buying to slow THB's rise but pair's fall below 31.40 caught many players by surprise, raising expectations THB will gain much further, say local dealers. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems helpless, says one dealer. Adds, intuitional investors may continue selling as market widely expecting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hike its policy rate by another 25 bps at October meeting. Pair tipped in 31.30-31.40 range for rest of session. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com) [ 08-26-10 0641ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100826e68q0007q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
