\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Higher; 8,975-8,995 Range Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,394 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2010\par 03:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0143 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR higher at 8,985 vs 8,975 late yesterday as concerns over global recovery spark risk aversion, dealers say. "The dollar is a bit well bid this morning, but expectation among market participants that Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the dollar comes close to IDR9,000 has limited the greenback's rise to IDR8,990 so far this morning," dealer says; tips 8,975-8,995 range for today. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD higher to 7.7771 vs 7.7766 yesterday on stronger USD/JPY overnight. Trader at Chinese bank tips pair to stay in tight range between 7.7750-7.7780 near term. "There are not many sell positions for the U.S. unit recently, allowing the pair to rise easily," says trader. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 32.033 vs 32.055 yesterday, weighed by exporters' selling, but losses limited on weak Taiex (+0.1% so far after down 2.6% yesterday), stronger USD/KRW, says foreign bank trader. Adds, suspects "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may lift the pair higher to 32.100 in late session." Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  prefers a weaker TWD to help island's exporters' month-end settlement. Tips pair in 32.000-32.100 band for day. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0134 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals on Shanghai Futures Exchange modestly higher in response to gains on Wall Street overnight as investors look beyond disappointing housing sales data in hopes U.S. property market has finally bottomed out. Orient Securities Futures analyst Ying Haoliang says chart-based factors also lift metals prices after recent declines; adds metals may stage technical rebound over next few sessions, tips Shanghai copper's nearby resistance at CNY58,000/ton. Benchmark December copper last up 1.2% at CNY57,290/ton, benchmark December zinc last up 1.7% at CNY16,945/ton. (yue.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0134 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW off 1,201 high, fails to convincingly break past psychologically key 1,200 mark, amid Kospi's 0.3% gain. Traders note, KRW closely tracking Kospi moves, which continues to lurch in, out of positive territory. Pair at 1,197.5 vs 1,196.0 late yesterday in Seoul. "The pair tried a couple of times earlier to take out the 1,200 mark, but didn't work. And it doesn't look easy," says local bank trader. Adds, if pair succeeds in breaching 1,200 in spite of exporters waiting near there to sell heavily, could exacerbate pair's rise. Another local bank trader tips 1,195-1,205 range rest of day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped higher as market awaits 2Q GDP data and likely \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move to keep rates at low levels; benchmark PSEi may rise to 3570 immediate resistance after last ending 0.7% higher at 3554.14, with support pegged at 3540. "We still see net foreign buying and that would provide support to the market," says Grace Cerdenia, \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  head at 2TradeAsia.com; adds "the BSP will most likely retain low interest rates." Property, banking stocks likely to lead market advance after shedding some gains in recent days. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] Any rebound in HSI, after 4-session 2.1% losing streak, unlikely major, sustainable, as market in short term likely still in correction phase, says \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2 's Ben Kwong; tips correction, after HSI peaked at 21,805 on August 8, could last a month, although downside from here may not be too much; adds possible HSI will dip below 20,000 briefly but if that happens, would attract medium-term investors. China Life (2628.HK) set to fall on tepid 1H business performance; more blue-chip results eyed with Henderson Land (0012.HK), China Resources (0291.HK), \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK), ICBC (1398.HK), Bank of China (3988.HK), BOC HK (2388.HK) expected today. HSI ends down 0.1% at 20,634.98 yesterday. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures a touch lower in early trade in mild downward correction following recent sharp gains. Analysts, however, don't anticipate big losses as demand for treasuries likely to remain solid amid on-going jitters over global economic recovery, especially after downbeat U.S. new home sales, durable goods data. "Despite the hefty foreign selling in futures yesterday of more than 9,000 contracts in net, bonds held up fairly well, which shows the overall demand for bonds remains solid," says \uc2 Daishin Securities\uc2  analyst Jeong Im-bo. Foreign investors' moves will remain key for market, but overall appetite for bonds likely to be kept solid. 3-year yield quoted +1 bp at 3.58%, 5-year yield +2 bps at 4.13%, September futures down 4 ticks at 112.06. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] Emergence of Chinese-led consortium weighing possible counteroffer to BHP Billiton's (BHP.AU) takeover bid for \uc2 Potash Corp. of Saskatchewan\uc2  (POT) has drawn attention to most important agricultural input you've never heard of--potash, key fertilizer ingredient. Canada and Belarus, two biggest producers, have set up cartel-like arrangements to keep prices buoyant, a major irritation for China. For China, food security concerns make potash national level issue as country doesn't produce enough. China's major source of potassium chloride, type of potash, at Chaerhan Salt Lake in Qinghai, one of its most remote provinces. Lake is production base of Qinghai Salt Lake Potash (000792.SZ), in which a Sinofert Holdings (0297.HK) unit held 18.49% stake at end-2009. According to 2009 annual report, QSLP is China's biggest potassium chloride producer with 25% market share; says two-thirds of China's 2009 demand met by imports. Government's efforts to support agricultural sector likely to result in ever-increasing fertilizer demand in coming years. Good for QSLP, but big headache for Beijing's leadership. See: =WSJ BLOG/China Real Time Report: China's Big Potash Appetite (dinny.mcmahon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei cuts early gains, narrowly off at 8843.07, on political uncertainty as Ichiro Ozawa, former secretary-general of ruling Democratic Party of Japan, expresses intention to run for Sept. 14 election to pick party president, who would likely become prime minister. "The political risk that Mr. Ozawa's participation will further delay government policies (to stem yen's rise) is weighing on the market," says Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities. Adds investors also frustrated that policies such as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary easing unlikely to come out while BOJ Gov. Shirakawa visits U.S. until Aug. 30. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY last 84.69 after rising to intraday high of 84.89, cap at 85.00 heavy due to selling by non-Japanese funds, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. Says ahead of Fed Bernanke speech in annual Jackson Hole Economic Symposium tomorrow, "investors are reluctant to buy the dollar above the psychological level of Y85.00"; adds investors also see Japan exporters' offers above 85.00. Citibank Japan strategist Osamu Takashima tips strong USD/JPY technical resistance at 84.73, then 84.83, 84.90. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian capital spending survey for 2Q, due at 0130 GMT, will be an important influence on sentiment toward the economy, but offshore stock markets will remain the dominant driver of AUD/USD, says John Kyriakopoulos, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at NAB. Says U.S. initial jobless claims will be the focus; data due 1230 GMT. Tips key support for AUD/USD is at 0.8780 and it will take a close below there to portend further falls this week. Adds, this recognizes the risk that a jump in expectations for full blow quantitative easing by the Fed could see a broad-based weakening in the USD, supporting AUD. AUD/USD now 0.8847. (james.glynn@dowjones.com) [ 08-25-10 2143ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100826e68q00017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
