\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Higher; 8,980-9,010 Band Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2010\par 03:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR higher at 8,995 vs 8,975 late yesterday as risk aversion resurfaces, dealers say. No sign of Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slow pair's rise, but \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expected to step into market if pair around 9,005. "Trading activity is quite healthy so far," dealer says. 8,980-9,010 range tipped for day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0109 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude extends losses in electronic trading, after 2.8% drop overnight triggered by unexpected build in U.S. gasoline stockpiles. Upward revision of global oil demand outlook by IEA didn't prop up crude oil prices, as gloomy sentiment on economic recovery drove decline in tandem with equities. "It's true that the U.S. economy is not in a good shape. But it's not all bad either," says \uc2 Mitsubishi Corporation\uc2  Futures analyst Shuji Sugata; adds market tends to overemphasize negative factors when sentiment negative. September Nymex crude down 57 cents at $77.45/bbl on Globex. (mari.iwata@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] RBNZ likely to hike official cash rate just once more in 2010 before pausing due to weak economic recovery, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  Chief Economist Darren Gibbs; "we think that developments over the past month have strengthened the case for slowing the rate of policy normalisation to the point where we now think that even one more 25 basis point hike on September 16 is a stretch." Adds locally, data flow has been discouraging relative to what has been expected; "the combination of local and offshore data has caused the market to reduce the probability of a 25 basis point hike at the September meeting to 56%, with only 28 basis points of tightening now priced for the remainder of the year." Says if RBNZ doesn't hike, unlikely to restart normalization cycle before end of year. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] Potential negative for AUD/USD is that Aug. 21 election race coming into its final week next week is neck and neck and the balance of power could be held by a few independents, says Greg Gibbs, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist RBS. Says has also been a negative race that offers little in way of a fresh vision or strong mandate. Adds, change of government to Liberal will involve several significant policy shifts, such as scrapping of mining tax, no plans for an emissions trading scheme, significant shift on broadband communication policy. Adds, while a mining tax scrapping may be viewed as potential positive for mining companies and boost for AUD, change of government creates significant uncertainty. Says a close election result with a few independents controlling the balance will create fear of policy inaction. AUD/USD now 0.8977. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP likely higher as investors turn risk averse after sharp losses in U.S. stocks overnight, while EUR weakness expected to weigh on risk-sensitive Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Pair may test 45.50 resistance, but if it breaks that level, 45.70 next stop vs 45.235 late Wednesday; support eyed at 45.00. "We're dealing with negative sentiment. The Dow was down over 200 points last night. We may see some banks continuing to cut short positions," says local bank trader; thinks \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may sell (USD) late if pair rises sharply. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to extend gains after hitting 1-week high of 31.880 late yesterday, boosted by stronger global USD, says foreign bank trader. Tips 31.800-31.950 band. Says, investors turn risk averse as "the global economy may face obstacles on the road to recovery." Still, suggests pair capped by exporters selling above 31.950. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 1.0% at 4409.8, hits 3-week low of 4388.0, although its outperforming Wall Street, where S&P 500 fell 2.8% on worse-than-expected U.S. trade data. Follows recent underperformance in S&P/ASX 200 as it reacted to disappointing China imports data. \uc2 Telstra\uc2  (TLS.AU) down 7.4% and James Hardie (JHX.AU) down 8.0% on weak earnings guidance, though resources heavyweight BHP (BHP.AU) down just 0.8% and major banks down only 0.9%-1.4%. "Investors are now questioning the domestic demand from China," says IG Markets Strategist Cameron Peacock. "But the Australian share market already copped a fair whack after those disappointing import numbers out of China. I still think recent Chinese data are consistent with their attempts to bring their economy under control." \uc2 Coca-Cola Amatil\uc2  (CCL.AU) up 4.6% after beating 1H earnings expectations. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD rising as players in Asia cover short positions, pushing pair to 1.2877 from earlier low 1.2830. Move comes after pair marks lowest level since July 23 in early Asia trade at 1.2830. Says further short covering may push pair up to around 1.2900. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Greg Gibbs says it's interesting that in midst of a risk sell-off, USD/JPY hasn't been able to break decisively lower, despite only tepid interest from government to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Notes this suggests market already significantly long JPY, and short USD. Adds USD/JPY should remain relatively stable for now. Says recent economic reports in Japan have been weak, including industrial production, machinery orders; Nikkei has badly underperformed, while the Japanese economy remains highly geared to export-driven demand. As such, says fundamental case for buying JPY is as weak as it is for any of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if not weaker. Adds JPY outlook mixed and there's not a good risk reward trade there at present; positioning is long, risk appetite will probably see it firm, but it's vulnerable to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/JPY now 85.10. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0051 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY set to rise following USD's strong gains vs EUR, dollar index's 1.9% rise, largest single-day rise in 2010 overnight. Dollar-yuan central parity likely to be set above 6.7800 vs 6.7768 yesterday; on OTC USD/CNY tipped in 6.7720-6.7800 for session. "We expect the dollar-yuan's trading level won't be as high as the central parity rate, because some trading desks are holding long dollar positions and may want to square them." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] JGB "yields look likely to come under further downward pressure, perhaps somewhat detached from Japan's own fundamentals" amid U.S. Treasury yield curve bull-flattening rapidly, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief strategist Chotaro Morita. 10-year cash JGB yield last down 3.0 bps at 0.980%, lowest level hit since August 14, 2003. 5-, 20-year yields also lower at 0.300%, 1.575% respectively, both lowest since August 2003. Adds, however, pattern since late July that USD/JPY's fall hurting Japan stock prices and lifting JGBs "may not be that simple in the future." With only slight differences in policy target rates between BOJ and Fed, "no one knows how much exchange rates will be affected by differences in the scale of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  quantitative easing," Morita says. USD/JPY fell to 15-year low Wednesday at 84.72; last at 85.08. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures rise, tracking gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight on increased concerns over global economic recovery; lends support to view BOK likely to keep key policy rate on hold at 2.25% later; decision due around 0100 GMT. "Foreign investors still hold the key...If they start chalking up bond futures again and force securities firms to put in stop loss buy orders," bonds could get another boost from current levels, lead market to see lower-than-expected bond yield levels, says bond manager at local securities firm. But notes, current price levels attractive enough to attract some profit-taking. September futures up 21 ticks at 111.32, 3-year yield quoted down 5 bps at 3.78%, 5-year yield down 5 bps at 4.33%. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 08-11-10 2119ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100812e68c0001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
