\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: China Govt Bonds End Up On 10-Yr Bond Sale Result\b0\par\par\uc2 1,256 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2010\par 09:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0730 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end up after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sells CNY30.44 billion, 10-year bonds at 3.28%, close to lower end of market expectations of 3.27%-3.33%; bond issue receives strong interest from cash-rich banks, insurers, easing concerns that IPO-related liquidity squeeze might hurt demand on fixed-income products, says Beijing-based analyst with local fund; "as the country's economic growth is set to slow down in the coming quarters, bond purchase is a safe choice for investors." Shanghai exchange government bond index ends at 126.40 vs 126.10 last close, might test 126.5 tomorrow. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0729 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian government bond prices higher across board as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaves policy rate at 6.50%. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, although well-expected, helps remove risk of possible rate hike to start as early as today," says dealer; adds, IDR's strength helps underpin demand for government bonds; 1-year yield now down at 6.03% vs 6.30% yesterday; 10-year yield down at 8.03% vs 8.09%; and 30-year yield down at 9.67% vs 9.73%. Dealers say bond prices may consolidate in coming days as yields already low although remain higher than yields in other regional peer countries. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0729 GMT [Dow Jones] Demand for funds at the ECB's weekly refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell Tuesday, and \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says the amount of surplus liquidity in the banking system may fall to EUR95B in the last week of the current reserve maintenance period that ends Aug. 11. While liquidity dynamics will be related to results of the upcoming 1-month special term refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and autonomous factors, BarCap says: "With EUR95B or so of liquidity surplus risk, the Eonia are biased to the upside in the upcoming maintenance period." (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0727 GMT [Dow Jones] With little to change the market's mood, EUR/USD may well test 1.33 now, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 . "Data on both sides of the Atlantic might prove EUR/USD neutral. Therefore, a break of yesterday's 1.3261 high for a 1.33 test may easily be the most likely outcome today, especially if equities again stay firm," the bank says. EUR/USD now at 1.3210. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0723 GMT [Dow Jones] The Aussie may be getting hit by the decline in risk sentiment but those strong trade figures should provide some support. As Standard Bank points out, the trade surplus may have come in at nearly double expectations with al the gain coming from exports. "We think that the data is of significance even if the strength for the Aussie after the number soon ran out of steam due to rising risk aversion," the bank says. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0722 GMT [Dow Jones] There was no new attempt to break EUR/SEK descending support Tuesday, with the market instead setting itself on a corrective path and likely re-checking the 9.40 area, says SEB. Adds that given the low and bearish close last month the trend assumption must be corrected accordingly, but it should be observed that a move back above 9.55 would take it back to neutral again. EUR/SEK trades at 9.3812. (dominic.chopping@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0718 GMT [Dow Jones] The GBP will have one last burst of buying that should send it sailing over $1.60, especially if the USD remains as weak as it has been, says Nicholas Hastings in \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus. After that, though, economic realities will hit home and support for the U.K. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will wane. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0716 GMT [Dow Jones] Swedish CPI, excluding interest rate costs, is likely to continue to trend downwards going into '11, says SEB, and expects the figure to be mostly lower than the Riksbank's forecast. Expects the July number to decline 1.6% on the year "mainly due to large base effects from unusually small summer sales on clothes in '09." Notes the stronger krona is a downside risk, but recent signs of less downward pressure on international producer prices on consumer goods in combination with higher commodity prices on food indicate risks now are more balanced than earlier. Sweden's two year bond yield is 0.5 bps lower at 1.4%, while the 10-year bond yield also trades down 0.5 bps at 2.7%. (dominic.chopping@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0714 GMT [Dow Jones] Switzerland's rate of unemployment in July is expected to have fallen to 3.6%, from 3.7% in June, according to the average forecast of four economists' forecasts compiled by DJN. They predict the rate dropped for a sixth consecutive month as the robust economic rebound, driven by rising exports and consumer demand, persuaded companies to hire more workers. The data are due Friday, Aug. 6 at 0545 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0712 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR still steady at 8,940 after Bank Indonesia, as expected, leaves policy rate unchanged at 6.50%. "Bank Indonesia's decision was well anticipated given its reluctance to start tightening in order to allow the economy to continue growing," dealer with foreign bank says; adds sentiment on IDR still positive, without suspected Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pair may have fallen to 8,930. 8,940-8,950 range now tipped rest of day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0709 GMT [Dow Jones] Foreigners have long been enamored of Indian stocks, but typically not in mood for long-term relationship. That's changing, says Heard on the Street. One indication comes from declining use of participatory notes, which offer foreigners way to avoid registering with Indian authorities. Regulators have never been happy with this arrangement, in part because P-notes give anonymity, an opportunity for wealthy Indians to avoid taxes, encourage speculative capital flows. From SEBI's point of view, drop in use of P-notes is welcome; good news for India's markets is this hasn't discouraged inflows - a reflection of foreigners' growing confidence in New Delhi's intent to further liberalize economy. True, heavy investment by foreigners is always risky, registering as FII doesn't mean global funds won't rush to exit if spooked; but rush could prove less hasty if more foreigners are thinking long-term; win-win here is that market will become more liquid, more efficient; in this fast-changing economy, that's a development worth appreciating. See: "=HEARD ON THE STREET: A Less Fleeting Engagement With India." (harsh.joshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0705 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank Indonesia keeps policy rate unchanged at 6.50% as expected, blames high inflation in July on seasonal factor. Though statement tone slightly hawkish, it offers little clue when bank likely to start rate hikes, analysts say. "We predict the rate hikes to start in the fourth quarter, with 25 basis points in November and another 25 basis points in December," says Bank Danamon economist Anton Gunawan; previously he expected rate hike to start in January. Meanwhile, OCBC economist Gundy Cahyadi notes BI statement that it wants to manage inflationary expectation going forward indicates "a rate hike should not be ruled out later in the year." (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com) [ 08-04-10 0330ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM000