\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Steady At 8,940; Rate Call Well Expected\b0\par\par\uc2 1,828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2010\par 09:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0712 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR still steady at 8,940 after Bank Indonesia, as expected, leaves policy rate unchanged at 6.50%. "Bank Indonesia's decision was well anticipated given its reluctance to start tightening in order to allow the economy to continue growing," dealer with foreign bank says; adds sentiment on IDR still positive, without suspected Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , pair may have fallen to 8,930. 8,940-8,950 range now tipped rest of day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0709 GMT [Dow Jones] Foreigners have long been enamored of Indian stocks, but typically not in mood for long-term relationship. That's changing, says Heard on the Street. One indication comes from declining use of participatory notes, which offer foreigners way to avoid registering with Indian authorities. Regulators have never been happy with this arrangement, in part because P-notes give anonymity, an opportunity for wealthy Indians to avoid taxes, encourage speculative capital flows. From SEBI's point of view, drop in use of P-notes is welcome; good news for India's markets is this hasn't discouraged inflows - a reflection of foreigners' growing confidence in New Delhi's intent to further liberalize economy. True, heavy investment by foreigners is always risky, registering as FII doesn't mean global funds won't rush to exit if spooked; but rush could prove less hasty if more foreigners are thinking long-term; win-win here is that market will become more liquid, more efficient; in this fast-changing economy, that's a development worth appreciating. See: "=HEARD ON THE STREET: A Less Fleeting Engagement With India." (harsh.joshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0705 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank Indonesia keeps policy rate unchanged at 6.50% as expected, blames high inflation in July on seasonal factor. Though statement tone slightly hawkish, it offers little clue when bank likely to start rate hikes, analysts say. "We predict the rate hikes to start in the fourth quarter, with 25 basis points in November and another 25 basis points in December," says Bank Danamon economist Anton Gunawan; previously he expected rate hike to start in January. Meanwhile, OCBC economist Gundy Cahyadi notes BI statement that it wants to manage inflationary expectation going forward indicates "a rate hike should not be ruled out later in the year." (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0704 GMT [Dow Jones] China poised to accelerate expansion of crude oil, products storage capacity with major implications for oil demand. According to Xinhua News Agency, China's oil product storage capacity may reach close to 500 million barrels by 2015, nearly 50% above last year's level. That's significant as inventory buildup largely unrelated to GDP growth; tanks cannot be left idle due to maintenance costs. Recent oil pipeline explosion, heavy rains in NE China underscore need for greater stockpiling to cover any sudden supply disruptions. IEA members such as U.S. required to hold oil stocks equivalent to 90 days' worth of net imports; in contrast, China's end-June crude oil inventory wouldn't cover two months of net imports. "China cannot afford to allow its oil inventory to be stagnant. It must build oil inventory at a faster pace than demand if China wants to improve its consumption-day coverage," says Paul Ting, U.S.-based independent analyst specializing in China. See: "=CHINA ENERGY WATCH: Big Boost In Store For Oil Stocks, Demand" (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0653 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares still mostly lower, with pullback in most Asian markets and negative U.S. stock futures holding buyers back. STI off 0.3% at 3004.72 after briefly slipping below 3000 in post-lunch trade to low of 2997.23; support at 2979 (July 30 low). "On a fundamental view, we believe that Singapore's prospects are better than most regional economies'. Look for such technical pullbacks for re-entry opportunities," says \uc2 Kim Eng Securities\uc2 . Overall participation remains low, with volume less than half of yesterday's 2.25 billion shares, with small caps most active. Key decliners among blue chips include Jardine group of stocks, \uc2 CapitaMalls Asia\uc2  (JS8.SG), off 2.3% at S$2.11, \uc2 Keppel Corp\uc2 . (BN4.SG), off 2.4% at S$9.11. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0651 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.3% at 1360.16; local dealer says market succumbing to profit-taking but traded volumes thinner than of late at 335 million shares. "This is not a surprise after the strong gains over the past week and a half," says dealer; still, notes index supported at the current 1360 level, but if decisively breached could test next support at 1355 (10-day moving average). Market breadth negative with 394 decliners vs 201 advancers; decliners include CIMB (1023.KU) down 0.8% at MYR7.39, Berjaya Corp. (3395.KU) down 1.0% at MYR1.03, Genting (3182.KU) down 0.8% at MYR7.88. Advancers include AirAsia (5099.KU) +0.7% at MYR1.51, E&O (3417.KU) +1.7% at MYR1.20. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares still down 0.2% at 2967.500 in moderate volume, unaffected by Bank Indonesia leaving key interest rate unchanged at 6.50%, traders say; add, weak Asian markets bring negative sentiment to local bourse; tip support at 2950. "There are fears that rising inflation for August due to spending during Ramadan will in the end force the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  next month to hike its key interest rate," says trader at \uc2 Kresna Securities\uc2 . Foreigners net sellers of IDR144 billion worth of shares. Among decliners, car maker Astra (ASII.JK) down 2.2% at IDR46,500, food producer \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) down 2.9% at IDR4,200, while while tin producer Timah (TINS.JK) down 3.1% at IDR2,325. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0648 GMT [Dow Jones] Cash bunds have pared gains, after a sharp surge on Tuesday, and while most signs point to slower growth in advanced economies, the 10-year bund yield may not slip below 2.50% just yet, says WestLB. US data in the afternoon should supply further indications that the growth momentum is slackening, whereby particularly the ADP report will be closely watched in view of the payroll figures due on Friday. "However, on the whole, the data is unlikely to be sufficiently weak to drive the 10 year bund yield significantly towards the 2.50% level," WestLB says. The 10-year bund is a tad lower at 103.41 to yield 2.605%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.2% at 21,501.07 vs 21,455.79 midday, rising for 11th session out of 12, as strong buying interest more than offsets profit-taking pressure. Volume modest at HK$36.75 billion, some still eying 22,000 as target this month. "The market may only succumb to temporary profit-taking near term, as earnings-driven upward momentum appears intact," says Taifook Securities. Such view supported by \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2  (0293.HK) now +3.8% at HK$18.06, marking 2.5-year high, after posting consensus-beating 1H net profit, which soared over eightfold to HK$6.84 billion. Mainland developers succumbing to profit-taking: China Overseas Land (0688.HK) down 1.9% at HK$17.18, \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) off 3.9% at HK$16.60. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] September bunds are up 0.14 at 129.20. \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2  says the short-term bias has changed to positive after the strong gains made on Tuesday. The bank sees immediate resistance at 129.29, a break of which could open the door for a climb towards 129.54. Support is pegged at 128.75 and 128.58. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] Nomura says while it remains positive on India on 12-month perspective, it recommends "near-term tactical neutral stance," following recent market rally (Sensex +5.8% in 3 months) and given possible near-term impact of tightening in monetary aggregates. "Valuations have now normalised. The earnings season has passed rather uneventfully with flat to lower earnings relative to consensus, so we see no trigger for markets on that count," says house. Adds that recent system-wide squeeze in liquidity, emerging signs of easing in economic activity leads it to think that "we might be heading into a period of market consolidation." Stays bullish on banks, real estate but says moving to underweight on autos, especially following period of significant outperformance; adds that weakness in capex stocks (infrastructure, construction) would offer good buying opportunity. Sensex last +0.1% at 18,123.04. (shikhar.balwani@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0635 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly lower after Wall Street declines Tuesday; Tokyo shares weighed by strong JPY; Nikkei down 2.1%, S&P/ASX down 0.7%, Shanghai Composite down 0.6%, STI down 0.5%, NZX-50 down 0.4%, Kospi down 0.1%; HSI flat, Taiex +0.2%, Sensex flat, SET +0.7%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, JPY climbs; USD/JPY at 85.40, touches 8-month lows, vs 85.85 late NY trade Tuesday, EUR/JPY at 112.76 vs 113.60; EUR/USD at 1.3203 vs 1.3233. Benchmark 10-year JGB yield below 1.0% for first time in 7 years, trades as low as 0.995%, amid speculation government could pressure BOJ to take further easing steps. Slew of data from Australia: June trade surplus biggest on record at A$3.54 billion vs May's A$1.83 billion surplus, A$1.9 billion surplus forecast, on surging mining exports; Australian Industry Group-Commonwealth Bank Performance of Services Index down 2.2 points in July vs June at 46.6, below 50-point mark separating contraction from expansion; Commonwealth Bank of Australia-Australian Chamber of Commerce & Industry Business Expectations Survey falls to seasonally adjusted 51.7 in July vs 1Q's 64.0; 2Q existing home price index +3.1% on-quarter, +18.4% on-year. Hong Kong July \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI down at 51.3 vs June's 52.6. South Koreans' 1H overseas direct investment +37.3% on-year at $11.48 billion. RBI prunes Deputy Governor K.C. Chakrabarty's responsibilities, possibly triggered by his recent unscheduled comments to media on monetary policy. Bank Indonesia keeps benchmark rate unchanged at 6.50%, but says soon to introduce liquidity tightening moves, govern banks' reserve requirements. Spot gold at [ 08-04-10 0312ET ]\par \par \uc2 $1,191.20/oz, up $5.60 vs NY close. September Nymex crude oil futures down 50 cents at $82.05/bbl. (leslie.shaffer@dowjones.com) [ 08-04-10 0312ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100804e68400058\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
