\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Fitch's Vietnam Downgrade No Surprise-Capital Econ\b0\par\par\uc2 1,450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2010\par 04:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0220 GMT [Dow Jones] Fitch downgrade of Vietnam's credit rating no surprise given government's muddled policy-making, says Capital Economics. "Over the last six months domestic policies have see-sawed between fighting inflation and boosting growth while the balance of payments, although on an improving trend, remains far weaker than elsewhere in Asia." House says while 2010 GDP growth will likely hit or exceed government target, further interest rate increases along with another VND devaluation are necessary to ensure economic upswing extends into 2011. Capital Economics says it shares Fitch concerns about coming pressures from rising bad loans. While inflation eased to +8.2% in June, still likely to touch +9.0% by year-end, with house also forecasting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to lift benchmark interest rate to 9.0% vs 8.0% currently by end-2010. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian house price, housing credit data show weakness in interest rate sensitive areas of economy through 2Q, following RBA rate hikes in March, April and May, says \uc2 UBS\uc2  chief economist Scott Haslem. Notes house price growth has also clearly slowed, reversing strength in 1Q that had evidently concerned RBA; soft credit data in June also likely to reflect an ongoing desire to repay debt. Data reinforce expectations RBA will leave interest rates unchanged at its August meeting next week, and increases likelihood it will remain on hold for several months to come. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] Markets got spooked yesterday when RBNZ Governor Alan Bollard said "pace and extent of further OCR increases is likely to be more moderate than was projected in the June Statement," but may be jumping gun by predicting pause in current hiking cycle as early as October. Bollard unlikely to put current tightening cycle on hold barely two months into it. Rather, three more 25 bp hikes in next three meetings much more likely, would still only bring OCR to 3.75%, a supportive level vs current "very supportive" 3.00%. But most economists have different view: Of 11 polled by Dow Jones, only four tip RBNZ to keep hiking while majority expect pause before year-end. Data will be key, as will U.S. recovery. Bank of NZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Mike Jones says all Bollard did Thursday was remind market he can pause, but "it doesn't necessarily mean it will be at the next meeting." See: =MONEY TALKS: RBNZ Set To Continue Tightening For Rest Of 2010 (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Huge AUD/JPY sell-stops stand below 77.50, says Singapore dealer. AUD/JPY last 77.80.(jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares moving in narrow band, with negative sentiment after overnight losses in Wall Street tempered by expectations of more strong 2Q earnings results; benchmark PSEi down 0.1% to 3426.35 in moderate volume, with 3400 as support and 3450 as resistance; market breadth positive, with gainers ahead of losers 40 to 34. Advancers led by Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), up 0.2% to PHP59.90 and \uc2 Bank of the Philippine Islands\uc2  (BPI.PH), up 0.7% to PHP47.60, ahead of rights offer where shares to be sold at discount of around 30%, while losers paced by Ayala Land (ALI.PH), down 0.3% to PHP14.50 and Philippine National Bank (PNB.PH), down 2.5% to PHP37.55. PCCI Securities Brokers says aside from declines in U.S. share prices on weak earnings and jobless claims data, most of trending and volume oscillators are turning bearish for the near-term. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank Indonesia suspected of buying around $50 million with USD/IDR at 8,950 via state banks as pair slides to lows not seen since June 2007, three dealers say. "The amount of dollars bought by Bank Indonesia was small as the market is a bit thin," dealer with foreign bank says; suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifts USD/IDR slightly to 8,955 from 8,950, but pair still lower than 8,975 late yesterday. Bank Indonesia deputy governor Hartadi Sarwono told Dow Jones yesterday \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue "cautious \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " to smoothen \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuation. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds lower on Shanghai Securities News report that China's regulator will raise amount it allows insurers to invest in stock market to 25% of their assets, from 20% currently; report fuels concerns that insurers, one of key investors in local bond market, might cut debt holdings to lift equities investment, says Guotai Junan Securities; notes \uc2 China Everbright Bank\uc2 's upcoming IPO also adds pressure to money market liquidity. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.00 vs 126.06 last close, likely in 125.9-126.0 band rest of session. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely slightly higher, supported by strong 1H earnings, firmer IDR, traders say; tip resistance at 3120 after ending up 1.3% yesterday at fresh record high 3096.816. But trader at First Asia Capital warns that "bearish Asian markets may inspire profit taking after the recent rally." Car maker Astra (ASII.JK) may gain after reporting 1H net profit up 52%, cigarette maker Gudang garam (GGRM.JK) may rise after reporting 1H net profit up 24%. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY falls tad, tracking lower dollar-yuan central parity rate at 6.7750 vs 6.7787 yesterday. On OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7745 vs 6.7761 last close; "there's some dollar selling given the lower fixing and a stronger euro," says Shanghai-based trader at European bank. "But overall it's still rangebound." Adds typical thinner trading during summer months, lack of market-moving news contribute to stable USD/CNY in near term. USD/CNY tipped in narrow 6.7730-6.7770 band for session. Offshore 1-year USD/CNY NDFs at 6.6830/6.6870 vs 6.6860/6.6900 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese industrial output growth likely to continue slowing into year-end as government's economic stimulus measures such as purchase incentives either show diminishing returns or are set to expire, also as overseas demand pulls back, says Norio Miyagawa, economist at Mizuho Securities Research & Consulting. Data show June production fell seasonally adjusted 1.5% on-month; "Today's figure shows that industrial activity is going to slow some for the rest of the year, in part as the U.S. economy also slows some and as Chinese policy adjustments suggest a slight chilling of strong growth there." Tips October-December output to slow to +0.5% on-quarter, after projected +1.1% in July-September. Adds, if recent JPY strength persists, could affect corporate earnings projections, prompting capex cuts. But says if global economic growth manages to avoid losing much more momentum, "we could see a pickup again in industrial output from around the middle of 2011 as part of the natural business cycle, since we'd then have firms looking to increase inventories again." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR continues sliding to 8,950 (lowest since early June 2007) vs 8,975 late yesterday on USD's weakness vs major rivals. "Most of the dollar sellers are foreign names," dealer says; adds, foreigners likely sell USD to buy Indonesian stocks. Notes foreign net buying amounted to IDR1.2 trillion yesterday; no sign of Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this morning. Many analysts have expected pair to continue sliding toward 8,800 by year-end. "(IDR8,800) is the equilibrium for the dollar," says Stanchart economist Eric Sugandi; 8,930-8,970 range tipped for Friday. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 31.980 vs 32.068 yesterday due to large sell orders for month-end exporter settlements, says trader with foreign bank. Tips pair in 31.950-32.050 band today, key support at 32.00 given \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop local unit rising rapidly. "Some investors have tried to trade below 32.00 in the last few sessions, but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought USD at the closing to make the pair stay above that level. I expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do the same later today," she says. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com) [ 07-29-10 2220ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100730e67u0001w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
