 demand pulls back, says Norio Miyagawa, economist at Mizuho Securities Research & Consulting. Data show June production fell seasonally adjusted 1.5% on-month; "Today's figure shows that industrial activity is going to slow some for the rest of the year, in part as the U.S. economy also slows some and as Chinese policy adjustments suggest a slight chilling of strong growth there." Tips October-December output to slow to +0.5% on-quarter, after projected +1.1% in July-September. Adds, if recent JPY strength persists, could affect corporate earnings projections, prompting capex cuts. But says if global economic growth manages to avoid losing much more momentum, "we could see a pickup again in industrial output from around the middle of 2011 as part of the natural business cycle, since we'd then have firms looking to increase inventories again." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR continues sliding to 8,950 (lowest since early June 2007) vs 8,975 late yesterday on USD's weakness vs major rivals. "Most of the dollar sellers are foreign names," dealer says; adds, foreigners likely sell USD to buy Indonesian stocks. Notes foreign net buying amounted to IDR1.2 trillion yesterday; no sign of Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this morning. Many analysts have expected pair to continue sliding toward 8,800 by year-end. "(IDR8,800) is the equilibrium for the dollar," says Stanchart economist Eric Sugandi; 8,930-8,970 range tipped for Friday. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 31.980 vs 32.068 yesterday due to large sell orders for month-end exporter settlements, says trader with foreign bank. Tips pair in 31.950-32.050 band today, key support at 32.00 given \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop local unit rising rapidly. "Some investors have tried to trade below 32.00 in the last few sessions, but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought USD at the closing to make the pair stay above that level. I expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do the same later today," she says. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD falling vs JPY, USD due to falling share prices in Japan as well as recent weaker-than-expected Australia CPI data that pushed back investor expectations for RBA's next rate-hike, say senior dealers in Tokyo. Some dealers say U.S., European hedge funds recently increasing selling of AUD. Yet, pace of declines likely be not fast as big non-Japanese banks buying a lot of these units, providing some support. AUD/USD last at 0.8984, AUD/JPY last at 77.80. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 07-29-10 2219ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100730e67u0001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
