\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Bank Indonesia Likely Buys USD At IDR8,950-Dealers\b0\par\par\uc2 1,392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2010\par 04:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank Indonesia suspected of buying around $50 million with USD/IDR at 8,950 via state banks as pair slides to lows not seen since June 2007, three dealers say. "The amount of dollars bought by Bank Indonesia was small as the market is a bit thin," dealer with foreign bank says; suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lifts USD/IDR slightly to 8,955 from 8,950, but pair still lower than 8,975 late yesterday. Bank Indonesia deputy governor Hartadi Sarwono told Dow Jones yesterday \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to continue "cautious \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " to smoothen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fluctuation. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds lower on Shanghai Securities News report that China's regulator will raise amount it allows insurers to invest in stock market to 25% of their assets, from 20% currently; report fuels concerns that insurers, one of key investors in local bond market, might cut debt holdings to lift equities investment, says Guotai Junan Securities; notes \uc2 China Everbright Bank\uc2 's upcoming IPO also adds pressure to money market liquidity. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.00 vs 126.06 last close, likely in 125.9-126.0 band rest of session. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely slightly higher, supported by strong 1H earnings, firmer IDR, traders say; tip resistance at 3120 after ending up 1.3% yesterday at fresh record high 3096.816. But trader at First Asia Capital warns that "bearish Asian markets may inspire profit taking after the recent rally." Car maker Astra (ASII.JK) may gain after reporting 1H net profit up 52%, cigarette maker Gudang garam (GGRM.JK) may rise after reporting 1H net profit up 24%. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY falls tad, tracking lower dollar-yuan central parity rate at 6.7750 vs 6.7787 yesterday. On OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7745 vs 6.7761 last close; "there's some dollar selling given the lower fixing and a stronger euro," says Shanghai-based trader at European bank. "But overall it's still rangebound." Adds typical thinner trading during summer months, lack of market-moving news contribute to stable USD/CNY in near term. USD/CNY tipped in narrow 6.7730-6.7770 band for session. Offshore 1-year USD/CNY NDFs at 6.6830/6.6870 vs 6.6860/6.6900 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese industrial output growth likely to continue slowing into year-end as government's economic stimulus measures such as purchase incentives either show diminishing returns or are set to expire, also as overseas demand pulls back, says Norio Miyagawa, economist at Mizuho Securities Research & Consulting. Data show June production fell seasonally adjusted 1.5% on-month; "Today's figure shows that industrial activity is going to slow some for the rest of the year, in part as the U.S. economy also slows some and as Chinese policy adjustments suggest a slight chilling of strong growth there." Tips October-December output to slow to +0.5% on-quarter, after projected +1.1% in July-September. Adds, if recent JPY strength persists, could affect corporate earnings projections, prompting capex cuts. But says if global economic growth manages to avoid losing much more momentum, "we could see a pickup again in industrial output from around the middle of 2011 as part of the natural business cycle, since we'd then have firms looking to increase inventories again." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR continues sliding to 8,950 (lowest since early June 2007) vs 8,975 late yesterday on USD's weakness vs major rivals. "Most of the dollar sellers are foreign names," dealer says; adds, foreigners likely sell USD to buy Indonesian stocks. Notes foreign net buying amounted to IDR1.2 trillion yesterday; no sign of Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this morning. Many analysts have expected pair to continue sliding toward 8,800 by year-end. "(IDR8,800) is the equilibrium for the dollar," says Stanchart economist Eric Sugandi; 8,930-8,970 range tipped for Friday. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 31.980 vs 32.068 yesterday due to large sell orders for month-end exporter settlements, says trader with foreign bank. Tips pair in 31.950-32.050 band today, key support at 32.00 given \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop local unit rising rapidly. "Some investors have tried to trade below 32.00 in the last few sessions, but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought USD at the closing to make the pair stay above that level. I expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do the same later today," she says. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD falling vs JPY, USD due to falling share prices in Japan as well as recent weaker-than-expected Australia CPI data that pushed back investor expectations for RBA's next rate-hike, say senior dealers in Tokyo. Some dealers say U.S., European hedge funds recently increasing selling of AUD. Yet, pace of declines likely be not fast as big non-Japanese banks buying a lot of these units, providing some support. AUD/USD last at 0.8984, AUD/JPY last at 77.80. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares likely to slightly rebound after failing to break 490 technical support this week, with investors coming back to buy with hopes market to recover in August. "Many companies are facing high \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs due to high interest rates, but I expect the current economic recovery to continue and buying appetite to soon return to the market," says trader. Tips key index in 492-495 range after ending flat at 491.11 yesterday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares down 0.7% at 7747.06, weighed by weak Asian shares, profit-taking, says Mega International Investment Service manager Stanley Chou. Notes investors trimming stock positions as major companies "mostly provide neutral with conservative view" at their 2Q investors conferences this week, he says. Losses led by TSMC (2330.TW), last down 2.4% at NT$61.50, as investors remain concerned about potential chip oversupply, rising inventories in 4Q, he says. Still, "results and guidances from major companies are mostly not negative so far, so it is a good time to buy on dips," he says. Tips Taiex in 7700-7800 band for day. TSMC posts better-than-expected 2Q results Thursday, with 2Q net profit +65% on-year at record NT$40.28 billion, with Chairman Morris Chang hiking his 2010 chip contract industry revenue growth forecasts to 40% from 36%. HTC (2498.TW) +0.4% at NT$572.00, extending yesterday's 6.9% gain, after handset maker says late Thursday it expects 3Q revenue to more than double to record of about NT$70 billion. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] August HSI futures down 0.8% at 20,993, as cash market tipped to finally pull back after succumbing to profit-taking following 8-day winning streak. Contract ended yesterday at big 68-point premium to cash, vs August fair value estimated at 40-50-points discount to cash, suggesting bullish near-term view persists. August open interest at 78,276 lots. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI little changed at 1358.92, trapped in narrow 3-point range in subdued early trade. "The market's outlook remains positive, but investors are taking a cautious approach at the moment due to a lack of new bullish developments," dealer says; tips key index to remain in narrow 1355-1360 band until midday. Property stocks higher amid signs of improvement in property sales, with Bolton (1538.KU) +5.7% at MYR1.02, Ivory (5175.KU) +2.9% at MYR1.40. Among decliners, tech-related names lower, with JCY (5161.KU) down 2.3%, Telekom (4863.KU) down 0.9% at MYR3.36. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com) [ 07-29-10 2216ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100730e67u0001r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
