\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Bank Indonesia 'May Loosen Up A Bit' On IDR Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 1,706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juli 2010\par 08:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0656 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's approach of 'cautious \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' to smooth fluctuations of USD/IDR pair still being digested by market players, dealers say. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Hartadi Sarwono said "maintains cautious \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out exchange rate fluctuation" after pair nearly breached IDR9,000 mark yesterday. Dealers surprised as there was no visible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "BI usually tries to keep pair from breaching 9,000. I'm still digesting BI's recent approach, but I think this may mean BI will loosen up a bit, let IDR to breach the psychological level, before buying of USD in small amounts," dealer at local bank says. Dealers suspected BI's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last seen Tuesday, buying around $30 million, just a fraction from hundreds of millions it usually spent to stem fluctuations. USD/IDR pares earlier gain, last at 9,007 vs 9,010 early, 9,003 late yesterday. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0655 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.2% at 1357.54 in moderate volume of 540 million shares. Trading mixed with some profit-taking after 4 days of gains, but "positive trend is still intact," dealer with local bank-backed brokerage says. Tips 1360 as immediate resistance; 1350 support likely intact. Advancers, decliners about equally matched; among top actives, Axiata (6888.KU) +5.1% at MYR4.36, Berjaya Corp (3395.KU) +2.8% at MYR1.12, Measat (3875.KU) +7.1% at MYR4.07, JCY (5161.KU) down 2.9% at MYR1.32, Maxis (6012.KU) down 0.9% at MYR5.27. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0653 GMT [Dow Jones] For the first time in months, global investors appear more confident about taking risks. This doesn't mean that they won't lose their confidence again, but it does mean the USD might look more attractive, says \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus by Nicholas Hastings. This more 'risk on' mode has emerged for two reasons: fears of a double-dip recession in the US have largely receded and worries about the European financial system are a lot less than they were now that bank stress test results have been published. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0648 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.1% at 21,071.52, snapping 7-session winning streak (+5.0%), but profit-taking looks modest so far, as China markets remain buoyant (Shanghai Composite last +0.6%), says Mark To at Wing Fung Financial. Tips 20,500-21,200 near-term trading range. "Consolidation looks inevitable ahead after the HSI's recent strong showing, but I think the underlying sentiment remains relatively strong, supported by the Chinese markets." ICBC (1398.HK) +0.7% at HK$5.92, ignoring plan to raise CNY45 billion via rights issue, as such fund-raising plan well flagged. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) down 0.8% at HK$80.25 ahead of 1H results Monday, mild fall vs 9.4% gain over past 7 sessions. Market volume modest at HK$33.96 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0638 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Japan June housing starts likely +1.5% on-year vs 4.6% fall in previous month, a Dow Jones, Nikkei poll of 24 economists shows; would be first rise in 2 months. Data likely to show demand for individual homes, condominium apartments increased due to pickup in income, employment conditions, analysts say. Sales of such property may continue to increase in months ahead, but moderate economic recovery may cap increase in housing starts. Data due 0500 GMT Friday. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0633 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds fall sharply after RBI official who didn't want to be identified told reporters more aggressive monetary policy action is needed as inflation cannot be tamed at current rate levels, says trader with state-run bank. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond slides to 100.18 after comments vs 100.38 before, 100.32 last close. Trader adds, market spooked by hawkish comments that policy rates should have gone up much more by now, comments come just 2 days after RBI hiked key rates. Says, rise in weekly inflation data for primary articles and food may have added to bearish sentiment. Tips benchmark 2020 bond in 100.15-100.40 range in session. India wholesale price index for primary articles +0.4% in week to July 17, +14.50% on-year. Food articles index +0.6% in week, +9.67% on-year, down significantly from +12.47% in previous week. Fuel products down 0.1% in week. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0626 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mixed; Nikkei down 0.6%, S&P/ASX 200 down 0.1%, Shanghai Composite flat, Kospi down 0.2%, HSI off 0.1%, NZX-50 +0.4%, STI +0.1%, Sensex +0.1%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, dollar weaker vs JPY, EUR as U.S. Treasury yields decline on concerns over U.S. economic recovery; USD/JPY at 87.22 vs 87.44 in late NY trade, EUR/USD at 1.3007 vs 1.2988, EUR/JPY at 113.47 vs 113.54. Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says current policy "modest and gradual appreciation" of SGD remains appropriate, as economic activity expected to remain high, though the pace of expansion will ease; MAS to start selling its own 3-month MAS Bills from 2H, new type of security will help MAS conduct money-market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . RBNZ hikes Official Cash Rate by 25 bps to 3.0%, in line with expectations after similar hike last month; but bank says further rate hikes would depend on market developments while pace, extent of those hikes may be more moderate than projected; NZD/USD rose to 0.7288 on hike, then fell back to 0.7226 as dovish statement digested; last at 0.7242. New Zealand June trade surplus at NZ$276 million vs revised NZ$768 million surplus in May, below NZ$320.5 million surplus forecast in Dow Jones poll. Japan merchandise trade surplus at Y243.29 billion in first 10 days of July vs Y43.38 billion deficit year-earlier period; country's June retail sales +3.2% on-year, vs revised +2.9% in May. Bank of Korea August Manufacturers' Outlook Index fell to 102 vs 104 in July; but BOK survey's index remains above key 100-point level for sixth straight month. Spot gold at $1,166.40/oz, up $3.10 vs NY close. September Nymex crude oil futures recently flat at $76.99/bbl. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] September bunds are up 0.15 at 127.96 but \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2  sees no reason to worry for investors holding short positions. "The overall outlook still favours the short side, and a rally to 128.18 or even the low 50s is an opportunity to sell at better levels," the bank says. Resistance is pegged at 128.18 and 128.50 while support is at 127.45 and 127.12. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0558 GMT [Dow Jones] The recent rally in USD/CHF is over for now, and the pair will hold below 1.10 over the coming year, says Hyposwiss. "The data underpinning the dollar aren't convincing, and while the U.S. economy is performing positively, the state of the national budget is anything but confidence-inspiring," says chief investment officer Thomas Stucki. Hyposwiss forecasts USD/CHF at 1.02 to 1.07 in three months, and at 1.0 to 1.05 in 12 months. USD/CHF trades at 1.0548. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0553 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 5.9% at HK$1.339 billion midday, 4.2% of total market volume vs 4.3% at Wednesday's midday, suggesting view HSI's 0.1% retreat normal consolidation following 7-day winning streak. A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) tops list with HK$163.63 million, no other stocks manage more than HK$100 million. China Mobile (0941.HK) in second place with HK$85.83 million, then come Tencent (0700.HK) with HK$82.33 million, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) with HK$62.08 million, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$60.61 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0551 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares off 0.5% at 849.97 at midday break. Benchmark volatile in active trade as sharp rise since end-May prompts retail investors to take profit while institutional, foreign investors hold positions amid absence of fresh drivers, says BFIT Securities' strategist Aphisit Limsupanark. Adds last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  GDP upgrade suggested upside for SET Index but weakness in overseas bourses hurt sentiment; 847-860 band tipped. Among top actives, \uc2 True Corp\uc2  (TRUE.TH) down 1.4% at THB4.32, \uc2 Charoen Pokphand Foods\uc2  (CPF.TH) down 1.2% at THB24, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) down 1.9% at THB88.75, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) down 0.3% at THB148.50. Bucking trend, \uc2 Thai Union Frozen Products\uc2  (TUF.TH) +7% at THB50 though off historical high of THB50.75 tapped intraday as news of \uc2 MW Brands\uc2  takeover could strongly lift 2011 earnings. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds end higher, lifted by positive cue from U.S. Treasurys, weak stocks earlier (though stocks climb to end +0.2%), and as liquidity remains flush, says local bank trader. "Buying interest (for longer duration) remains strong from investors, and because of the strong Taiwan dollar, foreign investors are also buying shorter maturities," he says. Yield on most-active 5-year bond ends down at 0.9404% vs 0.9476%, vs intraday low of 0.9315%, likely on selling by postal service, he says, adds previous low of 0.92% likely to support for now, 0.92%-0.95% band tipped tomorrow; 10-year ends down 1.3776% vs 1.3893%, 20-year down at 1.7600% vs 1.7626% in when-issued trade. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com) [ 07-29-10 0256ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100729e67t0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
