\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Likely In 9,015-9,035 Band Wed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,249 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2010\par 10:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0855 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0843 GMT misstated the dollar-rupiah's trading range in the headline. The correct version follows:) USD/IDR likely to stay in narrow 9,015-9,035 range Wednesday as market awaits further clarity on China's new policy on yuan, dealers say. "Players are also reluctant to push the dollar lower as Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's (suspected) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yesterday indicated that it doesn't want the dollar to fall below IDR9,000," dealer says; pair steady at 9,020 late but off IDR9,040 intraday high. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0851 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds tad lower after oil ministry official comment that government will meet June 25 to take decision on deregulating fuel prices, says trader with state-run bank. Adds comments by finance ministry official government sees no problem in weekly bond auctions also weighs slightly on bonds. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond at 101.43 now, 101.51 before comments, 101.58 last close. "There has only been little negative impact even though the government said it isn't considering fresh bond buy back or lowering the auction size. Also, until a fuel price hike is announced, traders will likely not (over)react," trader says. Tips benchmark 2020 bond in 101.35-101.65 range during session. India finance ministry official Tuesday said government has no plan yet on fresh bond repurchase; oil secretary S. Sundareshan said ministerial panel to meet June 25 to decide freeing fuel prices from government control. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0848 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts pare gains as some traders take profits ahead of the budget due at 1130 GMT. A London-based strategist says gilts have outperformed other safe havens in recent sessions, with the yield spread over bunds tightening by around 20bps in two weeks and some consolidation is due. "We have run-up on expectations and hopes and now's the time to see the facts," he says. September gilts up 0.17 at 119.55 and the 10-year gilt is up at 110.307 to yield 3.488%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0844 GMT [Dow Jones] The zloty is trading sideways with its performance versus core \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit by concerns over sentiment in Germany, a trader says. Trade between the range EUR/PLN4.05-to-4.10 is possible if euro zone sentiment deteriorates again, the trader says. EUR/PLN is at 4.0452 from 4.0412 late Monday, USD/PLN at 3.2844 from 3.2901. Average yield on 2-year bonds is at 4.70% from 4.68%, 5-year at 5.41% from 5.38%, 10-year at 5.85% from 5.83%. (marcin.sobczyk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0843 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR likely to stay in narrow 9,015-9,035 range Wednesday as market awaits further clarity on China's new policy on yuan, dealers say. "Players are also reluctant to push the dollar lower as Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (suspected) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday indicated that it doesn't want the dollar to fall below IDR9,000," dealer says; pair steady at 9,020 late but off IDR9,040 intraday high. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling takes a pre-budget hit with GBP/USD at the day's low of 1.4714 and EUR/GBP at the day's high of 0.8366. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Karen Jones says next resistance for the cross comes in at 0.84, next support for GBP/USD at 1.4680. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0840 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher at 3.2010 late vs 3.1865 yesterday; local dealer says MYR weaker on profit-taking after yesterday's strong gains (pair fell to 5-week low Monday). "The picture from Europe is mixed today and the problems are far from solved... investors may have realized Monday's ringgit rally was a bit overdone," dealer says. Tips pair to track USD/Asians tomorrow; 3.1900-3.2200 range expected. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0840 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan average interbank overnight rate unchanged at yesterday's 0.183%. Foreign bank trader says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again guides government-controlled banks to raise offering rate to 0.180% from 0.179% yesterday, 0.176% last week; "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to guide the rate higher gradually," she says. Still, upside for rate limited by ample liquidity in market, as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  accepts less NCDs/CDs purchases from banks recently. Tips rate to move around recent level Wednesday.(mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0836 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR forward premia edging higher despite spot rebound after yesterday's decline as investors remain cautious over possible RBI rate hike in near term, says trader with foreign bank; 1-year forward premium at 1.27 vs 1.24 late Monday. "The rally in the spot would normally push premia lower but there's some nervousness on the rate front, and that's pushing premia higher," says trader. However, sharp rise unlikely, premia should remain capped at 1.30 over next few sessions, trader adds. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0834 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD index chart suggests a 'return to the point of break-out' from its long-term downtrend which comes in at 83.85 says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . From the index's current level, that would imply EUR/USD could correct to 1.2640. \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  adds that if the index fails to hold 83.85 then the current downside correction would have risk toward the 2005 low at 80.39. The index now trades at 85.936, EUR/USD trades at 1.2310. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0832 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys are steady in London as the rally in risky assets abates. The move prompted by China saying it would allow the yuan rate to float more freely, has run out of steam. Attention shifts back to the troubles in the euro zone, with Fitch cutting \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  debt rating and S&P saying Spanish banks' bad debts could rise. The US Treasury auctions USD40B of 2-year notes Tuesday while economic releases include existing home sales data and Richmond Fed Survey both at 1400 GMT. 2-year note is a tad higher at 100-02+/32, yielding 0.705%, 10-year up at 103-05+/32, yielding 3.239%, 30-year up at 103-17/32 yielding 4.167%. September bunds up 0.21 at 127.61 and September gilts are 0.16 higher at 119.54. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0830 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds slightly firmer late as appetite for debt rises following improving sentiment, says dealer with local bank. "With less bad news from the euro zone and China allowing its yuan to appreciate, conditions are looking better," dealer says, adding with strong liquidity in the banking system people are more willing to put cash to work on bonds; tips yields to hold steady Wednesday as market awaits details of 3-year bond reopening auction (expected around 0500 GMT). Yields on May 2013 MGS down 1 bp at 3.14%, August 2015 MGS down 1 bp at 3.56% and November 2019 MGS down 4 bps at 4.02%.(elffie.chew@dowjones.com) [ 06-22-10 0455ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100622e66m0006w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
