\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: CNY Flexibility Positive To Asian Credits - MS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,547 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2010\par 03:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] China's flexibility on CNY is positive for Asian credit markets, particularly Chinese developers, banks, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  strategist Viktor Hjort. Says China will exit USD-peg, paves way for less aggressive hike of policy rate (house now tips no hike in China this year), country's loan growth could also exceed this year's quota of CNY7.5 trillion. "China property credit is highly sensitive to domestic credit growth and therefore to policy that drives credit. It's important to recognize that the actual pace of CNY appreciation matters less than the impact this policy change has on policy more broadly in China," says Hjort. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0130 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped to consolidate with downward bias as some investors may book profits on recent gains amid lack of major news from overseas. Benchmark PSEi may head towards 3300 support after rising 3.4% in last 5 sessions. Resistance pegged at 3350. "Players may take profit with very little leads from overseas," says Mon Bata, investor analyst with 2TradeAsia.com; adds decline might be limited ahead of FOMC meeting this week where prospects of yuan flexibility could be discussed. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks in broad-based rally, with China's pledge for increased CNY flexibility signalling confidence in global economic recovery, stronger Chinese purchasing power. STI +1.2% at 2868.37, with initial resistance at 2890, based on 61.8% retracement of fall to year-to-date low of 2648 from 52-week high of 3037. Market breadth at 8 gainers for every loser; commodity stocks among best performers; FTSE ST Basic Materials Index +2.5%. China plays also faring well with FTSE ST China Index +2.2%. "There's some positive reaction to the news but it's knee-jerk because it's not going to be a one-off (strong) revaluation but progressive. People will be watching how far they are going to extend the appreciation," says analyst at local brokerage. Notable gainers include Noble Group (N21.SG), +2.7% at S$1.89, Wilmar (F34.SG), +2.1% at S$5.95, \uc2 Straits Asia Resources\uc2  (AJ1.SG), +2.5% at S$2.06, Yanlord Land (Z25.SG), +4.1% at S$1.79, \uc2 Ying Li International\uc2  (5DM.SG), +3.6% at S$0.435. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0128 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.8% at 4587.7, continues to fade from 5-week high of 4622.0 after China leaves yuan reference rate unchanged, despite China's weekend comments flagging a resumption of gradual yuan appreciation. AUD/USD has fallen to 0.8774 from 0.8813 on the news. Expect S&P/ASX 200 support at last week's high of 4572.4. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0128 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI set to rise, extend 8-session winning streak (+4.7%) after China's decision to allow greater flexibility in yuan exchange rate, with China plays such as mainland banks, property, airline, domestic consumption counters expected to lead gains, says Mark To at Wing Fung Financial. Expects index to test key 20,500 level today. "Sentiment is expected to improve following the news, but as China won't let yuan appreciate too much in one time and it will be done gradually, any rise in the equity markets may not be too significant." HSI ended +0.7% at 20,286.71 Friday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0125 GMT [Dow Jones] It is hard to be convinced AUD/USD ready to resume rally, says Greg Gibbs, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS. Though he concedes he has "said that for two big figures now." Says support will certainly be firming around 0.8500/0.8700 zone. Adds, initial support will be highs on Friday at 0.8730. Topside, AUD will begin to find resistance from 0.8870-0.9000. On broader note, Gibbs says there's still plenty of room for sceptics; Chinese property market remains on recent decline; U.S. economic growth has lost momentum; European debt crisis far from resolved. Adds after good last couple of weeks for risk appetite levels no longer attractive from valuation perspective. AUD/USD now 0.8780. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR falls to lowest level since May 10 on China's policy to allow greater yuan flexibility, dealers say; pair now at 9,030 vs 9,110 late Friday, may test 9,000 later today. "More of the dollar sellers are foreign names," dealer says; adds no sign of Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow pair's slide, with dealers expecting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do so if pair falls below 9,000; 9,050 resistance tipped for today. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] Sell USD/KRW at 1178 targeting 1120 (+5.2%) with stop loss at 1220, says \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . Says post-PBOC move, stronger CNY will be positive for risk appetite, other EM Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will support China's contribution to global growth, improve competitiveness of other regional economies, making officials more comfortable with exchange rate appreciation. Notes, consistent with this view, USD/KRW has fallen to around 1178 at start of trade today, down from 1202.40 Friday. "KRW, we believe, had already been oversold in recent weeks as markets reacted to increased tensions with the North Korean regime and uncertainty about new \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations. Although there remains a risk of further belligerence from North Korea in the near-term, we have consistently been positive about the medium-term outlook for KRW based on the solid performance of the Korean economy, the likelihood of modest rate hikes later this year, and the prospect of eventual upgrades to the status of Korea's capital markets and its sovereign debt," house says. Adds, these factors suggest USD/KRW should trend lower rest of year; CNY news provides opportunity to position for medium-term KRW gains. USD/KRW last at 1181.60. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures down after government says it will normalize macroeconomic policies in "prudent, balanced" way, while BOK says it plans to roll back liquidity extended to market during global crisis, further boosting rate hike expectations, leading to flatter yield curve, traders say; 2-year monetary stabilization bond yield +5 bps at 3.79%, 3-year +6 bps at 3.78%, 5-year +2 bps at 4.43%, 10-year +2 bps at 4.93%, September futures down 9 ticks at 110.60. Sentiment "quite bad" for bonds ahead of 10-year bond auction, largely because of government's remarks, say local securities firm trader. Adds, if bonds fail to rebound from current levels, falls may accelerate. Notes, 3-year bonds particularly weak, puts cap for those yields at 3.80% (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0116 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD falls sharply to 7.7726 vs 7.7856 late Friday after PBOC's weekend announcement that it'll enhance yuan exchange rate flexibility; that's driving demand for Hong Kong dollars as freely-convertible local unit often traded as proxy for CNY, says senior trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair in 7.7680-7.7780 band. "The trading volume is quite thin in the morning, which is exaggerating the move and the pair is likely to be quite volatile today," trader says.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold consolidating early but likely to gain further when trading in Hong Kong and Tokyo gets into full swing, says Peter McGuire, managing director at Commodity Warrants Australia. Adds, news on depegging of CNY is positive for Chinese economy with consumers, importers to enjoy more purchasing power and this also supportive for gold given China is already a net gold importer. "I don't see any handbrake at the moment. We think it can hit $1,270 later today or tomorrow." McGuire says CWA tips gold at $1,350 and $1,400 sometime later this year, $1,500 by 2012 and $2,000 by 2015, although expects considerable volatility along the way. Spot gold at $1,259.30/oz, up $2.80 since Friday's NY close. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0111 GMT [Dow Jones] China's policy to allow greater yuan flexibility likely to benefit IDR as it will likely to help boost Indonesian exports to China, dealers say; "The indirect impact is that the expected USD weakening due to the yuan policy will drive the rupiah up," a dealer says; a central banker who declines to be named says China's policy won't prompt Bank Indonesia to come up with new policies after it last week introduced new controls to reduce hot money inflows. "We will see if the new (capital controls) will work as we expect or not," he adds. USD/IDR fell to 9,110 Friday, lowest level in a month. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com) [ 06-20-10 2130ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100621e66l0001j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
