er boosting rate hike expectations, leading to flatter yield curve, traders say; 2-year monetary stabilization bond yield +5 bps at 3.79%, 3-year +6 bps at 3.78%, 5-year +2 bps at 4.43%, 10-year +2 bps at 4.93%, September futures down 9 ticks at 110.60. Sentiment "quite bad" for bonds ahead of 10-year bond auction, largely because of government's remarks, say local securities firm trader. Adds, if bonds fail to rebound from current levels, falls may accelerate. Notes, 3-year bonds particularly weak, puts cap for those yields at 3.80% (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0116 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD falls sharply to 7.7726 vs 7.7856 late Friday after PBOC's weekend announcement that it'll enhance yuan exchange rate flexibility; that's driving demand for Hong Kong dollars as freely-convertible local unit often traded as proxy for CNY, says senior trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair in 7.7680-7.7780 band. "The trading volume is quite thin in the morning, which is exaggerating the move and the pair is likely to be quite volatile today," trader says.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold consolidating early but likely to gain further when trading in Hong Kong and Tokyo gets into full swing, says Peter McGuire, managing director at Commodity Warrants Australia. Adds, news on depegging of CNY is positive for Chinese economy with consumers, importers to enjoy more purchasing power and this also supportive for gold given China is already a net gold importer. "I don't see any handbrake at the moment. We think it can hit $1,270 later today or tomorrow." McGuire says CWA tips gold at $1,350 and $1,400 sometime later this year, $1,500 by 2012 and $2,000 by 2015, although expects considerable volatility along the way. Spot gold at $1,259.30/oz, up $2.80 since Friday's NY close. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0111 GMT [Dow Jones] China's policy to allow greater yuan flexibility likely to benefit IDR as it will likely to help boost Indonesian exports to China, dealers say; "The indirect impact is that the expected USD weakening due to the yuan policy will drive the rupiah up," a dealer says; a central banker who declines to be named says China's policy won't prompt Bank Indonesia to come up with new policies after it last week introduced new controls to reduce hot money inflows. "We will see if the new (capital controls) will work as we expect or not," he adds. USD/IDR fell to 9,110 Friday, lowest level in a month. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs remain slightly lower after Nikkei started above 10,000 mark (now +1.9%); but "it is hard to see a sharp rally in the share prices of advanced economies due to China's announcement" to increase flexibility in yuan, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief strategist Chotaro Morita; "this, in turn, makes it difficult to expect much of an upward push on JGB yields." 10-year cash JGB yield currently +1.0 bp at 1.205%. Rather, notes "it appears that bond markets are reacting to some extent to the strengthening message ahead of the G-20 summit (scheduled this weekend) that fiscal consolidation will take priority worldwide, as well as to the weakening of U.S. macroeconomic indicators." Says 10-year yield could fall to near 1.0% by year end. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com) [ 06-20-10 2130ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100621e66l0001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
