wer after Nikkei started above 10,000 mark (now +1.9%); but "it is hard to see a sharp rally in the share prices of advanced economies due to China's announcement" to increase flexibility in yuan, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief strategist Chotaro Morita; "this, in turn, makes it difficult to expect much of an upward push on JGB yields." 10-year cash JGB yield currently +1.0 bp at 1.205%. Rather, notes "it appears that bond markets are reacting to some extent to the strengthening message ahead of the G-20 summit (scheduled this weekend) that fiscal consolidation will take priority worldwide, as well as to the weakening of U.S. macroeconomic indicators." Says 10-year yield could fall to near 1.0% by year end. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals all up on positive sentiment from China's decision Saturday to allow managed float for USD/CNY; copper up $85 or 1.3% at $6,520/ton, aluminum up $21 or 1.1% at $1,965/ton, zinc up $38, 2.2% at $1,768/ton. Hong Kong-based trader says moves so far largely bringing complex into line with Comex copper contract, direction clearer when Chinese market opens 0100 GMT. "It's not really clear at the moment, some Chinese traders are trying to close out of long positions so we think it will pick up a little bit but not go too high." However, sees medium-term copper resistance as high as $7,000. Friday, September SHFE copper closed at CNY50,930, 7.8X LME dollar price, suggesting some potential to sell LME, buy SHFE on arbitrage trade, but now depends on outlook for USD/CNY. (david.fickling@dowjones.com) [ 06-20-10 2129ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100621e66l0001h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
