\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia Intervention \highlight\uc2  Likely Ate  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reserves May\b0\par\par\uc2 1,410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2010\par 08:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0617 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank Indonesia says its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves down to $75.6 billion as of May 31 from $78.58 billion April 30, without providing reasons; but analysts say a large chunk of drop may have been due to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amid capital outflows sparked by euro-zone debt problems; traders suspect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was in market frequently recently, as IDR under pressure, falling 1.7% vs USD in May; Bank Indonesia's acting governor Nasution said yesterday that outflows had abated.(i-made.sentana@dowjones)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0612 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 24.9% at HK$1.766 billion midday, as percentage of market total drops to 6.1% vs 7.5% at Wednesday's half-day, suggesting bears deterred by HSI's 1.8% rebound following 3-day losing streak. China Mobile (0941.HK) leads with HK$104.11 million, then \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) with HK$88.69 million. Next come \uc2 China Yurun Food\uc2  (1068.HK) with HK$83.79 million, China Life (2628.HK) with HK$79.04 million, ICBC (1398.HK) with HK$62.48 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0608 GMT [Dow Jones] Bullish move in S&P 500 futures signaling traders to get out of bonds, especially in Australia, though one senior trader says it's still a market too much on edge to do anything but trade. "Things are OK on the economic front, but who knows how long that will last? The short-term moves are still so sharp, you can only make a day trade of it. We're not seeing a lot of true investors coming to the table right now." Trader says S&P 500 futures move above its 200-day moving average, acted as catalyst for Aussie bond price drop, with 3-year off 11 ticks at 95.16. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0607 GMT [Dow Jones] The increase in Swiss retail sales in April seems to signal the end of deflationary dangers in Switzerland, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "The growth gap between nominal and real retail sales has narrowed to virtually nothing, and as such the latest report seems to confirm the end of deflationary trends just now, as hinted by virtually every other Swiss price indicator," according to economist Reto Huenerwadel. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0603 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank Indonesia keeps policy rate unchanged at 6.5%, repeating its mantra that rate consistent with 2010 inflation target of between 4%-6% and efforts to help spur economic recovery. Indonesian markets on midday break but little impact likely as decision widely expected; all 14 economists polled by Dow Jones tipped Bank Indonesia to stand pat. Comments suggest it intends to keep rates unchanged in coming months; OCBC's economist Gundy Cahyadi still expects Bank Indonesia to hike rate to 7.25% by year-end as inflation likely to gain pace. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0545 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +1.8% midday at 762.77, in tandem with healthy gains in most regional bourses. Volume heavy, suggesting buying interest strong though investors may start to take profit after recent strong rebound. "It's been a fairly good session this morning but I think this might be as far as it may rise today. Without any real catalyst in sight, I don't see the SET rising much from here in the near term," says Bualuang Securities strategist Pongrat Ratanatawanananda. Resistance pegged at 766, 50-day moving average, support at 757. Among most actives, \uc2 Thanachart Capital\uc2  (TCAP.TH) +5.0% at THB25, \uc2 Indorama Ventures\uc2  (IVL.TH) +2.3% at THB18.10, Banpu (BANPU.TH) +1.7% at THB592, Bangkok Bank (BBL.TH) +3.5% at THB118.50. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0540 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds end sharply lower, tracking robust 2.3% gain in local stocks, prompting stop-loss selling by securities houses, says local brokerage trader. "We'll have to watch stocks on Monday as further gains will push yields higher," he says. Yield on reopened 10-year bond ends at 1.4173%, highest since it started when-issued trade, vs previous close at 1.3880%; tipped to test 1.43% resistance Monday. 5-year yield ends at nearly 2-month high of 1.0237% vs 0.9950%, tipped in 1.03%-1.05% band. (alex.pevzner@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0539 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +2.3% at 7360.28 led by big rebound in electronic stocks, tracking gains in Asian bourses, strong Wall Street overnight, says Alex Huang, assistant VP at Mega Securities. Tips main index to consolidate in 7300-7450 band tomorrow, as investor confidence remains weak given sluggish trade. "Europe's fiscal troubles will continue to sideline investors in the near term," he says. Hon Hai Precision (2317.TW) settles +4.6% at NT$125.0 on comments from \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 's (AAPL) \uc2 CEO Steve Jobs\uc2  firm isn't a "sweatshop." \uc2 Chimei Innolux\uc2  (3481.TW) in Hon Hai group +4.1% at NT$35.60. \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) +3.0% at NT$30.80, Siliconware Precision (2325.TW) +3.7% at NT$35.45, Acer (2353.TW) +5.2% at NT$79.10 on technical rebounds. (crystal.hsu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0537 GMT [Dow Jones] Rallying AUD/USD still has more room to go, says Philip Burke, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Dealer for \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  in Sydney. "There seems to be some good model buying and a wide range of accounts buying Aussie today," says Burke. In rally, AUD/USD breaks to 0.8500, up from 0.8406 late yesterday, with strong US stock market helping morning gains, strong local data helping in afternoon. "It's a case of that doom and gloom slowly dissipating," says Burke, who sees resistance at 0.8550 and 0.8700, though he thinks first level won't hold. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0533 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 2.4% at 4484, hits 2-week high of 4489.1, albeit on light trading volumes. Financials, materials and energy stocks leading broad-based gains, in line with U.S. and Asian markets. AUD/USD also firm, though S&P/ASX 200 leading the rally. "It appears participants are slowly placing greater emphasis on underlying economic and company fundamentals rather than all the current background noise," says IG Markets strategist Ben Potter. "Confidence has improved to the point where traders are happy to hold positions over the course of the day rather than booking quick profits." Focus turns to tonight's U.S. private sector jobs data, followed by Friday's U.S. non-farm payrolls. Traders anxious to see if Wall Street can rise two days in a row, something it hasn't done for 23 trading sessions. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0532 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY climbs to 113.66 vs its day's low of 112.70, as "long-term investors appear to be buying on the cheap on expectations for its further gains" amid stock market rise, says Mitsubishi UFJ Trust and Banking Corp. senior trader Hideaki Inoue. Nikkei +3.2%. Adds, unit may extend gains to 114.00 later in day. "Players' sentiment points to more recovery," which may encourage Europe players to lift unit further later in day if global stock markets track Asia stocks. Attention to jobs data including today's ADP employment data, ISM Non-Manufacturing Employment Index. Adds, EUR/JPY may rise to psychological key 115.00 if closely-watched Friday's non-farm payrolls data come in higher-than-expected. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0528 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +2.1% midday at 2790.887 in moderate volume with telecommunication, bank blue chips leading gains on hopes for strong 1H earnings, traders say; tip resistance at 2810 vs 2801.203 intraday high. "Gains across Asian markets have inspired investors to buy back recently-battered shares," says trader at Lautandhana Securities; still, expects retail funds to take profit late. Among gainers, Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) +4.2% at IDR8,700, \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) +2.9% at IDR5,450, while heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) +1.9% at IDR8,000. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com) [ 06-03-10 0217ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100603e6630004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
