\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Off Low; 9,220-9,280 Range Likely\b0\par\par\uc2 1,691 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2010\par 06:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR bounces, now trading at 9,250 from 9,225 intraday low, but still tad below 9,255 late yesterday as local participants buy pair in thin trade, dealers say. "There's no sign of Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as dollar demand is not very strong," a dealer says; 9,220-9,280 still tipped as range for today.(i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Bank Indonesia likely to keep overnight policy rate unchanged at 6.5% Thursday as inflation still benign, despite having picked up in May; all 14 economists polled by Dow Jones expect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep rate at same level since August to help spur economic growth. "We think Bank Indonesia's tone will remain neutral-to-dovish this Thursday as inflation remains in the low-end of its inflation target range of 4%-6% and uncertainties about euro-zone sovereign problems weigh on the global outlook," says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  economist Johanna Chua; expects hiking to start in 1Q 2011; meanwhile some economists predict rate hike to start as early as in 3Q. ( I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0427 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD extends gains as weaker local stocks prompt investors to shift funds into U.S. unit, says senior trader at U.S. bank. Tips pair's near-term support at 7.7920, resistance at 7.8000. HSI down 0.4%. "Still, a big rise of the pair isn't likely as the upcoming IPOs might attract inflows, capping the pair at 7.8000 in the near term," trader says. Notes Agricultural Bank of China (AGBC.YY) is expected to raise up to US$30 billion in its IPO in July. Pair last at 7.7944 vs 7.7933 this morning, up from 7.7917 yesterday.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0425 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals seeing some long liquidation in Asia, with sentiment gloomy, at least in short term; 3-month nickel breaks down through $20,000 level for first time since Feb. 24, while copper off 1.2% at $6669/ton with 1,643 lots done, both contracts seeing heavy volume in past hour as EUR/USD testing 1.22 level again, now at 1.2198 vs 1.2263 intraday high. "The investor community is starting to reweight their positions on concerns over near-term Chinese demand and in the knowledge that growth in Europe is going to be anaemic. Consumers might be buying these dips though," says trader at U.S. bank. Tips initial support for nickel at $19,755, neckline of reverse head and shoulders that launched post-February rally; then $19,000. "Anything below $20,000 is in the buy zone for consumers as we are still seeing some good indications from the stainless steel sector," he says. LME nickel at $19,950/ton, down $550 with 233 lots traded. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0424 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end higher, buoyed by bargain-hunting with positive domestic economic news supporting sentiment. Benchmark PSEi ends +0.7% at 3289.56 amid heavy trading. "Geopolitical and (global) financial concerns are still there but domestic investors are in-tuned to positioning themselves into the market," says DBP-Daiwa Securities research head Ron Rodrigo; notes wide expectations by economists that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to keep policy rates on hold at meeting Thursday also boosting interest in local equities. Says market may continue to advance Thursday even as investors eye overseas developments, next resistance 3300, support 3250. \uc2 Banco De Oro Unibank\uc2  (BDO.PH) ends +4.5% at PHP46.50; Ayala Land (ALI.PH) +3.7% at PHP14.00. \uc2 Ayala Corp\uc2 . (AC.PH), down 1.5% at PHP325.00; PLDT (TEL.PH) off 0.4% at PHP3,430 limit market advance. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.8% at 9638.49, lower vs (positive) morning close of 9747.54, as enthusiasm following PM Yukio Hatoyama's resignation fades; USD/JPY, EUR/JPY slightly down vs earlier when selling in JPY seen on concerns over increased political turbulence weighed on safe-haven appeal of JPY. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has a bigger impact on Japanese stocks," says Yumi Nishimura, senior market analyst at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Says Nikkei may trade between 9600-9750 in afternoon. 27/33 Topix subindexes lower; trading houses down on falling crude; \uc2 Mitsui & Co\uc2  (8031.TO) off 7.3% at Y1,179, hit by concerns about costs stemming from massive oil spill in Gulf of Mexico. \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) off 2.3% at Y2,723. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0413 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds now mostly flat after weak opening as market anxious over likely RBI rate hike after comments from India PM Manmohan Singh that government will continue to take all steps necessary to ease price pressures. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond at 101.94 vs 101.98 last close, second-most traded 8.20% 2022 bond at 102.70 vs 102.82 last close. "The market is in a wait-and watch mode after comments from the Prime Minister and RBI Governor too yesterday that inflation is still higher than it should be," trader says. Tips benchmark 2020 bond in 101.70-102.10 range, 2022 bond in 102.60-102.90 range during session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0413 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan political turmoil in wake of PM Hatoyama's resignation to continue weighing on JPY in coming weeks ahead of Upper House elections (likely July 11) as uncertainty prompts foreign investors, traders to sell yen, says Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in Japan. "The uncertainty until the decision about the next prime minister and finance minister, and even after the new Cabinet is formed, the uncertainty about the election should be a negative for the yen." But says even if current finance minister Kan, seen as less tolerant of JPY strength, becomes next PM as many expect, does not necessarily mean major change in government \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. "Kan obviously prefers a weaker yen, but so far the Keidanren (Japan Business Federation) and other business lobbyists are not pushing strongly for government action against yen strength." Says whoever becomes next PM, finance minister, key possible triggers for government verbal, actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be sharper falls in country's share markets, with many watching Nikkei for any breach of 9,000, and more pressure from business community. On EUR/JPY, says euro zone's own political problems likely to temper any gains in cross; German President Kohler resigned Monday, increasing political concerns in key euro-zone member. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0410 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +0.4% at 510.74 after Prime Minister Nguyen Tan Dung yesterday told \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to urge commercial banks to further lower rates to support economic growth. Dung said Vietnam sticking to GDP growth target of 6.5% for 2010. "Firms are expected to post better results for the second half of the year with banks lowering their interest rates," says trader. Notes foreign and long-term investors buying in blue chips. Volume low with 40.7 million shares valued at VND1.3 trillion traded. Among actives, \uc2 Ocean Group JSC\uc2  (OGC.VH) +0.3% at VND32,300, \uc2 Saigon Thuong Tin Commercial Joint Stock Bank\uc2  (STB.VH) down 0.9% at VND20,300, Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) down 0.8% at VND37,000. Tips index to stay around 510 for rest of week. (trong-khanh.vu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD falls to 32.294 midday vs 32.418 yesterday, trading in tight 32.250-32.300 band in most of morning session as market lacks cues with Taiex choppy, Korea markets closed, says local bank trader. Volume moderate at US$496 million midday; "traders are cautious over recent global uncertainties" such as euro zone debt problems, he says. Tips pair in 32.250-32.310 band in afternoon. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0401 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +0.4% at 16,644.07, just off 16,678.09 early high, aided by some short-covering after a 2.2% sell-off yesterday; 22 of the 30 index issues up, with rise led by auto shares after strong May vehicle sales numbers. "The positive opening is on expected lines. There is some short-covering in sectors that were beaten down yesterday like metals and select infrastructure plays," says dealer at domestic fund house. Adds, Sensex could trade range bound for session, with upside capped at 16,700. News flow from Europe, China could provide direction later. Among auto plays, \uc2 Mahindra & Mahindra\uc2  (500520.BY) +2.0% at INR575.00, Maruti (532500.BY) +1.8% at INR1,283. \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) +1.3% at INR735. Among metal plays, \uc2 Sterlite Industries\uc2  (500900.BY) +1.1% at INR636.30. \uc2 Infosys (500209.BY) \uc2 +0.9 at INR2,648.00, Larsen (500510.BY) +0.8% at INR1,605.50. But, \uc2 Tata Power\uc2  (500400.BY) down 1.4% at INR1,242. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares down 0.6% at 3038.08 in thin volume, following trend of offshore markets, says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Grant Williamson; "once again we are lacking any domestic news or any stock specific news. It's all extremely quiet." \uc2 Fisher & Paykel Appliances\uc2  (FPA.NZ) down 1.8% at NZ$0.55; "investors are still a little bit uncertain regarding \uc2 Fisher & Paykel Appliances\uc2  and would like to see proof of a turnaround in demand for their products and earnings." Telecom (TEL.NZ) +0.5% at NZ$1.88 after touching fresh all-time low of NZ$1.85 earlier in session; "It's just bouncing off that low." Says dearth of local drivers mean "all eyes will be on international markets once again." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com) [ 06-02-10 0041ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100602e6620003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
