\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesia Weighs Subsidy Move,May Stoke Inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 1,532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2010\par 03:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0141 GMT [Dow Jones] If Indonesia puts into affect ban on motorcycles consuming subsidized gasoline, which country is considering, would definitely to help stoke inflation, although impact likely be moderate, analysts say. Move being considered to reduce subsidy costs. "It's being studied, and we must get approval from the parliament before we can implement it," Evita Legowo, director-general of oil and gas at the Energy and Mineral Resources Ministry, tells Dow Jones Newswires today; adds if approved, could implement policy as early as August. Still unclear if government would put plan into effect considering rejections from parliament, outcry from public over various options to reduce subsidy aired by government in recent years. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0138 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD down at 32.219 vs 32.345 late yesterday on exporter selling above 32.200, tracking losses in USD vs Asians, says local bank trader. Tips pair in 32.170-32.300 band rest of morning. Notes "though local shares are slightly lower now, it seems like the Taiex may be headed for consolidation today and regional bourses also seem more stable," helping to add marginally to investor confidence. Expects trading volume to be smaller today as investors on sidelines waiting for further cues. (charmian.kok@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks pull back slightly on Wall Street's decline, reluctance to buy with long weekend ahead. STI off 0.3% at 2687.06, with immediate support at year-to-date low of 2648 set earlier this week. Decliners outnumbering gainers by 2 to 1 in broad market, with most FTSE ST sub-indexes down. "Investors are unlikely to take on fresh positions," says NRA Capital. CIMB says headwinds inevitable in near term as investors keep tight watch on potential financial-market instability; "nevertheless, mid-term, our base case remains that European problems are unlikely to derail the global growth recovery and Asia's growth trajectory. With valuations undemanding and a cyclical recovery in place, we expect further market upside in 2H10." Among blue chips, notable decliners include yesterday's top performers; NOL (N03.SG) off 2.2% at S$1.76, SembMarine (S51.SG) off 1.6% at S$3.78. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD bounces back as short-term players buy, could rise as high as 1.2350 by end of global day "if no more bad news comes out" on euro-zone debt, financial market problems or on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  EUR-selling, says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major Japanese bank. Says after EUR/USD failed to break 4-year low hit last week at 1.2143, speculators in Asia bought pair back. Any breach of 1.2143 would expose 1.2133, half-way point between pair's all-time low, all-time high. "People are rather nervous about that half-way point, so there was enough relief from it holding above there to prompt some euro-buying this morning," trader says. But says EUR/USD outlook remains bleak for foreseeable future as "there are absolutely no reasons for macro-focused hedge funds and other investors who trade on economic fundamentals to buy the euro now."(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD continues to bounce back after sharp overnight fall, now at 7.7875 vs low of 7.7695 last night, though still below 7.8008 late yesterday. Brokers at U.K. bank expect HKD to remain on stronger side today, with pair to be capped at 7.7950 amid inflows related to upcoming IPOs, safe haven status of HK. They note market still looking at reasons into USD/HKD's major move last night, which partly due to major US$2 billion trade overnight by a German bank to sell USD, buy HKD, as well as several subsequent trades by other names to buy HKD, pushing pair even lower, with lack of market liquidity during overnight hours making moves more pronounced. They added there were no major news or underlying developments that triggered the HKD fluctuation. (jeffrey.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0132 GMT [Dow Jones] A Chinese fire sale of EUR holdings looks unlikely despite Financial Times report that China's SAFE met with foreign banks to discuss issue of European crisis and expressed concerns about Greece, Ireland, Italy, Portugal and Spain, Credit Agricole says. "Such news should not be exaggerated. We don't expect China to sell massively its European holdings, not only because of a lack of alternative, but also because it may become interesting to buy back EUR at some point in the future." Still, reckons China may sharpen focus going forward, aiming for top-quality European names, and be more reluctant to invest in lower-rated countries. Also says market reaction "suggests that the recent consolidation is fragile, and that both the market rebound and the EUR are still vulnerable to possible bad news emanating from Europe." EUR/USD last at 1.2212 vs 1.2193 late in NY trade. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures steady as investors trade cautiously due to increased volatility in local bond market of late, market participants say. "The market seems to be finding it hard to stabilize... while the longer-end bonds remain relatively weak," says local securities firm trader. Expects yield curve to steepen; short-term notes like monetary stabilization bonds, 3-year government bonds likely strong as rate hike concerns recede while bonds of 5-year tenor or longer expected to be weak. But "investors with either a long or a short position in bonds seem to have become increasingly risk-sensitive," leading to choppy trade. 3-year yield flat at 3.61%, tipped in wide 3.54%-3.68% range. 5-year yield +1 bp at 4.38%, June futures +2 ticks at 111.50. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR edges higher to 9,350 vs 9,320 late yesterday as U.S. stocks' slide overnight drove USD higher vs major rivals, dealers say; most deals so far between local participants, but foreigners also expected to buy pair later today. Pair tipped in 9,325-9,380 range, likely to trade closer to upper end of range, with players cautious on possible Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if 9,400 region revisited. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB tad up at 32.55-32.56 vs 32.53 late yesterday in thin trade, with portfolio outflows following recent sharp falls in local shares supportive. "The baht in the near term is unlikely to turn significantly stronger as investors are still concerned over euro-zone's financial health," says Bangkok-based dealer; adds as such they're in risk-aversion mode. Notes BOT yesterday says 70%-80% of THB50.94 billion net selling by foreign investors so far in May has already left country. Pair tipped in 32.50-32.60 band. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may open lower, tracking Wall Street's decline; Conita Hung at Delta Asia thinks index may test psychological 19,000 as sentiment remains bearish due to lingering euro-zone credit concerns. "It's hard to expect a turnaround in trading sentiment, as euro-zone credit concerns are not expected to be cleared in the near term." Says HSI not yet bottomed out, reckons benchmark index likely to fall below its year-to-date trough of 18,974 near-term. Expects more volatility ahead of Friday's expiry of May futures. Companies with European exposure, such as \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK), Esprit (0330.HK), likely to remain weak. HSI ends +1.1% at 19,196.45 Wednesday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP may fall as EUR, most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  recover some ground vs greenback early in Asian trade. Still, market likely to be volatile, PHP's rise should be limited. Pair may slide to 46.60 vs 46.71 late Wednesday; support eyed at 46.50, resistance at 47.00. Manila-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader says USD-demand not substantial but should be enough to temper PHP's gain. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to rise following EUR's sharp decline overnight; USD/CNY tipped to retest 6.8300 vs 6.8291 last close though central parity likely in range, expected to be set at 6.8281 vs 6.8280 yesterday as PBOC aims to keep CNY steady. Shanghai-based trader at local bank says traders likely to keep close eye on PBOC as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may support CNY either in automatic price matching system or spot market if pair rises sharply. He says USD/CNY ended lower Wednesday as large state-owned bank sold (USD) heavily late. Still, "it's difficult to say whether the bank did that on behalf of their corporate clients or the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," trader says. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com) [ 05-26-10 2141ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100527e65r0001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
