\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Up; Bank Indonesia May Move Around 9,400\b0\par\par\uc2 1,526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2010\par 03:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR edges higher to 9,350 vs 9,320 late yesterday as U.S. stocks' slide overnight drove USD higher vs major rivals, dealers say; most deals so far between local participants, but foreigners also expected to buy pair later today. Pair tipped in 9,325-9,380 range, likely to trade closer to upper end of range, with players cautious on possible Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if 9,400 region revisited. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB tad up at 32.55-32.56 vs 32.53 late yesterday in thin trade, with portfolio outflows following recent sharp falls in local shares supportive. "The baht in the near term is unlikely to turn significantly stronger as investors are still concerned over euro-zone's financial health," says Bangkok-based dealer; adds as such they're in risk-aversion mode. Notes BOT yesterday says 70%-80% of THB50.94 billion net selling by foreign investors so far in May has already left country. Pair tipped in 32.50-32.60 band. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may open lower, tracking Wall Street's decline; Conita Hung at Delta Asia thinks index may test psychological 19,000 as sentiment remains bearish due to lingering euro-zone credit concerns. "It's hard to expect a turnaround in trading sentiment, as euro-zone credit concerns are not expected to be cleared in the near term." Says HSI not yet bottomed out, reckons benchmark index likely to fall below its year-to-date trough of 18,974 near-term. Expects more volatility ahead of Friday's expiry of May futures. Companies with European exposure, such as \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK), Esprit (0330.HK), likely to remain weak. HSI ends +1.1% at 19,196.45 Wednesday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP may fall as EUR, most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  recover some ground vs greenback early in Asian trade. Still, market likely to be volatile, PHP's rise should be limited. Pair may slide to 46.60 vs 46.71 late Wednesday; support eyed at 46.50, resistance at 47.00. Manila-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader says USD-demand not substantial but should be enough to temper PHP's gain. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to rise following EUR's sharp decline overnight; USD/CNY tipped to retest 6.8300 vs 6.8291 last close though central parity likely in range, expected to be set at 6.8281 vs 6.8280 yesterday as PBOC aims to keep CNY steady. Shanghai-based trader at local bank says traders likely to keep close eye on PBOC as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may support CNY either in automatic price matching system or spot market if pair rises sharply. He says USD/CNY ended lower Wednesday as large state-owned bank sold (USD) heavily late. Still, "it's difficult to say whether the bank did that on behalf of their corporate clients or the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," trader says. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield rising as players selling cash bonds with JPY slightly weakening vs USD, EUR, pace of declines in Tokyo shares slowing (Nikkei now at 9464.22 vs session low 9395.29), says trader at Tokyo securities house. But adds "financial markets are very volatile now, so once Tokyo or Asian stocks start to fall and the euro drops sharply against the yen, demand for JGBs will likely grow again," driving yields downward. 10-year JGB yield now up 1 bp at 1.215%, may hover in 1.200%-1.220% band. Lead June JGB futures at 140.69, down 0.13. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares likely to open lower, tracking weakness in U.S. stocks. Shanghai Composite Index may test immediate support at 2600, after ending +0.1% at 2625.79 yesterday. "Sentiment is still fragile given the headwinds from euro zone's economic uncertainties and domestic tightening measures, but there seems to be a glimmer of hope of a rebound given some consolidation activity recently in the Shanghai market," says Zhang Qi from Haitong Securities. Shenzhen Composite Index ends down 0.1% at 1039.45. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD recovers to 7.7828 after being markedly down overnight to a low of 7.7695 vs 7.8008 late yesterday. Trader at European bank says sharp swing prompted by big trade from German bank to sell up to US$2 billion in USD to buy HKD, though notes such sharp move in USD/HKD not entirely surprising given relatively thin volumes. USD/HKD still trading safely within the trading band set between 7.7500-7.8500 to maintain HKD's peg to the USD, so was no need for HKMA to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , unlike in earlier months when USD/HKD was hovering at 7.7500 amid weakness in USD, while heavy inflows boosted HKD value. Pair last fell below 7.7700 May 6, when it hit low of 7.7655. (jeffrey.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds may fall, tracking losses in U.S. Treasurys overnight, says foreign bank trader. Heavy selling pressure in 5-year bond likely to continue as investors sell note to buy 10-year reopened bond with better liquidity, may weigh on overall market. Tips key 10-year yield in 1.3400%-1.3650% band vs 1.3503% last close; 5-year tipped 0.9600%-0.9800% vs 0.9677%. "We may continue to see some profit taking by banks and securities houses as bonds have recently had a good run, helped by worries over euro zone's debt problems and tensions in Korea," he says. (charmian.kok@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.6% at 4333.3 after hitting 4336.5 as materials lead a fairly broad-based uptick in early trading. BHP (BHP.AU) up 1.6%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) up 1.1% after rising 5.3% and 7.3% in London. Banks are mixed, with ANZ (ANZ.AU) down 0.2%, CBA (CBA.AU) down 0.4% after rising yesterday. \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) up 1.2%, NAB (NAB.AU) up 1.2% after falling yesterday. Traders playing down overnight reports that China may review its European investments. "I think these reports should be put in context," says Macquarie Private Wealth Division Director Martin Lakos. "I'm sure China would review its foreign reserve holdings all the time and it's not in their interest to hurt those investments. Don't forget there was a fair bit of positive momentum in Europe last night and Wall Street was strong until the last hour of trading. Equity market are oversold and it wouldn't surprise if they are trying to form a base around these levels." Ausenco (AAX.AU) down 14% on profit warning. Tower (TAL.AU) down 14% ahead of capital raising. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY recovers some ground as Japanese institutional players, importers buy after cross rate fell close to 9-year low overnight, senior trader at major Tokyo bank says. EUR/USD also tad higher, last at 1.2188. Still, thinks gains merely technical rebound; "the upside in the euro will likely be short-lived due to ongoing euro-zone debt problems and sluggish Tokyo stocks." Nikkei last down 0.9%. EUR/JPY at 109.78 vs early low of 109.20; tips resistance at 110.50. EUR/USD's upside likely limited at 1.2250. USD/JPY slightly higher at 90.02 vs 89.82 early but may not rise above 90.30. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to consolidate in tight range, but biased upward, on gains on European bourses overnight, says Randy Chang, trader at KGI Securities. Tipped in 7100-7200 band today vs yesterday's +1.1% close at 7167.35. Notes losses in some regional markets may weigh, but suspects Taiwan government funds may step into market to keep index from sharp losses. Says while players to consider Wall Street declines, unlikely big factor as declines moderate. Adds, Finance minister Lee Sush-Der says Taiwan could activate National Stabilization Fund to support stocks if Korean crisis turns into war, also to lend confidence. "Foreign investors have been selling less Taiwan shares recently, which may indicate a slow return to confidence," he says. Eyes electronics stocks, Hon Hai Precision (2317.TW) after China's state media reported another employee fell to death Wednesday. Tips China plays such as Ruentex Development (9945.TW) may outperform after China's Shanghai Securities News reported yesterday insurance regulator will allow domestic insurers to invest in all HK-listed main board shares; news positive for Chinese firms. (charmian.kok@dowjones.com) [ 05-26-10 2126ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100527e65r00013\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
