\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:USD/CNY NDFs Extend Gains;6.7700 Likely Resistance\b0\par\par\uc2 1,551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2010\par 07:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0543 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY NDFs offshore extend gains on USD general strength vs major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; 1-year USD/CNY NDFs at 6.7560/6.7619 vs 6.7170/6.7220 late yesterday. Still, upside limited on lingering hopes China may allow CNY appreciation vs USD this year, says Hong Kong-based trader at Asian bank. "My personal feeling is that the one-year NDFs will be very toppish around 6.7700," trader says. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0537 GMT [Dow Jones] For now, probably best to simply get out of way of rallying Aussie bond market, wait for another time to try a little profit-taking, says Stephen Nash, head of strategy for FIIG. Korean concerns helped lift bond prices today, with 3-years up 10 ticks at 95.32, and though those concerns could dissipate easily, risk-averse market sentiment won't. "We are in this period of assessing just how bad the European growth outlook will be. That's going to take some time and during this period, any bad news will be featured," says Nash. "Not to mention, in terms of the RBA, the case for tightening has simply gone away for the time being." (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0535 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares down 2.1% at 728.97 midday in active trade, taking cue from regional slide following concerns over health of euro zone. Rebound after recent steep fall may emerge intrasession but unlikely to be strong enough to reverse benchmark to positive close, says Phillip Securities' Assistant VP Teerada Chanyingyong. Adds political situation still on radar as end of protests still leave consequent problems. Support put at 724, resistance at 742. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) down 2.5% at THB236, Banpu (BANPU.TH) down 4.1% at THB564, PTT Aromatics & Refining (PTTAR.TH) down 3.6% at THB26.50, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) down 6.0% at THB90.50. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] China's move to hike investments in Syria masks challenges it is facing operating there. State-owned \uc2 China Petrochemical Corp\uc2 . (0386.HK) has struggled to maintain output from 2 Syrian fields acquired in $2 billion takeover of Tanganyika Oil in 2008. Fields produce heavy oil that is expensive to produce, difficult to refine. \uc2 Royal Dutch Shell PLC\uc2  (RDSB) said in April it was struggling to keep output from its fields in Syria's Dier-Ez-Zor area above 100,000 bbl this year. Last week, Shell sold 35% stake in Syrian oil unit to \uc2 China National Petroleum Corp\uc2 . But there is broader logic to China's Syrian investments, especially boost to its influence in Middle East, key strategic area. See "=CHINA ENERGY WATCH: Syria's Appeal Holds Despite Output Fall" (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0519 GMT [Dow Jones] Aluminum contango financing deals which allow speculators, market participants to make money by storing metals and profiting from difference between cash and futures price likely to persist, says Adrian Lismore, deputy head of commodities marketing at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "Some of these deals are now being rolled over," he says. Aluminum financing has kept estimated 75% of global aluminum inventories off the market, supporting prices since 2008 financial crisis. Some market participants fear price of aluminum could fall below cost of production if interest rates rise or market flips into backwardation for some reason, which could cause deals to unravel, pushing glut of supply into market. LME 3-month aluminum at $2,051/ton, down $33 with 621 lots traded so far. (james.campbe