\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Aussie Bond Rally Symptom Of Larger Issue - FIIG\b0\par\par\uc2 1,627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2010\par 07:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0537 GMT [Dow Jones] For now, probably best to simply get out of way of rallying Aussie bond market, wait for another time to try a little profit-taking, says Stephen Nash, head of strategy for FIIG. Korean concerns helped lift bond prices today, with 3-years up 10 ticks at 95.32, and though those concerns could dissipate easily, risk-averse market sentiment won't. "We are in this period of assessing just how bad the European growth outlook will be. That's going to take some time and during this period, any bad news will be featured," says Nash. "Not to mention, in terms of the RBA, the case for tightening has simply gone away for the time being." (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0535 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares down 2.1% at 728.97 midday in active trade, taking cue from regional slide following concerns over health of euro zone. Rebound after recent steep fall may emerge intrasession but unlikely to be strong enough to reverse benchmark to positive close, says Phillip Securities' Assistant VP Teerada Chanyingyong. Adds political situation still on radar as end of protests still leave consequent problems. Support put at 724, resistance at 742. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) down 2.5% at THB236, Banpu (BANPU.TH) down 4.1% at THB564, PTT Aromatics & Refining (PTTAR.TH) down 3.6% at THB26.50, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) down 6.0% at THB90.50. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] China's move to hike investments in Syria masks challenges it is facing operating there. State-owned \uc2 China Petrochemical Corp\uc2 . (0386.HK) has struggled to maintain output from 2 Syrian fields acquired in $2 billion takeover of Tanganyika Oil in 2008. Fields produce heavy oil that is expensive to produce, difficult to refine. \uc2 Royal Dutch Shell PLC\uc2  (RDSB) said in April it was struggling to keep output from its fields in Syria's Dier-Ez-Zor area above 100,000 bbl this year. Last week, Shell sold 35% stake in Syrian oil unit to \uc2 China National Petroleum Corp\uc2 . But there is broader logic to China's Syrian investments, especially boost to its influence in Middle East, key strategic area. See "=CHINA ENERGY WATCH: Syria's Appeal Holds Despite Output Fall" (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0519 GMT [Dow Jones] Aluminum contango financing deals which allow speculators, market participants to make money by storing metals and profiting from difference between cash and futures price likely to persist, says Adrian Lismore, deputy head of commodities marketing at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "Some of these deals are now being rolled over," he says. Aluminum financing has kept estimated 75% of global aluminum inventories off the market, supporting prices since 2008 financial crisis. Some market participants fear price of aluminum could fall below cost of production if interest rates rise or market flips into backwardation for some reason, which could cause deals to unravel, pushing glut of supply into market. LME 3-month aluminum at $2,051/ton, down $33 with 621 lots traded so far. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0519 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 2.4% at 4288.8, hits 2-day low of 4282.8, albeit on moderate volume. Index remains under pressure as offshore falls worsen, with Asian markets down 2%-3%, S&P 500 futures down 1.6% and AUD/USD down 0.9%. Markets are reacting negatively to tightening credit conditions, due to the European financial crisis, with 3-month Libor's rise to 10-month high causing widespread concern. "The markets are clearly very worried about credit conditions," says Patersons senior private client adviser Peter Morgan. "Company valuations are very attractive but a credit crunch can be diabolical and the fear factor is massive at the moment." Morgan says margin calls will be back on the agenda tomorrow if the S&P/ASX 200 closes below 4300.0 and Wall Street has another bad night. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0514 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares down 2.8% midday at 2536.547 in moderate volume with blue chips leading losses amid renewed worries over Europe's debt problems; support at 2530 vs 2532.331 intraday low, traders say. "Sentiment is very weak and many investors are worried because they think the bearish trend will continue for the rest of the month," says trader at Lautandhana Securities. Among decliners: gas and oil company Medco (MEDC.JK) down 6.5% at IDR2,500, car maker Astra (ASII.JK) down 3.6% at IDR36,550, Coal miner Bumi (BUMI.JK) down 2.2% at IDR1,740. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0506 GMT [Dow Jones] IDR showing unusual resilience in face of latest risk scare, will likely outperform other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  thanks to solid outlook for local economy, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that should limit any downside. USD/IDR down 0.7% YTD even as USD has made small gains vs most other Asians, which have been hit by concerns about economic fallout from Europe's debt crisis. Many analysts forecast USD may drop well below IDR9,000 before December vs IDR9,350 last. This reflects stark improvement in Indonesia's status among foreign investors, who sold IDR furiously in previous crises. Country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves +39% over last 12 months; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been using that war chest to support IDR, including suspected $20 million \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday. Another key to IDR's resilience is \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's low exposure to euro zone-bound trade, as global markets fret over possible loss of demand due to unfolding sovereign debt crisis there. See: "=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Indonesian Rupiah May Remain Solid Despite Risk Aversion." (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  keeps March 2011 forecast for 10-year bond yield unchanged at 8.25% (currently 7.37%) despite sharp fall in yields as "inflation continues to trend above estimates (10% WPI, 15% CPI) and bond issues are still sizeable." Says, easing yields imply 3%-7% upside risk (assuming no loss/gain) to FY11 earnings forecasts for government banks with \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  (500112.BY), Bank of India (532149.BY) being most rate sensitive. Notes, easing yields may boost near-term (1QFY11) earnings for government banks. "However, the big positive would be if banks, particularly SBI, was to use these gains to raise their non-performing loan coverage." House maintains marketweight on Indian banking sector with \uc2 Axis Bank\uc2  (532215.BY), \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2  (500180.BY), \uc2 Bank of Baroda\uc2  (532134.BY) , Union Bank (532477.BY) and \uc2 Punjab National Bank\uc2  (532461.BY) as preferred plays. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0453 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs higher as intensifying European fiscal issues and tension in Korean Peninsula propel investors to move their assets to safe-haven bonds, says RuiXue Xu, fixed-income strategist at RBS Securities. Adds Japanese institutional investors still eager to buy bonds further, meaning JGBs should gain more. 10-year yield last at 1.220%, down 3bps; Xu says it may fall below 1.20% near term. June futures last at 140.51, up 0.26. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0452 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end lower, tracking sharp losses in regional markets amid mounting concerns on Europe's fiscal health. Benchmark PSEi down 2.8% to 3-month low of 3102.59 in heavy volume; all sub-indices registered declines, led by services and holding firms. "This is a knee-jerk reaction to what's happening abroad but it's quite possible we could have a rally after this," says April Tan, \uc2 CitisecOnline.com\uc2  research head; notes benchmark index down 6.8% since last week; resistance eyed at 3200, psychological support at 3000. Among losers, \uc2 Ayala Corp\uc2 . (AC.PH) off 1.5% at PHP320.00, SM Investments (SM.PH) off 4.5% at PHP372.50, PLDT (TEL.PH) off 3.9% at PHP2,360. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW still trading sharply higher at 1,264.1 vs 1,214.5 late yesterday in Seoul on heightening tension between two Korea's after reports North Korean government last week ordered its citizens, troops to be ready for combat. Traders note further upside capped around current levels for now following suspected USD selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , largely between KRW1,265-KRW1,272. "(I) think they (BOK) are continuously (selling the dollar) little by little...but I don't expect the dollar to pull-back much," from here given weak risk appetite, says local bank trader. Another trader tips pair in 1,260-1,272 range for rest of day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 1.4% at 1256.05 midday, extending losing streak to 8th consecutive day to reach lowest level since mid-February; worries over euro-zone debt further compounded by falls in regional markets amid Korean tensions. Dealers say KLCI's fall below 200-day moving average support at 1266 also point to bearish outlook for market in near-term; "A further retracement towards the immediate technical support level of 1250 appears imminent," RHB Research says; adds, investors likely to continue to shun stocks "to avoid catching a falling knife." Most blue chips down, with CIMB (1023.KU) down 2.5% at MYR6.65, Maybank (1155.KU) down 2.1% at MYR7.10, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2  (1961.KU) down 2.0% at MYR4.78; market debutant Sarawak Cable (5170.KU) +2.9% at 72 sen, well of earlier high of 77.5 sen as profit-taking sets in. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com) [ 05-25-10 0137ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100525e65p00044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
