5.BY), \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2  (500180.BY), \uc2 Bank of Baroda\uc2  (532134.BY) , Union Bank (532477.BY) and \uc2 Punjab National Bank\uc2  (532461.BY) as preferred plays. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0453 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs higher as intensifying European fiscal issues and tension in Korean Peninsula propel investors to move their assets to safe-haven bonds, says RuiXue Xu, fixed-income strategist at RBS Securities. Adds Japanese institutional investors still eager to buy bonds further, meaning JGBs should gain more. 10-year yield last at 1.220%, down 3bps; Xu says it may fall below 1.20% near term. June futures last at 140.51, up 0.26. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0452 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end lower, tracking sharp losses in regional markets amid mounting concerns on Europe's fiscal health. Benchmark PSEi down 2.8% to 3-month low of 3102.59 in heavy volume; all sub-indices registered declines, led by services and holding firms. "This is a knee-jerk reaction to what's happening abroad but it's quite possible we could have a rally after this," says April Tan, \uc2 CitisecOnline.com\uc2  research head; notes benchmark index down 6.8% since last week; resistance eyed at 3200, psychological support at 3000. Among losers, \uc2 Ayala Corp\uc2 . (AC.PH) off 1.5% at PHP320.00, SM Investments (SM.PH) off 4.5% at PHP372.50, PLDT (TEL.PH) off 3.9% at PHP2,360. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW still trading sharply higher at 1,264.1 vs 1,214.5 late yesterday in Seoul on heightening tension between two Korea's after reports North Korean government last week ordered its citizens, troops to be ready for combat. Traders note further upside capped around current levels for now following suspected USD selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , largely between KRW1,265-KRW1,272. "(I) think they (BOK) are continuously (selling the dollar) little by little...but I don't expect the dollar to pull-back much," from here given weak risk appetite, says local bank trader. Another trader tips pair in 1,260-1,272 range for rest of day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 1.4% at 1256.05 midday, extending losing streak to 8th consecutive day to reach lowest level since mid-February; worries over euro-zone debt further compounded by falls in regional markets amid Korean tensions. Dealers say KLCI's fall below 200-day moving average support at 1266 also point to bearish outlook for market in near-term; "A further retracement towards the immediate technical support level of 1250 appears imminent," RHB Research says; adds, investors likely to continue to shun stocks "to avoid catching a falling knife." Most blue chips down, with CIMB (1023.KU) down 2.5% at MYR6.65, Maybank (1155.KU) down 2.1% at MYR7.10, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2  (1961.KU) down 2.0% at MYR4.78; market debutant Sarawak Cable (5170.KU) +2.9% at 72 sen, well of earlier high of 77.5 sen as profit-taking sets in. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 2.4% at 19,193.70 midday, tracking U.S. stocks' fall, while geopolitical concerns also weigh on regional markets after Yonhap news agency reports North Korean leader Kim Jong-il ordered military to be ready for combat. Volume uninspiring at HK$33.32 billion. \uc2 Celestial Securities\uc2 ' Eugene Law says rotational selling prevails, with today's selling pressure especially heavy on drug stocks; Sinopharm (1099.HK) slumps 8.2% to HK$26.70, \uc2 Sino Biopharmaceutical\uc2  (1177.HK) drops 11.2% to HK$2.94. Law tips HSI "most likely" will breach 19,000 short term, with index not trading below 19,000 since July 2009. Foxconn (2038.HK) worst-performing blue chip, tumbles 9.9% to HK$5.30, likely in part weighed by Xinhua news agency report an employee died Tuesday after falling from building at Shenzhen plant. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0443 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end flat at 486.25, after moving between 479.61 to 489.59 in volatile trade; foreigners' then locals' bargain-hunting helped support after key index fell nearly 12% month-to-date. "Though many global markets fell as a result of the euro-zone's debt issue, buying appetite in the local market remains relatively strong today because many stocks have fallen significantly recently," says Hanoi-based trader. Volume moderate with 44 million shares, valued at VND1.36 billion, traded. Among actives, Vneco (VNE.VH) ends +4.5% at VND18,500, Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) ends down 0.5% at VND36,900, Itaco (ITA.VH) ends +3.3% at VND34,200. Traders tip buying sentiment to remain strong tomorrow with key index to test 490. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0443 GMT [Dow Jones] Current selloff in palladium, platinum driven by fears of repeat of September 2008 crash, when metals lost two-thirds of value, according to Tokyo-based trader. Palladium down $11, 2.2% at $433/oz, platinum down $21, 1.5%, at $1,503/oz, having dipped below $1,500/oz. "I feel it's a bit speculative at the moment. Corporates are holding a lot more cash now than after Lehman," reducing the risk of flight to cash which drove 2008 drops. Argues worst of last week's falls, which saw platinum lose 12%, drop to $1,452/oz while palladium lost 17%, touched $398/oz, aided by absence of many PGM traders who were at annual Platinum Week dinner in London. Sees gold possibly retesting $1,200/oz level briefly, but dollar strength should limit rises; gold at $1,192.52/oz, only riser among precious metals. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares not spared Asia-wide selloff. STI down 2.0% at 2670.56 midday, barely above year-to-date low of 2665 set in February. If 2665 fails to hold, next support at 2605 (minor low in October). Market breadth at 9 decliners for every gainer. "The downtrend is still intact because risk aversion factors are still in place," says Phillip Securities analyst Phua Ming-Weii; "Libor-OIS (overnight index swap) spread remains high and the uptrend in bonds is still intact. Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also continue to sell off. It is unlikely that equities rally with these (conditions) present." New listing Prudential (K6S.SG) fails to whet appetite, last at US$7.54 vs US$7.72 opening price, which tad higher than London's last closing price of GBP5.30, equivalent to US$7.65. Key decliners among blue chips include \uc2 Jardine Cycle & Carriage\uc2  (C07.SG), off 5.0% at S$25.64, NOL (N03.SG), off 4.2% at S$1.81, \uc2 Sembcorp Industries\uc2  (U96.SG), off 4.2% at S$3.87. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com) [ 05-25-10 0119ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100525e65p00040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
