\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: IDR May Stay Solid Despite Risk Aversion - \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA\b0\par\par\uc2 1,655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2010\par 07:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0506 GMT [Dow Jones] IDR showing unusual resilience in face of latest risk scare, will likely outperform other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  thanks to solid outlook for local economy, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that should limit any downside. USD/IDR down 0.7% YTD even as USD has made small gains vs most other Asians, which have been hit by concerns about economic fallout from Europe's debt crisis. Many analysts forecast USD may drop well below IDR9,000 before December vs IDR9,350 last. This reflects stark improvement in Indonesia's status among foreign investors, who sold IDR furiously in previous crises. Country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves +39% over last 12 months; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been using that war chest to support IDR, including suspected $20 million \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday. Another key to IDR's resilience is \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's low exposure to euro zone-bound trade, as global markets fret over possible loss of demand due to unfolding sovereign debt crisis there. See: "=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Indonesian Rupiah May Remain Solid Despite Risk Aversion." (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  keeps March 2011 forecast for 10-year bond yield unchanged at 8.25% (currently 7.37%) despite sharp fall in yields as "inflation continues to trend above estimates (10% WPI, 15% CPI) and bond issues are still sizeable." Says, easing yields imply 3%-7% upside risk (assuming no loss/gain) to FY11 earnings forecasts for government banks with \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  (500112.BY), Bank of India (532149.BY) being most rate sensitive. Notes, easing yields may boost near-term (1QFY11) earnings for government banks. "However, the big positive would be if banks, particularly SBI, was to use these gains to raise their non-performing loan coverage." House maintains marketweight on Indian banking sector with \uc2 Axis Bank\uc2  (532215.BY), \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2  (500180.BY), \uc2 Bank of Baroda\uc2  (532134.BY) , Union Bank (532477.BY) and \uc2 Punjab National Bank\uc2  (532461.BY) as preferred plays. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0453 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs higher as intensifying European fiscal issues and tension in Korean Peninsula propel investors to move their assets to safe-haven bonds, says RuiXue Xu, fixed-income strategist at RBS Securities. Adds Japanese institutional investors still eager to buy bonds further, meaning JGBs should gain more. 10-year yield last at 1.220%, down 3bps; Xu says it may fall below 1.20% near term. June futures last at 140.51, up 0.26. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0452 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end lower, tracking sharp losses in regional markets amid mounting concerns on Europe's fiscal health. Benchmark PSEi down 2.8% to 3-month low of 3102.59 in heavy volume; all sub-indices registered declines, led by services and holding firms. "This is a knee-jerk reaction to what's happening abroad but it's quite possible we could have a rally after this," says April Tan, \uc2 CitisecOnline.com\uc2  research head; notes benchmark index down 6.8% since last week; resistance eyed at 3200, psychological support at 3000. Among losers, \uc2 Ayala Corp\uc2 . (AC.PH) off 1.5% at PHP320.00, SM Investments (SM.PH) off 4.5% at PHP372.50, PLDT (TEL.PH) off 3.9% at PHP2,360. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW still trading sharply higher at 1,264.1 vs 1,214.5 late yesterday in Seoul on heightening tension between two Korea's after reports North Korean government last week ordered its citizens, troops to be ready for combat. Traders note further upside capped around current levels for now following suspected USD selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , largely between KRW1,265-KRW1,272. "(I) think they (BOK) are continuously (selling the dollar) little by little...but I don't expect the dollar to pull-back much," from here given weak risk appetite, says local bank trader. Another trader tips pair in 1,260-1,272 range for rest of day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 1.4% at 1256.05 midday, extending losing streak to 8th consecutive day to reach lowest level since mid-February; worries over euro-zone debt further compounded by falls in regional markets amid Korean tensions. Dealers say KLCI's fall below 200-day moving average support at 1266 also point to bearish outlook for market in near-term; "A further retracement towards the immediate technical support level of 1250 appears imminent," RHB Research says; adds, investors likely to continue to shun stocks "to avoid catching a falling knife." Most blue chips down, with CIMB (1023.KU) down 2.5% at MYR6.65, Maybank (1155.KU) down 2.1% at MYR7.10, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2  (1961.KU) down 2.0% at MYR4.78; market debutant Sarawak Cable (5170.KU) +2.9% at 72 sen, well of earlier high of 77.5 sen as profit-taking sets in. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 2.4% at 19,193.70 midday, tracking U.S. stocks' fall, while geopolitical concerns also weigh on regional markets after Yonhap news agency reports North Korean leader Kim Jong-il ordered military to be ready for combat. Volume uninspiring at HK$33.32 billion. \uc2 Celestial Securities\uc2 ' Eugene Law says rotational selling prevails, with today's selling pressure especially heavy on drug stocks; Sinopharm (1099.HK) slumps 8.2% to HK$26.70, \uc2 Sino Biopharmaceutical\uc2  (1177.HK) drops 11.2% to HK$2.94. Law tips HSI "most likely" will breach 19,000 short term, with index not trading below 19,000 since July 2009. Foxconn (2038.HK) worst-performing blue chip, tumbles 9.9% to HK$5.30, likely in part weighed by Xinhua news agency report an employee died Tuesday after falling from building at Shenzhen plant. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0443 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end flat at 486.25, after moving between 479.61 to 489.59 in volatile trade; foreigners' then locals' bargain-hunting helped support after key index fell nearly 12% month-to-date. "Though many global markets fell as a result of the euro-zone's debt issue, buying appetite in the local market remains relatively strong today because many stocks have fallen significantly recently," says Hanoi-based trader. Volume moderate with 44 million shares, valued at VND1.36 billion, traded. Among actives, Vneco (VNE.VH) ends +4.5% at VND18,500, Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) ends down 0.5% at VND36,900, Itaco (ITA.VH) ends +3.3% at VND34,200. Traders tip buying sentiment to remain strong tomorrow with key index to test 490. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0443 GMT [Dow Jones] Current selloff in palladium, platinum driven by fears of repeat of September 2008 crash, when metals lost two-thirds of value, according to Tokyo-based trader. Palladium down $11, 2.2% at $433/oz, platinum down $21, 1.5%, at $1,503/oz, having dipped below $1,500/oz. "I feel it's a bit speculative at the moment. Corporates are holding a lot more cash now than after Lehman," reducing the risk of flight to cash which drove 2008 drops. Argues worst of last week's falls, which saw platinum lose 12%, drop to $1,452/oz while palladium lost 17%, touched $398/oz, aided by absence of many PGM traders who were at annual Platinum Week dinner in London. Sees gold possibly retesting $1,200/oz level briefly, but dollar strength should limit rises; gold at $1,192.52/oz, only riser among precious metals. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares not spared Asia-wide selloff. STI down 2.0% at 2670.56 midday, barely above year-to-date low of 2665 set in February. If 2665 fails to hold, next support at 2605 (minor low in October). Market breadth at 9 decliners for every gainer. "The downtrend is still intact because risk aversion factors are still in place," says Phillip Securities analyst Phua Ming-Weii; "Libor-OIS (overnight index swap) spread remains high and the uptrend in bonds is still intact. Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also continue to sell off. It is unlikely that equities rally with these (conditions) present." New listing Prudential (K6S.SG) fails to whet appetite, last at US$7.54 vs US$7.72 opening price, which tad higher than London's last closing price of GBP5.30, equivalent to US$7.65. Key decliners among blue chips include \uc2 Jardine Cycle & Carriage\uc2  (C07.SG), off 5.0% at S$25.64, NOL (N03.SG), off 4.2% at S$1.81, \uc2 Sembcorp Industries\uc2  (U96.SG), off 4.2% at S$3.87. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian government's new bond register is of little help in identifying who's buying country's debt given two thirds of buyers remain anonymous through custodians, says Sydney based interest rate strategist. "Basically it's kind of useless." Notes AOFM wasn't given powers to compel investors to identify themselves. "Basically, they took a survey and (a majority) of people declined to answer, so it doesn't really help." AOFM identifies by country 37.9% of bond holders in 3-months to March 31, leaving 62.1% unaccounted for. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese government bonds higher, with 10-year yield last down 3 bps at 1.220% and lead June futures up 0.30 at 140.55 due to lingering concerns over euro-zone fiscal issues. "Investors should buy more long-term bonds, which are still relatively cheap," says Kenro Kawano, strategist at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . 20-year JGB yield last down 2 bps at 1.995%, lowest point since December. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 05-25-10 0106ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100525e65p0003t\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (indonesia near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 21/08/2023\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl